var OVERLAY_PEOPLE_PROFILES=[{"id":"PER-james-hubbell","n":"James Hubbell","cat":"family","roles":["\"Little scamp\" reading Harpers magazine; letters too short","\"Master James\" — received good letter; wishes to see him","Advised not to hurry back — letter received","Anxious to hear from","Arrived — enlisted for 153rd NY"],"rel":"Son (youngest)","lc":46,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-09-23-001","LTR-1861-09-26-001","LTR-1861-10-17-001","LTR-1861-11-30-001","LTR-1861-12-08-002","LTR-1861-12-16-001","LTR-1861-12-22-001","LTR-1861-12-23-001","LTR-1861-12-29-001","LTR-1862-01-13-001","LTR-1862-01-17-001","LTR-1862-01-24-001","LTR-1862-02-07-001","LTR-1862-02-11-001","LTR-1862-02-12-001","LTR-1862-03-06-001","LTR-1862-03-21-001","LTR-1862-03-26-001","LTR-1862-04-01-001","LTR-1862-04-06-001","LTR-1862-04-21-001","LTR-1862-05-12-001","LTR-1862-05-27-001","LTR-1862-06-06-001","LTR-1862-06-30-001","LTR-1862-07-08-001","LTR-1862-07-28-001","LTR-1862-10-29-001","LTR-1863-02-13-001","LTR-1863-04-23-001","LTR-1863-07-06-001","LTR-1863-09-07-001","LTR-1863-09-15-001","LTR-1863-09-18-001","LTR-1864-05-23-001","LTR-1864-06-02-001","LTR-1864-07-29-001","LTR-1864-08-02-001","LTR-1864-08-08-001","LTR-1864-08-26-001","LTR-1864-09-21-001","LTR-1864-10-04-002","LTR-1864-10-22-001","LTR-1864-11-30-001","LTR-1864-12-27-001","LTR-1865-06-12-001"],"first":"1861-09-23","last":"1865-06-12","s":"James Hubbell, the youngest of the four brothers, followed an unusual path from schoolboy in Champlain to West Point cadet to combat soldier. Appointed to the Military Academy in 1862 with help from Mr. Wheeler and encouragement from Alexander — who saw him as \"one educated soldier in the family\" — James studied under Everest and accumulated demerits at the typical cadet rate, but by 1864 he had left West Point, enlisted as a lance corporal in the 153rd New York, and was drilling recruits at an $860 bounty. He fought through Sheridan's Shenandoah campaign, survived a flesh wound to the thigh at Cedar Creek in October 1864, and was hospitalized at Frederick and later Chestnut Hill in Philadelphia, where he celebrated Thanksgiving on turkey and ice cream while his wound healed."},{"id":"PER-frances-hubbell","n":"Frances ('Fannie') Hubbell","cat":"family","roles":["\"Ask Frances if she has forgotten me\"","\"Really gone\" (to Canada)","Alone at home — Mother visiting Charlotte","At home with Mother","At home — friends in Longeurit/Grand Ligne heard Henry dangerously wounded"],"rel":"Sister","lc":44,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-09-23-001","LTR-1861-10-19-001","LTR-1861-11-30-002","LTR-1861-12-08-001","LTR-1861-12-16-001","LTR-1861-12-20-001","LTR-1861-12-22-001","LTR-1861-12-29-001","LTR-1862-01-09-002","LTR-1862-01-13-001","LTR-1862-01-15-001","LTR-1862-01-17-001","LTR-1862-01-25-001","LTR-1862-02-05-001","LTR-1862-02-07-001","LTR-1862-02-12-001","LTR-1862-03-03-001","LTR-1862-05-01-001","LTR-1862-06-06-001","LTR-1862-06-22-001","LTR-1862-06-30-001","LTR-1862-07-08-001","LTR-1862-07-14-001","LTR-1862-07-26-001","LTR-1862-07-28-001","LTR-1862-09-09-001","LTR-1862-09-21-001","LTR-1862-10-06-001","LTR-1862-10-10-001","LTR-1862-10-15-001","LTR-1862-10-29-001","LTR-1862-11-19-001","LTR-1863-01-14-001","LTR-1863-04-23-001","LTR-1863-06-29-001","LTR-1864-07-29-001","LTR-1864-08-08-001","LTR-1864-08-22-001","LTR-1864-09-18-001","LTR-1864-09-21-003","LTR-1864-11-30-001","LTR-1864-12-27-001","LTR-1865-01-11-001","LTR-1865-06-12-001"],"first":"1861-09-23","last":"1865-06-12","s":"Frances \"Fannie\" Hubbell served as the family's anchor at home in Champlain, Clinton County, New York, managing the household correspondence that kept four brothers connected to their mother across hundreds of miles of war. She taught French in Montreal and Longueuil, Canada, and later became preceptress of the academy in Champlain, drawing affectionate complaints from her brothers when her letters grew scarce — Alexander once threatened to \"pull her ears\" over a \"little scribble on a little scrap of paper.\" Frances was the primary recipient of the brothers' most detailed letters, the one who relayed news between camps, and the sister who stood with Mother Frances through every agonizing gap in the mail, including the months-long search for Henry after Antietam."},{"id":"PER-henry-hubbell","n":"Henry Hubbell","cat":"family","roles":["\"Dear, dear, precious Henry\" — insupportable agony","\"Saw Henry grasp his leg, take a step or two, threw himself down\"","Anxious about him; wants address","Brother (overcoat reference)","Brother (quoted: \"However\")"],"rel":"Son","lc":36,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-09-23-001","LTR-1861-09-26-001","LTR-1861-09-27-002","LTR-1861-10-03-001","LTR-1861-12-08-001","LTR-1862-01-13-001","LTR-1862-02-05-003","LTR-1862-02-07-001","LTR-1862-02-11-002","LTR-1862-02-12-001","LTR-1862-02-12-002","LTR-1862-02-17-001","LTR-1862-02-23-001","LTR-1862-03-26-001","LTR-1862-06-30-001","LTR-1862-07-26-001","LTR-1862-07-27-001","LTR-1862-09-19-001","LTR-1862-09-21-001","LTR-1862-09-21-002","LTR-1862-09-27-001","LTR-1862-09-28-001","LTR-1862-09-30-001","LTR-1862-09-30-002","LTR-1862-10-01-001","LTR-1862-10-02-001","LTR-1862-10-06-001","LTR-1862-10-29-001","LTR-1862-11-19-001","LTR-1862-12-09-002","LTR-1862-12-21-001","LTR-1863-04-24-001","LTR-1863-04-30-001","LTR-1863-05-04-001","LTR-1863-07-08-001","LTR-1863-12-09-001"],"first":"1861-09-23","last":"1863-12-09","s":"Henry Hubbell, the eldest brother, enlisted in Company D of the 34th New York and produced some of the collection's most vivid writing — from his account of the regiment's passage through hostile Baltimore in July 1861 to his confession after Fair Oaks that he was \"almost tempted to desert sometimes\" from loneliness. He fought at Ball's Bluff, through the Peninsula Campaign, and into the Maryland Campaign, where he fell at Antietam on September 17, 1862, seen grasping his leg and throwing himself down with Confederate troops within two rods. The family spent months searching for him — Mother wrote colonels, Mr. Copeland read reports from the battlefield, and Alexander vowed to visit his grave near Antietam — but Henry was never conclusively found alive, and by April 1863 Charles wrote with finality: \"I think there is not the least doubt that Henry was killed.\""},{"id":"PER-luther","n":"Corporal Luther","cat":"military","roles":["Alexander's comrade — Mother sends regards","Coming home with Alexander — visiting \"our house\"","Companion — gets passes to go uptown together","Companion — out in city Christmas Eve, dinner Dec 26","Corporal; nearly drowned with Lt. Fitch; paid $5 for lost boat"],"rel":"","lc":35,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-12-08-001","LTR-1861-12-08-002","LTR-1861-12-11-001","LTR-1861-12-16-001","LTR-1861-12-22-001","LTR-1861-12-23-001","LTR-1861-12-29-001","LTR-1862-01-17-001","LTR-1862-01-24-001","LTR-1862-01-28-001","LTR-1862-02-23-001","LTR-1862-03-03-001","LTR-1862-03-06-001","LTR-1862-03-17-001","LTR-1862-04-11-001","LTR-1862-05-01-001","LTR-1862-05-31-001","LTR-1862-06-08-001","LTR-1862-07-26-001","LTR-1862-10-29-001","LTR-1862-11-28-001","LTR-1863-02-06-001","LTR-1863-02-21-001","LTR-1863-02-28-001","LTR-1863-03-12-001","LTR-1863-03-22-001","LTR-1863-04-23-001","LTR-1863-05-16-001","LTR-1864-02-18-001","LTR-1864-04-25-001","LTR-1864-05-08-001","LTR-1864-07-02-001","LTR-1864-07-08-001","LTR-1864-09-18-001","LTR-1865-07-27-001"],"first":"1861-12-08","last":"1865-07-27","s":"Corporal Amos C. Luther was Alexander Hubbell's closest companion throughout the war, serving beside him in Company H of the 60th New York from their first days in Baltimore through the final muster-out at Ogdensburgh in July 1865. The two men shared tents, pulled guard duty together, took a joint photograph, spent Christmas Eve 1861 in the city, and sent money home in the same packages — Alexander once noting that Luther was \"snoring away like a good fellow\" beside him. Luther was promoted to sergeant, nearly drowned with Lt. Fitch when their boat sank in the Chesapeake, fell out on the march to Winchester, was hospitalized with illness, and took a ball through his right side and arm in the trenches before Atlanta in July 1864, but he recovered and came home with Alexander to visit \"our house\" in Champlain."},{"id":"PER-charles-hubbell","n":"Charles Hubbell","cat":"family","roles":["153rd NY left for North Carolina; sent bounty money home","153rd in Maryland — hasn't written","Asked about military life","At winter quarters Newtown — don't worry","Brother"],"rel":"Son","lc":34,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-09-05-001","LTR-1861-09-23-001","LTR-1861-09-26-001","LTR-1861-09-27-001","LTR-1861-10-06-001","LTR-1861-11-15-001","LTR-1861-12-08-002","LTR-1861-12-16-001","LTR-1861-12-19-001","LTR-1861-12-22-001","LTR-1861-12-29-001","LTR-1862-01-13-001","LTR-1862-02-05-002","LTR-1862-02-07-001","LTR-1862-02-11-002","LTR-1862-02-23-001","LTR-1862-04-01-001","LTR-1862-04-21-002","LTR-1862-06-06-001","LTR-1862-07-08-001","LTR-1862-07-26-001","LTR-1862-09-21-001","LTR-1862-09-21-002","LTR-1862-09-28-001","LTR-1862-10-02-001","LTR-1862-10-29-001","LTR-1862-12-09-002","LTR-1863-01-14-001","LTR-1864-07-29-001","LTR-1864-08-02-001","LTR-1864-08-08-001","LTR-1864-08-15-001","LTR-1864-10-04-001","LTR-1864-12-09-001"],"first":"1861-09-05","last":"1864-12-09","s":"Charles Hubbell enlisted in the 153rd New York in the fall of 1862, training at Plattsburgh Barracks where he described the stone buildings, four-high bunks, and straw-tick beds in the collection's most detailed account of recruit life. He served in the defenses of Washington, visited Alexander at Fairfax Station and Aquia Creek, was posted to New Orleans where he carried mail up the Red River and documented the city's above-ground burials and mule-drawn streetcars, and fought through the Shenandoah Valley campaign under Sheridan. Charles was the brother who pushed back hardest on family myths — insisting Henry could not have avoided the fight at Antietam because \"the roll was called and every man was obliged to go\" — and he ended the war on occupation duty in Savannah, counting the days until his October 1865 discharge."},{"id":"PER-alexander-hubbell","n":"Alexander F. Hubbell","cat":"family","roles":["\"Never looked so finely and well\"","3rd sergeant; big fat face, hearty as a buck","60th Regt. on its way; Henry wants to see him","Brother","Brother — Henry wrote him but wrong address"],"rel":"Son","lc":28,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-09-11-001","LTR-1861-09-22-001","LTR-1861-09-27-001","LTR-1861-12-19-001","LTR-1861-12-20-002","LTR-1861-12-26-001","LTR-1862-02-11-001","LTR-1862-02-12-002","LTR-1862-02-17-001","LTR-1862-02-23-001","LTR-1862-04-21-002","LTR-1862-05-12-001","LTR-1862-07-12-001","LTR-1862-07-27-001","LTR-1862-09-21-001","LTR-1862-09-21-002","LTR-1862-09-28-001","LTR-1862-09-30-002","LTR-1862-10-02-001","LTR-1862-10-06-001","LTR-1862-10-12-001","LTR-1862-10-29-001","LTR-1862-12-26-001","LTR-1863-01-02-001","LTR-1863-01-14-001","LTR-1863-03-12-001","LTR-1863-05-31-001","LTR-1865-06-12-001"],"first":"1861-09-11","last":"1865-06-12","s":"Alexander F. Hubbell enlisted in Company H of the 60th New York Volunteers in September 1861 and served continuously for nearly four years, rising from private to sergeant and carrying the regimental colors at Chancellorsville. His letters — the largest single voice in the collection — trace the war from Camp Wheeler at Ogdensburgh through Baltimore, Winchester, Gettysburg, Lookout Mountain, and the Atlanta campaign, culminating in the eloquent valedictory of June 1865: \"after four years of unparalleled struggling we stand a free and united nation.\" A man of deep religious conviction who attended prayer meetings and abstained from whiskey rations, Alexander was also fiercely political, shifting from devotion to McClellan (\"my heart still yearns toward the man\") to pragmatic acceptance of Lincoln, while his racial views reflected the painful contradictions of a Union soldier who fought to end slavery but \"could never consent to see these black men mingled with our white.\""},{"id":"PER-ransom","n":"Captain Ransom","cat":"military","roles":["\"Very — very kind\"","\"Very, very kind\"","60th NY officer — took tea with Mother, health improving, returning to regiment","Accused of taking company ration money","Alexander sends regards — 'Give my regards to Capt. Ransom'"],"rel":"","lc":27,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-09-11-001","LTR-1861-09-23-001","LTR-1861-09-26-001","LTR-1861-10-03-002","LTR-1861-11-13-001","LTR-1861-12-08-001","LTR-1861-12-16-001","LTR-1861-12-26-001","LTR-1862-02-07-001","LTR-1862-03-17-001","LTR-1862-06-13-001","LTR-1862-06-22-002","LTR-1862-10-01-001","LTR-1862-10-02-001","LTR-1862-10-06-001","LTR-1862-10-12-001","LTR-1862-11-12-001","LTR-1862-11-19-001","LTR-1863-01-02-001","LTR-1863-02-06-001","LTR-1863-03-12-001","LTR-1863-06-03-001","LTR-1863-11-21-001","LTR-1863-12-09-001","LTR-1863-12-16-001","LTR-1864-07-02-001","LTR-1864-07-29-001"],"first":"1861-09-11","last":"1864-07-29","s":"Captain Ransom commanded Company H of the 60th New York and was Alexander Hubbell's most important officer throughout the war, elected unanimously at Camp Wheeler in September 1861 and described repeatedly as \"very, very kind.\" He cared for Alexander during illness, sent Alexander's pay home to Champlain, carried Mother Hubbell's letters back to the regiment, took tea with the family when on leave, and wrote to officials seeking Alexander's discharge when Mother applied through the lieutenant colonel. Ransom survived a boiler explosion, weathered accusations of taking company ration money, and served from the regiment's formation through at least mid-1864, appearing in 27 letters across nearly three years of the war."},{"id":"PER-mrs--hubbell","n":"Mother (Mrs. F. A. Hubbell)","cat":"civilian","roles":["Asked about $15 and miniature","Lonely in \"great castle of a house\" — no company","Mother","Mother — $20 directed to her","Mother — worrying about Henry"],"rel":"","lc":23,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-09-05-001","LTR-1861-09-20-001","LTR-1861-09-21-001","LTR-1861-09-23-001","LTR-1861-09-26-001","LTR-1861-09-27-002","LTR-1861-11-15-001","LTR-1861-11-30-003","LTR-1861-12-08-001","LTR-1861-12-20-002","LTR-1861-12-22-001","LTR-1861-12-23-001","LTR-1861-12-26-001","LTR-1861-12-29-001","LTR-1862-01-09-001","LTR-1862-01-13-001","LTR-1862-01-15-001","LTR-1862-01-24-001","LTR-1862-01-25-001","LTR-1862-02-12-002","LTR-1862-02-17-001","LTR-1862-02-23-001","LTR-1863-06-29-001"],"first":"1861-09-05","last":"1863-06-29","s":"Frances Augusta Hubbell, the brothers' mother, wrote from the family home in Champlain with a voice that swung between fierce maternal love and barely contained anguish — calling Henry \"dear, dear, precious Henry, my darling boy\" months after Antietam and crying out \"Where are you? Oh, where are you?\" to Alexander after Lookout Mountain. She endured the war largely alone in what she called her \"great castle of a house,\" occasionally visiting Charlotte or hosting Captain Ransom for tea, while managing the farm, forwarding letters between her sons' scattered regiments, and navigating the military bureaucracy to seek news of Henry and a discharge for Alexander. By January 1865, with Champlain under military occupation after the St. Albans Raid — 150 soldiers quartered in town, cavalry stables built, borders sealed against Canada — she feared James had fallen into guerrilla hands and poured her moral counsel into letters that read as a mother's attempt to hold her fractured family together by sheer force of will."},{"id":"PER-alex-douglass","n":"A. Douglas (Alek)","cat":"civilian","roles":["Captain wanted him instead — too healthy to spare","Commissary sergt. — $21/month","Fellow soldier — \"not sorry we came to the war\"","Gone to Fortress Monroe as guard with Lieut. Knapp","Headache for a day or two"],"rel":"","lc":17,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-09-30-001","LTR-1862-09-30-002","LTR-1862-10-10-001","LTR-1862-11-18-001","LTR-1862-12-09-001","LTR-1863-02-13-001","LTR-1863-02-22-001","LTR-1863-03-19-001","LTR-1863-07-20-001","LTR-1863-09-15-001","LTR-1863-09-25-001","LTR-1863-10-12-001","LTR-1864-03-06-001","LTR-1864-05-03-001","LTR-1864-05-23-001","LTR-1864-10-04-001","LTR-1864-10-22-001"],"first":"1862-09-30","last":"1864-10-22","s":"A. Douglas, known as \"Alek,\" served alongside Charles and James Hubbell in the 153rd New York, rising to commissary sergeant at $21 per month and becoming a trusted comrade who broke the news of Henry's wounding to Charles at 9 PM on September 30, 1862. He went on furlough when his father was severely hurt by falling timber, served as guard at Fortress Monroe, and declared himself \"not sorry we came to the war,\" but his story took a dark turn during Sheridan's Shenandoah campaign when he straggled on the march with another man and was captured by guerrillas in early October 1864. Douglas was still listed as missing at the Battle of Cedar Creek on October 19, and Charles noted his continued absence alongside the regiment's devastating casualty count of 23 killed and wounded from a company reduced to just 22 men."},{"id":"PER-scott","n":"Lieutenant Scott","cat":"military","roles":["\"Strong evidence\" — previous intelligence about Henry","Company officer, arrested on false charges","Expected on his way back","Fixed watch for free","Officer — bought rebel knife for $5"],"rel":"","lc":13,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-09-21-001","LTR-1861-09-27-001","LTR-1861-10-06-001","LTR-1861-11-30-001","LTR-1862-02-05-003","LTR-1862-06-13-001","LTR-1862-07-08-002","LTR-1862-07-12-001","LTR-1862-07-28-001","LTR-1862-09-28-001","LTR-1862-12-09-001","LTR-1863-02-28-001","LTR-1863-04-27-001"],"first":"1861-09-21","last":"1863-04-27","s":"Lieutenant Scott served in Company D of the 34th New York alongside Henry Hubbell, sharing picket duty on the Potomac in the fall of 1861 and fixing Henry's watch for free. He was arrested on false charges early in the war — his absence lamented by the company, which needed his discipline — and later redeemed himself through brave conduct at Fair Oaks in June 1862. After Henry went missing at Antietam, Scott became a key source of intelligence for the family: he returned to report that the missing \"were probably mortally wounded\" and that Henry had been seen on one knee, providing what Mother Hubbell called \"strong evidence\" in her desperate search for answers."},{"id":"PER-silas","n":"Silas","cat":"civilian","roles":["\"Fireside patriot\" — man at home finding fault","\"Has a good deal of business\"","Business prospects — Chicago emigration","Charles sent vote to him","Discharged — \"cabosibles\""],"rel":"","lc":13,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-11-15-001","LTR-1861-12-26-001","LTR-1862-05-09-002","LTR-1862-06-17-001","LTR-1862-07-26-001","LTR-1863-02-22-001","LTR-1863-03-02-001","LTR-1863-03-29-001","LTR-1863-07-06-001","LTR-1863-07-20-001","LTR-1863-10-21-001","LTR-1864-04-23-001","LTR-1864-11-04-001"],"first":"1861-11-15","last":"1864-11-04","s":"Silas was a Champlain neighbor and acquaintance whom the Hubbell brothers regarded with a mixture of familiarity and contempt — Henry doubted his farming would succeed, noting \"I fancy he does not work much,\" and both Henry and Alexander dismissed him as a \"fireside patriot\" who found fault with the war from the safety of home. He escaped the draft through luck, dabbled in land sales and business prospects including possible emigration to Chicago, and gave lectures in Champlain while the brothers fought. Charles sent his vote to Silas for the 1864 election, suggesting he retained some practical role in family affairs despite the soldiers' scorn for men who stayed behind."},{"id":"PER-rich","n":"Capt. Rich","cat":"military","roles":["Brought 1,100 conscripts from NY","Cashiered","Expected to be Major (then corrected — Adjutant to be Major)","Going to Champlain; asked about Charles","Got back from NY"],"rel":"","lc":13,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-12-20-001","LTR-1862-06-08-001","LTR-1862-07-12-001","LTR-1862-10-29-001","LTR-1862-12-04-001","LTR-1862-12-08-001","LTR-1863-02-22-001","LTR-1863-07-20-001","LTR-1863-09-07-001","LTR-1863-09-29-001","LTR-1863-10-31-001","LTR-1863-11-19-001","LTR-1864-12-09-001"],"first":"1861-12-20","last":"1864-12-09","s":"Captain Rich served as aide-de-camp to General Slough, military governor of Alexandria, and later became captain of Charles Hubbell’s company in the 153rd New York — a development Mother Hubbell greeted by biting her tongue. He traveled frequently between the army and Champlain on recruiting business, once bringing 1,100 conscripts from New York to the front, and his movements between home and camp made him a recurring figure in the family’s correspondence from late 1861 through 1864."},{"id":"PER-hoyle","n":"Mrs. Hoyle","cat":"civilian","roles":["Brought letter from home","Investment advice","Name on newspapers","Pass provider","Person who can provide a travel pass — likely railroad agent or local official"],"rel":"","lc":11,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-10-03-001","LTR-1861-10-03-002","LTR-1862-02-04-001","LTR-1862-12-21-001","LTR-1863-03-16-001","LTR-1863-04-16-001","LTR-1864-04-06-001","LTR-1864-05-20-001","LTR-1864-09-21-001","LTR-1865-01-11-001","LTR-1865-06-16-001"],"first":"1861-10-03","last":"1865-06-16","s":"Mrs. Hoyle was a Champlain-area figure who maintained a steady connection to the Hubbell soldiers throughout the war, regularly sending newspapers — sometimes inscribed with Latin and English patriotic sentences — that Alexander received with gratitude. She provided travel passes, likely through a connection to the railroad or local government, and her store was used as a guard house when military forces occupied the area. Mrs. Hoyle also offered investment advice to the family, and her name appears across letters from 1861 through the war's end in 1865, making her one of the most persistent civilian presences in the brothers' correspondence."},{"id":"PER-sid","n":"Sid (& wife)","cat":"civilian","roles":["\"Fireside patriot\" — man at home finding fault","\"Ugly about our cows\"","Also at West Point","At Mrs. R.M. Hubbell's when Charles visited","Farming — Henry doubts he'll succeed"],"rel":"","lc":11,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-11-15-001","LTR-1862-05-09-001","LTR-1862-05-09-002","LTR-1862-06-13-001","LTR-1862-06-17-001","LTR-1862-07-26-001","LTR-1862-07-28-001","LTR-1862-10-10-001","LTR-1863-07-20-001","LTR-1863-08-21-001","LTR-1864-04-23-001"],"first":"1861-11-15","last":"1864-04-23","s":"Sid was a Champlain-area relative or neighbor whose farming efforts Henry observed with skepticism from Virginia, writing that he doubted Sid would succeed and noting he was married and installed with his wife and family in a big house. Henry encountered Sid at West Point, Virginia — the York River encampment, not the academy — in May 1862, one of several Champlain-area men scattered across the Union army. Like Silas, Sid was tagged as a \"fireside patriot\" by the brothers and was described as \"ugly about our cows,\" suggesting friction over the family's livestock and property during the men's absence at war."},{"id":"PER-culver-bostwick","n":"Culver Bostwick","cat":"civilian","roles":["\"Bully soldier\" — messes with Alexander","\"Stands the marches like a little hero\"","10 letters home, no answers — discouraged","Arrived at regiment — drawn new gun","Coming to join — at Elmira"],"rel":"","lc":10,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-03-20-001","LTR-1864-04-07-001","LTR-1864-05-06-001","LTR-1864-05-08-001","LTR-1864-05-10-001","LTR-1864-05-20-001","LTR-1864-06-08-001","LTR-1864-07-02-001","LTR-1864-08-08-001","LTR-1865-06-27-001"],"first":"1864-03-20","last":"1865-06-27","s":"Culver Bostwick joined Alexander Hubbell's regiment as a recruit in 1863, arriving via Elmira with a new gun that he immediately set to cleaning, and quickly proved himself — Alexander called him a \"bully soldier\" who \"stands the marches like a little hero.\" He messed with Alexander through the grueling Atlanta campaign, where both men were \"worn down bully thin\" by the summer of 1864, and he tried sharpshooting at rebels from the trenches. Bostwick wrote ten letters home without receiving a single answer, growing discouraged by the silence, but he survived the war and was discharged alongside the 60th New York veterans at their final muster-out in June 1865."},{"id":"PER-mcclellan","n":"Gen. McClellan","cat":"military","roles":["\"In great favor\"","\"Splendid looking fellow\" — seen several times","5 votes on train","Commanding general; \"carrying a regular siege at Richmond\"","Head of army; Henry seen him twice — \"young looking man\""],"rel":"","lc":9,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-28-001","LTR-1862-03-03-001","LTR-1862-03-21-001","LTR-1862-04-06-002","LTR-1862-04-21-002","LTR-1862-06-22-001","LTR-1864-04-22-001","LTR-1864-07-00-001","LTR-1864-10-10-001"],"first":"1862-02-28","last":"1864-10-10","s":"General McClellan appeared in the Hubbell letters first as a distant but admired figure — Henry saw him at Harpers Ferry in February 1862 and called him a \"young looking man\" and \"splendid looking fellow\" — and later as a political lightning rod during the 1864 election. Alexander was McClellan's most devoted supporter in the family, writing in April 1864 that \"my heart still yearns toward the man\" and noting that McClellan's speech had \"the right kind of ring,\" while also observing that border-state soldiers and \"Hibernians\" would vote for him. By October 1864, however, a straw poll on Alexander's hospital train showed 37 votes for Lincoln against only 5 for McClellan, and Alexander acknowledged that Lincoln was \"the favored one\" — a quiet concession that mirrored the army's broader shift."},{"id":"PER-albert-cook","n":"Albert Cook","cat":"civilian","roles":["34th NY — returned home; Alexander scornful","At Annapolis — allegedly \"seen and talked with Henry,\" wound \"very slight & in the leg\"","At Georgetown parole camp; visited; trying for 20-day furlough","Letter sent to him; love to Albert","POW at Annapolis, \"expressed his views very plainly to the rebels\""],"rel":"","lc":8,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-07-14-001","LTR-1862-11-10-001","LTR-1862-12-09-002","LTR-1862-12-26-001","LTR-1863-03-16-001","LTR-1863-05-19-001","LTR-1863-07-30-001","LTR-1863-09-03-001"],"first":"1862-07-14","last":"1863-09-03","s":"Albert Cook served in the Signal Corps attached to Banks's division and crossed paths with Alexander Hubbell at a signal station in Virginia in July 1862, where the two Champlain men shared coffee and long conversation fifty rods apart from their respective units. Cook was captured and held as a prisoner of war at Annapolis, where he reportedly \"expressed his views very plainly to the rebels,\" and later claimed to have seen and spoken with Henry Hubbell, reporting his wound as \"very slight and in the leg\" — a report that gave the family hope but that Alexander received with skepticism. After his parole, Cook tried for a twenty-day furlough from the Georgetown parole camp and eventually returned home to Champlain, drawing Alexander's scorn for leaving the service."},{"id":"PER-reich","n":"Capt. Reich","cat":"military","roles":["\"Head man there yet\" — Sutler or company","Company commander — presented with $125 sword","Company commander, Co. D, 34th NY","Discharged","Dishonorable discharge — refused order, put in irons"],"rel":"","lc":7,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-06-06-001","LTR-1861-09-11-001","LTR-1861-09-22-001","LTR-1862-01-09-001","LTR-1862-06-13-001","LTR-1862-06-22-002","LTR-1862-07-28-001"],"first":"1861-07-06","last":"1862-07-28","s":"Captain Reich commanded Company D of the 34th New York, Henry Hubbell's unit, from the regiment's formation in the summer of 1861 and was presented with a $125 sword by his men. He served as a courier carrying letters and money between the regiment and Champlain, but his military career ended in disgrace at Fair Oaks in June 1862 when he refused the colonel's order to move the company, had his sword and belt taken, was put in irons, and received a dishonorable discharge read before the entire regiment on dress parade. Henry believed Reich simply wanted out of the army, and the company missed him despite the circumstances — his departure left a gap in leadership that the men felt keenly."},{"id":"PER-copeland","n":"Mr. Copeland","cat":"civilian","roles":["Champlain acquaintance — asked to write","Family advisor/minister at home","James to give regards","Owed money?","Read a letter saying field searched, Henry not among dead; thinks prisoner"],"rel":"","lc":7,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-09-27-002","LTR-1861-12-23-001","LTR-1862-01-01-001","LTR-1862-03-06-001","LTR-1862-05-10-001","LTR-1862-10-06-001","LTR-1863-01-02-001"],"first":"1861-09-27","last":"1863-01-02","s":"Mr. Copeland was a family advisor and likely minister in Champlain who served as a moral anchor for the Hubbell family during the war, receiving regards from the brothers and writing letters to Alexander in camp. His most significant role came during the agonizing search for Henry after Antietam: Copeland read a letter reporting that the battlefield had been searched, bodies identified, and Henry was not among the dead — evidence that supported the family's hope that Henry had been taken prisoner rather than killed. He appeared in letters from September 1861 through early 1863, the period when the family's uncertainty about Henry's fate was at its most acute."},{"id":"PER-aunt-laura","n":"Aunt Laura","cat":"civilian","roles":["Aunt, correspondent","Finally wrote (long expected letter)","Hinted Uncle Henry might give acres","Letter received","Sent likeness with letter (with Nellie)"],"rel":"","lc":7,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-01-09-002","LTR-1862-02-12-001","LTR-1862-03-11-001","LTR-1862-04-02-001","LTR-1862-07-08-002","LTR-1863-03-12-001","LTR-1864-04-22-001"],"first":"1862-01-09","last":"1864-04-22","s":"Aunt Laura was a family member whose infrequent letters drew repeated complaints from Alexander and Henry — her correspondence was \"long expected\" when it finally arrived. She sent her likeness along with Nellie's and maintained enough connection to the family's affairs to hint that Uncle Henry might give the boys some acres, but her most consequential action was purchasing Henry Hubbell's farm, a transaction that Charles reported in March 1863 alongside worries that a man named Cleveland might claim the homestead. Aunt Laura's letters, spanning from early 1862 to April 1864, touched the family's anxieties about land and property — concerns that ran parallel to the war itself for a farming family whose men were all away at the front."},{"id":"PER-averill","n":"Miss Averill","cat":"civilian","roles":["Advocate — got Charles past eye exam; \"too honest\"","Bounty charges \"more moderate than supposed\"","Business agent — handling county bounty","Frances's friend — sled rides","Frances's friend — sliding in ravine"],"rel":"","lc":7,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-09-09-001","LTR-1864-02-18-001","LTR-1864-03-07-001","LTR-1864-03-07-002","LTR-1864-04-18-001","LTR-1864-06-08-001","LTR-1864-12-09-001"],"first":"1862-09-09","last":"1864-12-09","s":"The Averill family of Champlain figured in the Hubbell correspondence as both practical allies and social companions. Averill served as a business agent handling county bounty paperwork for Alexander in 1864, and was the advocate who talked the examining physician into passing Charles despite his near-sightedness at the Plattsburgh barracks in September 1862, telling Charles he was \"too honest\" for admitting the deficiency. Miss Averill was Frances Hubbell’s close friend, mentioned in connection with sled rides and sliding in the ravine near Champlain during the winters of 1863–64."},{"id":"PER-burdick","n":"Lieut. Burdick","cat":"military","roles":["Came with Ransom; pleasant man; Charles sees often","Champlain resident — Charles asks if Mother has called on her","Leaving for home in 3-4 days; invited soldiers to visit","May resign","Promoted to Captain"],"rel":"","lc":7,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-26-001","LTR-1863-02-13-001","LTR-1863-03-02-001","LTR-1863-03-12-001","LTR-1863-05-19-001","LTR-1863-08-16-001","LTR-1863-09-07-001"],"first":"1862-12-26","last":"1863-09-07","s":"Lieutenant Burdick of the 118th NY served alongside Charles Hubbell from late 1862 through mid-1863, rising from lieutenant to captain during the post-Chancellorsville officer reorganization in May 1863. Charles described him as a pleasant man after meeting him with Captain Ransom at Christmas 1862 near Alexandria, and Mrs. Burdick hosted visiting soldiers at her Alexandria lodgings in early 1863. Burdick resigned his commission in August 1863, departing Capitol Hill Barracks in \"very high spirits,\" one of several officers who chose to leave the 118th NY during its garrison duty in Washington."},{"id":"PER-hooker","n":"Gen. Hooker","cat":"military","roles":["\"In high favor with all the troops\" — giving 2-year men parting salute","\"Most feared by rebels\" — rumored killed","Commands new 20th Army Corps","Corps commander — 20th Army Corps","Leaving 20th Corps — farewell ride"],"rel":"","lc":7,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-04-16-001","LTR-1863-12-13-001","LTR-1864-04-06-001","LTR-1864-04-18-001","LTR-1864-04-22-001","LTR-1864-07-00-001","LTR-1864-08-08-001"],"first":"1863-04-16","last":"1864-08-08","s":"Major General Joseph Hooker commanded the 20th Army Corps through the critical campaigns of 1863-64, consolidating the 11th and 12th Corps under his leadership in April 1864 at Stevenson, Alabama. Alexander described him as \"fine looking\" when Hooker visited the troops before the Atlanta Campaign and reported that he was the general \"most feared by rebels\" during the fighting near Marietta in July 1864. Hooker's departure from the 20th Corps in August 1864 produced the collection's most cinematic scene: a legless Pennsylvania soldier calling out \"Good bye, General Hooker!\" from a stretcher, prompting Hooker to dismount, remove his hat, and shake the man's hand -- an image Alexander preserved with the tribute, \"The 20th Corps will never forget old 'Fighting Joe.'\""},{"id":"PER-brink","n":"Mrs. Brink","cat":"civilian","roles":["Family friend/neighbor","Fellow soldier / acquaintance from home","Fellow soldier, received Frances's letter","Passing summer","Soldier — Henry will pass him a paper"],"rel":"","lc":6,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-06-06-001","LTR-1861-09-23-001","LTR-1861-10-19-001","LTR-1861-11-30-002","LTR-1862-01-09-001","LTR-1863-07-06-001"],"first":"1861-07-06","last":"1863-07-06","s":"The Brink family maintained connections to the Hubbells both at home in Champlain and in the army, appearing in six letters between July 1861 and July 1863. A soldier named Brink served with Henry in the 34th NY from the regiment's arrival in Washington, receiving Frances's letters and papers passed along by Henry. Mrs. Brink was a family friend whom Alexander sent regards to from Camp Wheeler in September 1861, and Charles inquired about her in July 1863 from Alexandria, noting she was \"passing summer\" in Champlain."},{"id":"PER-nellie","n":"Nellie","cat":"civilian","roles":["Correspondent — sending picture","Female correspondent (possibly cousin or family friend)","Henry wrote about battle in her letter (not in collection)","Likeness received","Relative or friend (Henry wrote to her)"],"rel":"","lc":6,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-07-21-003","LTR-1861-10-19-001","LTR-1862-01-09-002","LTR-1862-02-12-001","LTR-1862-04-02-001","LTR-1862-06-16-001"],"first":"1861-07-21","last":"1862-06-16","s":"Nellie was a female correspondent -- likely a cousin or romantic interest -- who exchanged letters and photographs with Henry Hubbell throughout his service in the 34th NY from July 1861 to June 1862. Henry mentioned writing to her from Washington after Bull Run, promised her picture in October 1861, and received her likeness along with Aunt Laura's at Hampton, Virginia, in April 1862. She remained part of Henry's regular correspondence circle alongside Aunt Laura, and Henry wrote her a separate account of the Battle of Fair Oaks in June 1862 that does not survive in the collection."},{"id":"PER-bass","n":"Mrs. Bass","cat":"civilian","roles":["Convalescent — \"flirting dangerous business\"","Said \"he learned to do\" after 6 years teaching — \"favor yourself\"","Says 3 Northerners can't beat 1 Southerner","Schoolteacher — shot, probably fatally","Shot — mortal wound; made will overnight"],"rel":"","lc":6,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-03-03-001","LTR-1863-05-04-001","LTR-1864-03-20-001","LTR-1864-05-20-001","LTR-1864-06-08-001","LTR-1865-01-11-001"],"first":"1862-03-03","last":"1865-01-11","s":"Mr. M.M. Bass was a schoolteacher and Southern sympathizer in Champlain whose opinions infuriated the Hubbell brothers throughout the war. Alexander scorned him as a \"Yankee dough face\" from Stevenson, Alabama, in March 1864, while Mrs. Bass declared that three Northerners could not beat one Southerner. In May 1863, Isaac Dedds shot Bass in a jealousy dispute over the widow Mrs. Allison -- Bass made his will overnight but ultimately survived, and Alexander teased Mother's dramatic account of the shooting from Georgia, noting he had \"seen thousands of men wounded but hardly ever say anything come up to the pail of blood.\" By January 1865, Bass was trying to get passes for his scholars in a Champlain now under military rule."},{"id":"PER-mcdonald","n":"McDonald (R.W.)","cat":"civilian","roles":["Claimed to see Henry \"limping to the rear leaning upon his gun\"","His report about Henry being alive is now \"false rumor\"","Says Henry is missing (= R.W. McDonald of LTR-1862-10-01-001)","Source of 'good news' about Henry that Charles doubts — 'afraid it is too good news to be true'","Urged Henry to go to hospital twice"],"rel":"","lc":6,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-07-12-001","LTR-1862-10-02-001","LTR-1862-11-28-001","LTR-1862-12-04-001","LTR-1862-12-09-001","LTR-1863-05-04-001"],"first":"1862-07-12","last":"1863-05-04","s":"R.W. McDonald was Henry Hubbell's tentmate in the 34th NY who became a central figure in the family's agonizing search for information after Antietam. He urged Henry to go to the hospital twice before the battle in July 1862, and afterward reported that Henry was missing. McDonald's claim to have seen Henry \"limping to the rear leaning upon his gun\" gave the family hope through the autumn of 1862, but by December, Mother declared his report a \"false rumor,\" confirmed when McKee -- who was closer to the action -- wrote that he saw nothing of Henry after the wounding. McDonald's contradictory testimony haunted the family's investigation across six letters spanning July 1862 to May 1863."},{"id":"PER-slocum","n":"Gen. Slocum","cat":"military","roles":["Assigned to command of Vicksburg","Commands 12th Army Corps","Commands Corps (stated \"6th\" — likely means 20th)","Returns to command 20th Corps","Said 1st Div. should go to front, not 2nd"],"rel":"","lc":6,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-01-02-001","LTR-1864-02-21-001","LTR-1864-03-07-001","LTR-1864-04-06-001","LTR-1864-08-08-001","LTR-1864-08-30-001"],"first":"1863-01-02","last":"1864-08-30","s":"Major General Henry Slocum commanded the 12th Army Corps under which Alexander's 60th NY served, and later returned to lead the reconstituted 20th Corps during the final stages of the Atlanta Campaign in August 1864. Alexander identified his regiment as part of Slocum's corps in January 1863 at Fairfax Station, and reported Slocum's speech prophesying a speedy end to the war at Stephenson, Alabama, in February 1864. When the 11th and 12th Corps merged into the 20th under Hooker in April 1864, Slocum was sent to Vicksburg, but he returned to replace Hooker after the latter's resignation near Atlanta, commanding the corps through the March to the Sea and the war's end."},{"id":"PER-delaney","n":"Capt. Delaney","cat":"military","roles":["At home — recovering from wound","Boots to be addressed in his care","Presented sword by Co. H","Talked to surgeon and colonel — discharge impossible","Wounded fight of July 20"],"rel":"","lc":6,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-09-18-001","LTR-1863-11-21-001","LTR-1864-04-01-001","LTR-1864-07-24-001","LTR-1864-08-08-002","LTR-1864-08-30-001"],"first":"1863-09-18","last":"1864-08-30","s":"Captain Delaney of Company H, 60th NY Veterans, received a presentation sword from his men in September 1863 at Raccoon Ford -- a ritual of unit loyalty that Alexander witnessed. He interceded on Alexander's behalf in the futile discharge effort of November 1863, consulting both the surgeon and the colonel only to report that the power lay with Secretary Stanton or the President. Delaney was wounded in the arm at the Battle of Peachtree Creek on July 20, 1864, and sent home to recover, where Alexander directed Mother to address his boots in Delaney's care at Champlain."},{"id":"PER-albert","n":"Albert","cat":"civilian","roles":["Fellow soldier or acquaintance","Home on leave — sleigh riding","Mr. Cook's son/relative — feared shot at Shepherdstown; not heard from 1+ month","Wants to know which young lady cried when he left","With Misner — also not incarcerated"],"rel":"","lc":5,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-07-21-003","LTR-1861-12-20-001","LTR-1862-02-05-001","LTR-1862-10-06-001","LTR-1862-10-29-001"],"first":"1861-07-21","last":"1862-10-29","s":"Albert, likely Albert Cook's relative or a soldier from the Champlain community, served in Henry's regiment and appeared in five letters between July 1861 and October 1862. Henry mentioned him from Washington on the day of Bull Run, and he was home on leave for sleigh riding in December 1861. By October 1862, Mr. Cook was anxious about Albert, who had not been heard from in over a month and was feared shot at Shepherdstown. B. Misner, returned from Confederate captivity, confirmed Albert had been with him and was not among those incarcerated in Richmond."},{"id":"PER-colonel","n":"The Colonel (unnamed)","cat":"military","roles":["60th NY commander — \"savage looking,\" wolfish gray beard, threatens to resign","Believed Henry slightly wounded, taken prisoner","Gave Alexander tacit permission to visit Henry: \"If you are willing to run the risk, you can go\"","Ordered company movement; Reich refused","Regimental commander, 34th NY"],"rel":"","lc":5,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-07-21-003","LTR-1862-03-26-001","LTR-1862-06-22-002","LTR-1862-11-19-001","LTR-1863-04-30-001"],"first":"1861-07-21","last":"1863-04-30","s":"The unnamed colonel of the 60th NY appeared in Alexander's letters as a \"savage looking old fellow with a moustache and beard of a wolfish gray\" who threatened to resign if the regiment was kept on road duty near Baltimore in March 1862. Despite his gruff demeanor, Alexander reported him liked by all. He gave Alexander tacit permission to find Henry before Antietam -- \"If you are willing to run the risk, you can go\" -- enabling the brothers' last meeting. The colonel of the 34th NY, referenced separately in Henry's letters, ordered the company movement that Captain Reich refused, leading to Reich's dishonorable discharge on the Peninsula in June 1862."},{"id":"PER-b","n":"Mrs. B.","cat":"civilian","roles":["Acquaintance; \"Remember me to Mrs. B.\"","Family friend (likely Mrs. Brink)","Going to live with Mother in Stone Castle","Lost use of tongue (illness?) — \"exception to the sex\"","Regards"],"rel":"","lc":5,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-09-26-001","LTR-1862-03-03-001","LTR-1862-07-08-001","LTR-1864-04-18-001","LTR-1864-09-18-001"],"first":"1861-09-26","last":"1864-09-18","s":"Mrs. B., likely Mrs. Brink or Bostwick, was a recurring Champlain family friend to whom Alexander sent regards across multiple letters from September 1861 through September 1864. Alexander asked to be remembered to her from Camp Wheeler, Front Royal, and the Chattanooga hospital, and reported in April 1864 that she was going to live with Mother in the \"Stone Castle\" at Champlain. Her consistent presence as a recipient of regards across nearly three years of correspondence marks her as one of Mother's closest companions during the war."},{"id":"PER-gorman","n":"Gen. Gorman","cat":"military","roles":["\"Great praise\" for regiment","Brigade General (Willis A. Gorman)","Brigade commander — battle advice: keep cool, go ahead if family falls","Brigade commander — complimented 34th NY","Brigade commander — wheat joke"],"rel":"","lc":5,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-11-30-002","LTR-1861-12-20-002","LTR-1862-02-05-002","LTR-1862-04-06-002","LTR-1862-06-16-001"],"first":"1861-11-30","last":"1862-06-16","s":"Brigadier General Willis A. Gorman commanded the brigade containing Henry's 34th NY, alongside the 2nd NY and 1st Minnesota, from late 1861 through the Peninsula Campaign of 1862. Henry reported Gorman's compliment that the 34th NY \"did the best of them all\" during brigade drill at Camp McClellan in December 1861, and Gorman gave the chilling pre-battle advice at Yorktown in April 1862: \"keep cool and not to mind if brother or cousin fell, but to go ahead.\" By June 1862, five miles from Richmond, Gorman was giving the regiment \"great praise\" -- but the unit had been reduced from roughly 1,000 men to 500 in a single year of swamps, disease, and the Battle of Fair Oaks."},{"id":"PER-ingram","n":"Sergeant Ingram","cat":"military","roles":["\"Man of honor\" — handling money transfer; apple tree/Crimean War stories","Alexander visited his wife during furlough; Ingram will visit Mother","Called on Mother — Alexander glad","Going home on furlough; will visit Mother","Letter sent — no response"],"rel":"","lc":5,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-03-30-001","LTR-1863-03-16-001","LTR-1863-03-22-001","LTR-1863-04-23-001","LTR-1863-09-03-001"],"first":"1862-03-30","last":"1863-09-03","s":"Sergeant (later Lieutenant) Ingram of the 60th NY was one of Alexander's most trusted comrades, described as \"a man of honor\" who handled money transfers home to Mother in Champlain. He first appeared going home on furlough from Relay House in March 1862 with instructions to visit the Hubbell family, and Alexander later visited Ingram's wife during his own furlough. Ingram carried $47.90 of Alexander's money home in April 1863 -- just a week before Chancellorsville -- and called on Mother in Champlain, cementing the personal bond between the families that the war had created."},{"id":"PER-grandmother","n":"Frances M. Hubbell (Mother)","cat":"family","roles":["Died","Expected to host Thanksgiving dinner","Family — Charles hopes Frances and Mother went to Grandmother's for Thanksgiving","Involved in land sale that \"cheated us out of our rights\"","Regards sent"],"rel":"Mother","lc":5,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-04-01-001","LTR-1862-11-25-001","LTR-1862-11-28-001","LTR-1864-05-10-001","LTR-1864-11-04-001"],"first":"1862-04-01","last":"1864-11-04","s":"The Hubbell brothers' grandmother hosted Thanksgiving dinners and served as a family anchor in the extended kin network around Clinton County. Alexander blamed her involvement in the sale of 200 acres to Clark for $800 in April 1862, writing furiously from Relay House that the family had been \"cheated out of our rights.\" She was the destination of holiday visits -- Alexander imagined her Thanksgiving table from Harpers Ferry in 1862, and Charles hoped Mother and Frances had gone to Grandmother's for the holiday. Her death, reported in Charles's letter of November 4, 1864, from Cedar Creek, coincided with one of the family's worst periods: the company reduced to 22 men, James wounded, and Charles one of only two Lincoln voters in his unit."},{"id":"PER-brinkerhoff","n":"Mrs. Brinkerhoff","cat":"civilian","roles":["Champlain neighbor","Remembered","Remembered to — family friend/neighbor","Whispered in church — \"Have you heard from Alexander?\""],"rel":"","lc":5,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-05-31-001","LTR-1862-06-30-001","LTR-1863-03-12-001","LTR-1863-09-25-001","LTR-1863-11-15-001"],"first":"1862-05-31","last":"1863-11-15","s":"Mrs. Brinkerhoff was a Champlain neighbor who served as a companion to Mother Frances Hubbell during the war years, appearing in five letters from May 1862 through November 1863. Alexander sent regards to her from Harpers Ferry in his first combat letter, and Charles reported from Washington in September 1863 that Mrs. Brinkerhoff would live with Mother that winter to ease her solitude. In Mother's November 1863 letter, Mrs. Brinkerhoff whispered in church, \"Have you heard from Alexander?\" -- capturing the community's shared anxiety over the Hubbell brothers and the way war news traveled through Champlain's pews."},{"id":"PER-uncle-henry","n":"Uncle Henry","cat":"civilian","roles":["Family uncle — requested Mother visit Charlotte","Farm/land","Has a new baby boy","Place to be purchased","Visited after Thanksgiving"],"rel":"","lc":5,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-06-16-001","LTR-1862-07-08-002","LTR-1862-07-14-001","LTR-1865-07-13-001","LTR-1870-11-28-001"],"first":"1862-06-16","last":"1870-11-28","s":"Uncle Henry was a family patriarch whose property near Charlotte figured in the brothers' post-war plans. Henry Hubbell joked about the uncle's new baby boy from five miles outside Richmond in June 1862, and Alexander dreamed of buying Uncle Henry's place and going to college from a mountaintop near Warrenton, Virginia, in July 1862. After the war, Uncle Henry invited Mother to visit Charlotte, and he appeared in the collection's final letter -- Thanksgiving 1870 -- when Mother, Aunt Louise, and the family gathered at his home, five years after Appomattox."},{"id":"PER-sherman","n":"Gen. Sherman","cat":"military","roles":["Dispatch — \"let us do likewise\"","Former head doctor, now in Congress","Near coast — Savannah","On the move — Alexander trying to reach him","Ordered batteries silent during day"],"rel":"","lc":5,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-07-12-001","LTR-1864-02-24-001","LTR-1864-05-10-001","LTR-1864-07-29-001","LTR-1864-12-12-002"],"first":"1862-07-12","last":"1864-12-12","s":"General William Tecumseh Sherman commanded the Western Theater campaigns that shaped Alexander's final year of service, though the first \"Sherman\" in the collection was actually the 34th NY's regimental doctor who left for Congress in 1862. Alexander reported Sherman's dispatch to the troops at Buzzard's Roost in May 1864 -- the Army of the Potomac had \"thrashed the rebs\" and Sherman added \"let us do likewise\" -- connecting the two great simultaneous campaigns of 1864. Sherman ordered the batteries to fire into Atlanta every five minutes through the night in late July, and by December 1864 was marching toward Savannah while Alexander watched the Battle of Nashville unfold three days before the fighting began."},{"id":"PER-james-lucas","n":"James Lucas","cat":"civilian","roles":["Corporal — arrested for ration theft","Reduced to ranks — ration theft","Shot head and arm — Chaplain says probably won't recover","Shot through head and arm — reported killed, survived","Tentmate"],"rel":"","lc":5,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-11-18-001","LTR-1863-07-25-001","LTR-1863-08-16-001","LTR-1864-10-22-001","LTR-1864-11-04-001"],"first":"1862-11-18","last":"1864-11-04","s":"James Lucas (also Jim Lucas) served as Charles Hubbell's tentmate in the 153rd NY from November 1862, one of only three literate men among twelve in their tent. He was arrested and reduced to the ranks for his part in a ration theft scandal at Capitol Hill Barracks in the summer of 1863, losing a month's pay. Lucas survived the war's harshest test at the Battle of Cedar Creek on October 19, 1864, when he was shot through both the head and arm -- the Chaplain reported he would probably not recover, but he was later noted as having survived. His arc from tentmate to petty thief to grievously wounded soldier encapsulates the compressed extremes of enlisted life in the 153rd NY."},{"id":"PER-geary","n":"Lieut. Geary","cat":"military","roles":["Division commander — may push them to front","Division commander — slightly wounded (toe)","Issued farewell order — gone","Midnight march drill in rain","On skirmish line with Hooker"],"rel":"","lc":5,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-11-01-001","LTR-1863-11-21-001","LTR-1864-03-07-001","LTR-1864-07-00-001","LTR-1865-06-16-001"],"first":"1863-11-01","last":"1865-06-16","s":"Major General John White Geary commanded the 2nd Division of the 12th (later 20th) Corps throughout Alexander's service in the Western Theater, from Wauhatchie through the Atlanta Campaign. His son, Lieutenant Geary, was killed at the Battle of Wauhatchie on October 28-29, 1863, while the general himself was slightly wounded in the toe -- a personal loss that Alexander recorded alongside the serious wounding of brigade commander General Greene. Geary ordered a midnight march in the rain as a drill exercise at Wauhatchie in November 1863, and Alexander reported him on the skirmish line with Hooker near Marietta during the Atlanta Campaign. He issued a farewell order and departed after the war's end, as the 20th Corps was broken up near Washington in June 1865."},{"id":"PER-chaplain","n":"Chaplain (unnamed)","cat":"civilian","roles":["Regimental chaplain, 34th NY","Regimental chaplain, sick","Same as at beginning of service; Sunday prayer meetings","Spoke on Duty of Patriotism; predicted movement"],"rel":"","lc":4,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-07-21-001","LTR-1861-10-06-001","LTR-1861-12-26-001","LTR-1862-02-24-001"],"first":"1861-07-21","last":"1862-02-24","s":"The unnamed chaplain of the 34th New York served as a spiritual anchor for Henry's regiment from Bull Run through early 1862, leading Sunday prayer meetings at Camp McClellan and preaching on the \"Duty of Patriotism\" as marching orders arrived. His illness in October 1861 and his prediction that the regiment would not spend another Sabbath at camp marked the rhythms of garrison life and impending movement."},{"id":"PER-plummer","n":"Plummer","cat":"civilian","roles":["\"Great scamp\" — context unclear, someone from home","Farm contractor (unclear role; likely hired hand)","Has a contract (farm business) — James warned to watch him","Owes three loads of wood — \"a cheat\""],"rel":"","lc":4,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-01-17-001","LTR-1862-02-17-001","LTR-1862-03-17-001","LTR-1862-11-18-001"],"first":"1862-01-17","last":"1862-11-18","s":"Plummer was a Champlain contractor and hired hand who managed work on the Hubbell family property, though the brothers regarded him with deep suspicion. Alexander warned James to watch him, called him a \"great scamp,\" and Charles declared him \"a cheat\" who owed three loads of wood — a recurring example of the home-front business disputes the brothers managed from hundreds of miles away."},{"id":"PER-cousin-nellie","n":"Cousin Nellie","cat":"civilian","roles":["Correspondent (lazy with mail)","Cousin, negligent correspondent (same as Aunt Laurie)","Letter with Aunt Laura","Sent her likeness to Alexander"],"rel":"","lc":4,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-17-001","LTR-1862-02-23-001","LTR-1862-03-11-001","LTR-1862-04-01-001"],"first":"1862-02-17","last":"1862-04-01","s":"Cousin Nellie was a family correspondent whom Alexander teased for her slow letter-writing, grouping her with Aunt Laurie as negligent correspondents who waited four to six weeks to reply. She sent her likeness to Alexander in early 1862, a gesture he acknowledged warmly even as the family's correspondence network strained under wartime postal delays."},{"id":"PER-banks","n":"Mrs. Banks","cat":"military","roles":["Commanding general; army provisioned by wagon","Commands division Henry is in; false rumor of being \"cut to pieces\"","Wife of Gen. Banks — Sanitary Commission president","`stated`"],"rel":"","lc":4,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-03-03-001","LTR-1862-06-17-001","LTR-1864-04-15-001","LTR-1864-04-25-001"],"first":"1862-03-03","last":"1864-04-25","s":"Gen. Nathaniel Banks commanded the division in which Henry served in the Shenandoah Valley, and a false rumor that Banks's force had been \"cut to pieces\" alarmed Alexander in Baltimore in March 1862. Banks reappeared later in the war as commander of the Red River Campaign, where his wife served as president of the Sanitary Commission fundraiser that Charles observed in New Orleans in 1864."},{"id":"PER-uncle-john","n":"Uncle John","cat":"civilian","roles":["Family member — Charles says he need not write often","In Genesee Co., Michigan — not well","Visiting — estate share?","Wrote letter — \"strong democrat and a little sesesh\""],"rel":"","lc":4,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-03-16-001","LTR-1862-12-08-001","LTR-1863-03-02-001","LTR-1863-11-19-001"],"first":"1862-03-16","last":"1863-11-19","s":"Uncle John was a family member living in Genesee County, Michigan, whose poor health concerned the family in 1862 and whose political sympathies troubled them further — Charles identified him as \"a strong democrat and a little sesesh.\" He visited Champlain in late 1863, possibly in connection with estate business, and Charles told Mother he need not write often."},{"id":"PER-cyrus-kellogg","n":"Cyrus Kellogg","cat":"civilian","roles":["In squad to Aquia Creek — Hooker will fight two more years","In straggler squad — time out June 10; Hooker promises \"hard fighting\"","Out of hospital, at convalescent camp; visits uncle Norman","POW at Annapolis with Albert Cook"],"rel":"","lc":4,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-11-10-001","LTR-1862-12-26-001","LTR-1863-03-19-001","LTR-1863-03-29-002"],"first":"1862-11-10","last":"1863-03-29","s":"Cyrus Kellogg of the Champlain area was captured and held as a prisoner of war at Annapolis alongside Albert Cook in late 1862, then cycled through the convalescent camp system and a straggler squad before being sent back toward the front at Aquia Creek. He reported to Charles that Hooker promised \"hard fighting\" before June 1863, and his wartime trajectory illustrated the revolving door between hospital, camp, and combat."},{"id":"PER-simons","n":"Mr. Simons","cat":"civilian","roles":["Academy connection — \"brains in the right place\"","Academy — opinions about Potomac army","Frances's preferred colleague/teacher","Teacher — Charles asks Frances how his school is doing"],"rel":"","lc":4,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-11-18-001","LTR-1862-11-28-001","LTR-1863-03-16-001","LTR-1863-03-22-001"],"first":"1862-11-18","last":"1863-03-22","s":"Mr. Simons was a teacher at the Champlain Academy whom Frances Hubbell preferred over his colleague Mr. Karns, and whom Alexander credited with having his \"brains in the right place.\" Charles asked Frances how his school was doing, and Alexander noted his opinions on the Army of the Potomac — a civilian educator whose views carried weight with the Hubbell brothers."},{"id":"PER-slough","n":"Gen. Slough","cat":"military","roles":["\"Band box regt.\" — white shirts and collars","Brigade commander at Alexandria; 153rd NY his 'band box regiment'","Military governor of Alexandria","Military governor of Alexandria — Capt. Rich now on his staff","Wants 26th NY band"],"rel":"","lc":4,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-04-001","LTR-1862-12-08-001","LTR-1863-07-20-001","LTR-1863-12-09-002"],"first":"1862-12-04","last":"1863-12-09","s":"Gen. John P. Slough served as military governor of Alexandria and brigade commander over the 153rd New York, which he called his \"band box regiment\" for their regulation white shirts and collars. He placed Capt. Rich on his staff as aide-de-camp, pursued the 26th New York's band for his headquarters, and his proximity to Charles's daily life made him one of the most frequently mentioned commanders in the collection."},{"id":"PER-jim-lucas","n":"Jim Lucas","cat":"civilian","roles":["Heard gossip about Savages","Informed on ration thieves","One of 3 literate men in tent (tentmate from Nov 18 letter)","Promoted again to Corporal"],"rel":"","lc":4,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-26-001","LTR-1863-07-06-001","LTR-1863-07-25-001","LTR-1864-02-15-001"],"first":"1862-12-26","last":"1864-02-15","s":"Jim Lucas was Charles's tentmate in the 153rd New York and one of only three literate men among their twelve tent occupants, sharing the task of letter-writing for illiterate comrades. He informed on the ration theft ring that led to arrests in his company, was himself arrested as a corporal in the scandal, but was later promoted back to corporal — a small arc of military disgrace and redemption."},{"id":"PER-rye","n":"Mr. Rye","cat":"civilian","roles":["\"Persecuting a helpless woman\" — land dispute escalated","Champlain resident sent back from Canadian border","Land claim continues — boundary dispute","Land claim — will pay over appraisal value"],"rel":"","lc":4,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-01-02-001","LTR-1863-03-02-001","LTR-1863-04-16-001","LTR-1865-01-11-001"],"first":"1863-01-02","last":"1865-01-11","s":"Mr. Rye was a Champlain neighbor whose persistent land claim against the Hubbell property infuriated Alexander, who called him \"the old rascal\" for \"persecuting a helpless woman\" — their mother Frances. The boundary dispute ran from 1863 through at least 1865, when Rye was turned back from the Canadian border during the post-St. Albans Raid military lockdown of Champlain."},{"id":"PER-brookway","n":"Orderly Sgt. Brookway","cat":"military","roles":["Co. H — wounded at Gettysburg","Commands Co. — formerly Orderly Sergt.","Has charge of Alexander's mail","Still with company"],"rel":"","lc":4,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-05-16-001","LTR-1863-07-04-001","LTR-1864-08-22-001","LTR-1864-08-30-001"],"first":"1863-05-16","last":"1864-08-30","s":"Orderly Sergeant P.H. Brookway of Company H, 60th New York served alongside Alexander through the war's major campaigns, wounded at both Chancellorsville and Gettysburg. He rose from orderly sergeant to commissioned 1st Lieutenant commanding the company by 1864, handling Alexander's mail during his hospitalization near Atlanta and representing the steady NCO leadership that held the depleted regiment together."},{"id":"PER-strain","n":"Capt. Strain","cat":"military","roles":["Arranged clerk position — looked at handwriting","Arranged reassignment — booked for Savannah","Promoted to Major","Wife visited camp","Wife — wrote to husband on Charles's behalf"],"rel":"","lc":4,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-05-19-001","LTR-1863-10-12-001","LTR-1864-01-08-001","LTR-1864-03-06-001"],"first":"1863-05-19","last":"1864-03-06","s":"Major (later Lt. Col.) Strain was the 153rd New York officer who most directly shaped Charles's wartime experience, arranging his clerk position at General Banks's headquarters in New Orleans after examining his handwriting. His wife interceded with him on Charles's behalf at Mother's urging, and Dr. Sweeney's parallel care for both Charles and James made Strain and Sweeney the two officers the family most credited with protecting their sons."},{"id":"PER-boyle","n":"Mrs. Boyle","cat":"civilian","roles":["Bought cow — good friend of family","Sends Tribune newspaper to Charles","Sends papers to Charles","Sent N.Y. Tribune and papers","Sent papers including McClellan speech"],"rel":"","lc":4,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-07-30-001","LTR-1863-08-21-001","LTR-1864-06-20-001","LTR-1864-07-00-001"],"first":"1863-07-30","last":"1864-07-00","s":"Mrs. Boyle was a loyal friend of the Hubbell family in Champlain who regularly sent newspapers — particularly the New York Tribune — to both Charles and Alexander throughout the war. Her husband bought the family cow, and her consistent paper shipments, including draft lists and McClellan's speeches, kept the brothers connected to home-front news and politics from Alexandria to Georgia."},{"id":"PER-thomas","n":"Gen. Thomas","cat":"military","roles":["Detachment on duty with him","Expected to take offensive","Now Lieut./Col.","Visited troops — \"tremendous large man, beard snow white\""],"rel":"","lc":4,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-01-08-001","LTR-1864-04-18-001","LTR-1864-08-08-002","LTR-1864-12-12-002"],"first":"1864-01-08","last":"1864-12-12","s":"Gen. George H. Thomas appeared in Alexander's letters first as the commander whose detachment Alexander \"clandestinely\" left in Nashville to rejoin his regiment, and later as the imposing figure who visited troops before the Atlanta Campaign — \"a tremendous large man, beard snow white.\" Alexander was present at Nashville in December 1864, three days before Thomas's crushing defeat of Hood's Confederate army."},{"id":"PER-marshall-dunn","n":"Marshall Dunn","cat":"civilian","roles":["Bounty handler","Collected $5 from each soldier for bounty services","Has furlough papers — Orderly written to send them","State bounty — $5 fee"],"rel":"","lc":4,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-02-18-001","LTR-1864-03-07-001","LTR-1864-03-20-001","LTR-1864-04-18-001"],"first":"1864-02-18","last":"1864-04-18","s":"Marshall Dunn was the bounty handler who collected a five-dollar fee from each soldier in Alexander's regiment for processing state bounty claims. Alexander mentioned him in multiple letters from Nashville and Stevenson, Alabama in early 1864, part of the financial apparatus surrounding re-enlistment bounties that the brothers navigated with Mother and Frances as their \"business agents in Champlain.\""},{"id":"PER-sweeney","n":"Dr. Sweeney","cat":"military","roles":["Friend — \"did all he could for James & I\"","Letter to Mother — inquired if received","Sent James to hospital","Very kind to James and Charles — wished to transfer"],"rel":"","lc":4,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-08-26-001","LTR-1864-09-21-001","LTR-1864-10-22-001","LTR-1864-12-27-001"],"first":"1864-08-26","last":"1864-12-27","s":"Dr. Sweeney was the regimental surgeon of the 153rd New York who looked after both Charles and James Hubbell with particular care, sending James to hospital when he fell ill and doing \"all he could\" for both brothers on the march through the Shenandoah Valley. He wrote directly to Mother about the brothers' welfare, and Charles credited him alongside Lt. Col. Strain as the officers who made their service survivable."},{"id":"PER-kellogg","n":"Corporal Kellogg","cat":"military","roles":["\"Old sputterer\" — domestic tension","Corporal, Co. D","Corporal, Co. D — confirmed POW in Richmond"],"rel":"","lc":3,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-09-11-001","LTR-1861-10-17-001","LTR-1862-03-06-001"],"first":"1861-09-11","last":"1862-03-06","s":"Corporal Kellogg of Company D, 34th New York was confirmed as a prisoner of war in Richmond after being captured in a skirmish in late 1861, his fate verified when a fellow prisoner escaped and returned to the regiment. Alexander later referred to a Mr. Kellogg in Champlain as an \"old sputterer\" involved in domestic tensions — possibly a relative, reflecting the small-town networks that connected home and front."},{"id":"PER-brinkhoff","n":"Mrs. Brinkhoff","cat":"civilian","roles":["Acquaintance of Edgar Graves","Coming back — good for Mother","Respects sent"],"rel":"","lc":3,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-09-21-001","LTR-1862-05-12-001","LTR-1862-06-13-001"],"first":"1861-09-21","last":"1862-06-13","s":"Mrs. Brinkhoff was a Champlain acquaintance connected to Edgar Graves of nearby Chazy who appeared in Henry's letters from 1861 through 1862. Henry sent her respects from the Peninsula Campaign, and her anticipated return to Champlain was noted as good news for Mother — one of the civilian women whose presence or absence affected Frances Hubbell's increasingly isolated home life."},{"id":"PER-the-colonel","n":"\"The Colonel\"","cat":"military","roles":["Regimental colonel — \"small, smart, energetic-looking\"","Regimental colonel — criticized for baggage decision","Regimental colonel — drilling officers below prayer meeting"],"rel":"","lc":3,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-09-26-001","LTR-1861-09-27-002","LTR-1861-11-13-001"],"first":"1861-09-26","last":"1861-11-13","s":"The unnamed colonel of Alexander's 60th New York regiment at Camp Wheeler was described as \"small, smart, energetic-looking\" and appeared in Alexander's earliest letters pursuing soldiers who breached camp security and drilling officers during prayer meeting time. Alexander criticized his baggage management decisions during the regiment's journey from Ogdensburg to Baltimore, an early example of the enlisted man's perspective on command."},{"id":"PER-miner","n":"Lieut. Miner","cat":"military","roles":["Going to Charleston for signal corps","Long talk this evening","Officer (rank unclear), knows Frances; commends her school"],"rel":"","lc":3,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-05-001","LTR-1862-02-12-001","LTR-1862-07-14-001"],"first":"1862-02-05","last":"1862-07-14","s":"Lieutenant Miner of the 34th New York knew the Hubbell family well enough to commend Frances's school in Montreal and approve of James's West Point appointment. He departed for signal corps duty at Charleston in early 1862, and Alexander later reported having a long talk with him on Pine Mountain, Virginia — an officer who bridged the family's civilian and military worlds."},{"id":"PER-dodds","n":"Dodds","cat":"civilian","roles":["Pasturage source","Shot Bass — \"must have been deranged\"","Young man headed to army — warned about reality"],"rel":"","lc":3,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-03-06-001","LTR-1862-05-10-001","LTR-1864-05-20-001"],"first":"1862-03-06","last":"1864-05-20","s":"Dodds was a young Champlain man whom Alexander warned about the realities of army life in 1862, who later provided pasturage for the family's animals, and who shocked the community by shooting the schoolteacher Mr. Bass in 1864. Alexander received the news in Georgia and concluded Dodds \"must have been deranged\" — a violent eruption on the home front that rivaled the battlefield for drama."},{"id":"PER-beauregard","n":"Gen. Beauregard","cat":"military","roles":["Confederate commander at Corinth","Confederate general — reportedly coming to capture Yankees","Confederate general; rebels do not like him"],"rel":"","lc":3,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-04-01-001","LTR-1862-06-13-002","LTR-1862-06-22-001"],"first":"1862-04-01","last":"1862-06-22","s":"Confederate Gen. P.G.T. Beauregard appeared in the Hubbell letters as a figure of both military concern and contempt — Alexander expected a great battle with him at Corinth in April 1862, Henry reported that Confederate spectators expected Beauregard to capture Yankees at Fair Oaks, and Henry noted that Southern soldiers themselves \"do not like\" him."},{"id":"PER-emerson-smith","n":"Emerson Smith","cat":"civilian","roles":["At convalescent camp","Used all his wages"],"rel":"","lc":3,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-05-01-002","LTR-1863-03-19-001","LTR-1863-03-29-002"],"first":"1862-05-01","last":"1863-03-29","s":"Emerson Smith was a soldier from the Champlain area who used all his wages at Fortress Monroe in 1862 and later cycled through the convalescent camp system near Alexandria, where Charles tracked his status alongside other local men. His trajectory through the army's medical and disciplinary systems was typical of the Champlain community members the Hubbell brothers monitored throughout the war."},{"id":"PER-george-corbin","n":"George Corbin","cat":"civilian","roles":["16th Regt; shook hands","With 126th Regt"],"rel":"","lc":3,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-05-09-002","LTR-1863-04-27-001","LTR-1863-05-13-001"],"first":"1862-05-09","last":"1863-05-13","s":"George Corbin was a Clinton County acquaintance whom Henry encountered in the 16th Regiment at West Point, Virginia in May 1862, shaking hands amid the Peninsula Campaign. Charles later noted Corbin's service with the 126th Regiment near Alexandria in 1863, part of the web of familiar faces from home that connected the brothers to their community across multiple theaters of war."},{"id":"PER-david-savage","n":"David Savage","cat":"civilian","roles":["Regards from Charles","Regards sent","Wants secesh bill"],"rel":"","lc":3,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-07-12-001","LTR-1863-07-06-001","LTR-1864-04-23-001"],"first":"1862-07-12","last":"1864-04-23","s":"David Savage was a Champlain acquaintance to whom both Charles and Alexander sent regards through their letters, and who expressed interest in receiving a Confederate (\"secesh\") bill as a curiosity from the front. His family name recurred throughout the correspondence in connection with farm business, debts, and community news."},{"id":"PER-armstrong","n":"Col. Armstrong","cat":"military","roles":["Appointed Charles 1st Corporal","Ex-Lieut. Col., ex-Col. — must give account","Made speech at Plattsburgh persuading men to go"],"rel":"","lc":3,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-09-09-001","LTR-1862-10-15-001","LTR-1864-01-19-001"],"first":"1862-09-09","last":"1864-01-19","s":"Col. Armstrong of the 153rd New York appointed Charles as 1st Corporal upon enlistment and made recruiting speeches at Plattsburgh, but his career unraveled amid bounty fraud allegations and officer conflicts. Charles noted he was \"routed out of Plattsburgh\" by a lawsuit and eventually had to \"give account\" as ex-lieutenant colonel — a commanding officer whose trajectory from recruiter to accused fraud mirrored the regiment's loss of innocence."},{"id":"PER-b--misner","n":"B. Misner","cat":"civilian","roles":["Home on unauthorized furlough, politics changed, saw Confederate Hubbell","Prisoner in Richmond","Returned from Richmond — saw all Antietam prisoners, Henry not among them"],"rel":"","lc":3,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-10-06-001","LTR-1862-10-29-001","LTR-1862-11-10-001"],"first":"1862-10-06","last":"1862-11-10","s":"B. Misner was a Champlain soldier taken prisoner and held in Richmond, where he saw a young Hubbell serving as a Confederate officer — evidence that the family had members on both sides of the war. Upon his return to Champlain in October 1862, Misner delivered the devastating news directly to Mother that he had seen all the Antietam prisoners and Henry was not among them, effectively ending the prisoner theory that had sustained the family's hope."},{"id":"PER-savagham","n":"Savagham","cat":"civilian","roles":["\"Never been heard from\" — missing/deserted?","Charles's sleeping partner — \"short, chunky, Frenchman\"","Heard from Mathews — brother most likely in hospital"],"rel":"","lc":3,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-08-001","LTR-1863-02-13-001","LTR-1863-03-19-001"],"first":"1862-12-08","last":"1863-03-19","s":"Savagham was Charles's sleeping partner in the 153rd New York, described as a \"short, chunky, Frenchman,\" who relayed information from Mathews in Henry's regiment suggesting Henry was \"most likely in a hospital.\" He eventually disappeared from the letters with the ominous note \"never been heard from\" — possibly missing or deserted, another of the regiment's quiet casualties."},{"id":"PER-savage","n":"Mr. Savage","cat":"civilian","roles":["Financial settlement pending — $16.55","Holds Alexander's $500","Sold lots at $500/acre near Mrs. Turner's","`stated`"],"rel":"","lc":3,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-01-02-001","LTR-1863-08-21-001","LTR-1864-06-08-001"],"first":"1863-01-02","last":"1864-06-08","s":"Mr. Savage was a Champlain neighbor involved in the family's financial affairs who sold lots near Mrs. Turner's property at five hundred dollars per acre and held five hundred dollars of Alexander's savings. Charles tracked debts owed to and from the Savages, part of the complex web of small-town financial obligations the brothers managed from the field."},{"id":"PER-knapp","n":"Lieut. Knapp","cat":"military","roles":["Gone to Fortress Monroe","Gone to Moorestown on leave","Shook hands with Mrs. Burdick; called for Hubbell and Staats"],"rel":"","lc":3,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-02-13-001","LTR-1863-02-22-001","LTR-1863-09-29-001"],"first":"1863-02-13","last":"1863-09-29","s":"Lieutenant Knapp of the 153rd New York was an officer who socialized with Champlain soldiers and their visiting families, shaking hands with Mrs. Burdick in Alexandria and traveling with A. Douglas to Fortress Monroe on guard duty. His departures on leave to Moorestown marked the rhythms of garrison life that Charles documented from Alexandria and Capitol Hill."},{"id":"PER-col--armstrong","n":"Lieut. Col. Armstrong","cat":"military","roles":["Reportedly \"routed out of Plattsburgh\" — bounty fraud lawsuit","Resigned after difficulty with Col.; said goodbye to Co.","Visited with Knapp and Douglas"],"rel":"","lc":3,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-02-13-001","LTR-1863-02-22-001","LTR-1863-03-02-001"],"first":"1863-02-13","last":"1863-03-02","s":"Lt. Col. Armstrong of the 153rd New York resigned after a dispute with the regimental colonel in early 1863, said goodbye to his company, and was later \"routed out of Plattsburgh\" over bounty fraud allegations — friends of soldiers suing him over a reported $270 discrepancy. His fall from second-in-command to defendant illustrated the corruption that plagued the bounty system."},{"id":"PER-abraham-lincoln","n":"Abraham Lincoln","cat":"civilian","roles":["Visited Alexandria — cannon salute","Will vote for him","Would vote for him \"square and fair\""],"rel":"","lc":3,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-04-13-001","LTR-1864-10-26-001","LTR-1864-10-29-001"],"first":"1863-04-13","last":"1864-10-29","s":"Abraham Lincoln appeared in the Hubbell letters as a visitor to Alexandria in 1863, received with a cannon salute, and as the beneficiary of a family-wide political shift by November 1864. Alexander, who had favored McClellan, declared he would have voted \"square and fair\" for Lincoln; James cast his ballot for Lincoln from a Baltimore hospital; the family's wartime experience transformed their politics."},{"id":"PER-amos-luther","n":"Amos Luther","cat":"civilian","roles":["Now sergeant — well, \"not a vine in his mouth\"","Promoted to Howes's place","Wrote on Alexander's behalf — father visited"],"rel":"","lc":3,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-06-10-001","LTR-1863-08-25-001","LTR-1863-09-29-001"],"first":"1863-06-10","last":"1863-09-29","s":"Amos Luther, Alexander's closest companion in Company H of the 60th New York, was promoted to sergeant after Howes was reduced for drunkenness in August 1863. Luther wrote on Alexander's behalf, his father visited the regiment, and Charles reported him looking \"fat and healthy\" and without \"a vine in his mouth\" — a trusted comrade who rose through the ranks alongside Alexander."},{"id":"PER-steais","n":"Mr. Steais","cat":"civilian","roles":["Has draft list from home","Hospital again — breast complaint","Visiting brother Pet; 113th NY; convalescent camp; discharge soon"],"rel":"","lc":3,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-07-20-001","LTR-1863-10-31-001","LTR-1863-12-01-002"],"first":"1863-07-20","last":"1863-12-01","s":"Mr. Steais was a Champlain connection who brought draft lists from home to Charles at Capitol Hill and visited his brother Pet in the 113th New York at the convalescent camp. He suffered recurring hospital stays for a breast complaint, and his presence in Washington allowed Charles to exchange hometown news amid the garrison routine."},{"id":"PER-pet-steais","n":"Pet Steais","cat":"civilian","roles":["Getting discharge","Home — Charles suspects malingering","Hospital — chest problem"],"rel":"","lc":3,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-09-15-001","LTR-1863-11-19-001","LTR-1863-12-09-002"],"first":"1863-09-15","last":"1863-12-09","s":"Pet Steais served in the 153rd New York alongside Charles and was repeatedly hospitalized for a chest problem, eventually securing a discharge that Charles suspected was malingered. Charles noted Steais was \"home\" while the regiment remained in service — one of several soldiers whose medical exits contrasted with the Hubbell brothers' determination to serve their full terms."},{"id":"PER-james-sweeney","n":"Dr. James Sweeney","cat":"military","roles":["Asst. Surgeon — saw Frances 3 weeks before","In charge of Division Hospital","New surgeon — from their area; \"little faller\""],"rel":"","lc":3,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-06-21-001","LTR-1864-06-22-001","LTR-1865-07-13-001"],"first":"1864-06-21","last":"1865-07-13","s":"Dr. James Sweeney joined the 153rd New York as assistant surgeon in Louisiana in 1864, having seen Frances Hubbell shortly before departing Champlain. James called him a \"little faller\" from their area, and by war's end he was in charge of the Division Hospital in Savannah — a hometown doctor whose presence in the regiment gave the Hubbell brothers a medical advocate they could trust."},{"id":"PER-schofield","n":"Gen. Schofield","cat":"military","roles":["Army moved to right — battle","Commands 23rd Corps — moving to right","Forces on right — successful attack"],"rel":"","lc":3,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-08-02-001","LTR-1864-08-08-001","LTR-1864-08-08-002"],"first":"1864-08-02","last":"1864-08-08","s":"Gen. John M. Schofield commanded the 23rd Corps during the Atlanta Campaign, where Alexander tracked his flanking movements toward the Macon & Atlanta railroad in August 1864. Alexander noted Schofield's forces driving the enemy and cutting the last remaining road into Atlanta, part of the coordinated squeeze that forced the city's evacuation on September 2."},{"id":"PER-col--strain","n":"Lieut. Col. Strain","cat":"military","roles":["Got Charles commissary/Banks HQ position — very friendly","Wounded in arm — fight of 19th","Wounded — did he lose a leg?"],"rel":"","lc":3,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-10-22-001","LTR-1864-12-09-001","LTR-1864-12-27-001"],"first":"1864-10-22","last":"1864-12-27","s":"Lt. Col. Strain of the 153rd New York was wounded in the arm at Cedar Creek in October 1864, and James later wondered from his Philadelphia hospital whether Strain had lost a leg. Strain had previously arranged Charles's headquarters clerk position and was consistently described as \"very friendly\" to the Hubbell brothers — an officer whose personal advocacy shaped their wartime paths."},{"id":"PER-aunt-l","n":"Aunt L.","cat":"civilian","roles":["Aunt (Henry wrote to her)","Aunt (initial only)"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-07-21-003","LTR-1861-09-22-001"],"first":"1861-07-21","last":"1861-09-22","s":"Aunt L. appeared only by initial in Henry's and Alexander's letters, mentioned as a family member Henry wrote to and whose nephew was reportedly a Confederate officer seen in Richmond by B. Misner. Her identity remained partially obscured by the transcription's abbreviation."},{"id":"PER-the-captain","n":"\"The Captain\" (Ransom)","cat":"military","roles":["Company captain","Company captain, going home"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-09-22-001","LTR-1861-10-03-001"],"first":"1861-09-22","last":"1861-10-03","s":"\"The Captain\" referred to Capt. J.B. Ransom, who commanded Alexander's Company H in the 60th New York and whom Henry referenced in his earliest letters as the officer forming the company that Alexander would join. He appeared in Alexander's letters both as a field leader and as a figure traveling home — a constant presence in Alexander's military life."},{"id":"PER-albert-savage","n":"Albert Savage","cat":"civilian","roles":["Dead — Alexander astonished; Henry had \"thought a great deal on\" his death","Died — friend/acquaintance from home"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-11-15-001","LTR-1862-02-23-001"],"first":"1861-11-15","last":"1862-02-23","s":"Albert Savage was a friend or acquaintance from the Champlain area whose death astonished Alexander and deeply affected Henry, who had \"thought a great deal on\" it. Willie Savage, likely a relative, wrote to Alexander without mentioning Albert's death — a gap in communication that Alexander found troubling."},{"id":"PER-the-boys","n":"\"the boys\"","cat":"civilian","roles":["Brothers at home working the farm","James and Charles — anxious about Shenandoah fight"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-11-30-003","LTR-1864-09-21-002"],"first":"1861-11-30","last":"1864-09-21","s":"\"The boys\" referred collectively to James and Charles as the younger brothers still at home working the Hubbell farm before their enlistments, and later to them as soldiers whose safety in the Shenandoah Valley campaign caused anxious waiting."},{"id":"PER-the-major","n":"\"The Major\"","cat":"military","roles":["Regimental major — complimented Ransom","Regimental major, went to Relay House"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-12-08-002","LTR-1861-12-16-001"],"first":"1861-12-08","last":"1861-12-16","s":"\"The Major\" was the unnamed regimental major of the 60th New York who complimented Capt. Ransom's leadership and traveled to the Relay House on regimental business, mentioned in Alexander's early Baltimore garrison letters as part of the regiment's officer corps."},{"id":"PER-partridge","n":"Mr. Partridge","cat":"civilian","roles":["Champlain acquaintance — Charles's teacher?","Hosting convention James attends"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-12-08-002","LTR-1862-01-28-001"],"first":"1861-12-08","last":"1862-02-04","s":"Mr. Partridge was a Champlain acquaintance — possibly Charles's former teacher — who hosted a convention that James attended. He represented the educational and civic community in Champlain that shaped the Hubbell brothers before their military service."},{"id":"PER-hayward","n":"Colonel Hayward","cat":"military","roles":["Regimental colonel — dissatisfied with Co. C, moved Co. H","Resigned — 60th NY \"Orphan Regiment\""],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-12-22-001","LTR-1862-01-28-001"],"first":"1861-12-22","last":"1862-02-04","s":"Colonel Hayward commanded the 60th New York early in the war, reorganizing companies when dissatisfied with Co. C's performance by moving Co. H into their position. His resignation left the regiment as an \"Orphan Regiment\" without a permanent colonel — a leadership vacuum that affected the 60th's cohesion."},{"id":"PER-orderly-dickinson","n":"Orderly Dickinson","cat":"civilian","roles":["Bought paper, envelopes, bread for Alexander with own money","Orderly — out in city Dec 26"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-12-23-001","LTR-1862-09-27-001"],"first":"1861-12-23","last":"1862-09-27","s":"Orderly Dickinson of Alexander's company bought paper, envelopes, and bread for Alexander with his own money, a small act of generosity that Alexander recorded with gratitude. He appeared in the letters during the Baltimore garrison period as one of the company's NCOs managing daily logistics."},{"id":"PER-grandmother-mcniel","n":"Frances M. Hubbell (Mother)","cat":"family","roles":["\"Real patriot\" — letter prized; visit planned","Alexander has written to her"],"rel":"Mother","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-12-23-001","LTR-1862-05-10-001"],"first":"1861-12-23","last":"1862-05-10","s":"Grandmother McNiel (also McNeill) was Frances Hubbell's mother, whom Alexander called a \"real patriot\" who had \"sent two sons to the war\" and whose letters he prized. She lived in Charlotte, New York, and the brothers urged Mother to visit her — a matriarch whose own sacrifice mirrored her daughter's."},{"id":"PER-wheeler","n":"Mr. Wheeler","cat":"civilian","roles":["Confederate cavalry — raided railroad","Helping James — \"how kind\""],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-01-24-001","LTR-1864-09-02-001"],"first":"1862-01-24","last":"1864-09-02","s":"Mr. Wheeler appeared in two distinct contexts: as a Confederate cavalry raider who disrupted railroad operations in the Western theater, and as someone helping James — \"how kind\" — suggesting either two different individuals or a complex civilian who aided the family despite wartime divisions."},{"id":"PER-green","n":"General Green","cat":"military","roles":["Colonel promoted to Brig. Gen.; descendant of Rev. War General Greene","New/expected colonel"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-01-28-001","LTR-1862-05-01-001"],"first":"1862-02-04","last":"1862-05-01","s":"Colonel Green of the 60th New York was promoted to Brigadier General and identified as a descendant of Revolutionary War General Nathanael Greene. His arrival as the new or expected colonel marked a command transition in the regiment after Colonel Hayward's departure."},{"id":"PER-lablue","n":"Lablue","cat":"civilian","roles":["Getting along very well","Member of Capt. Ransom's Company, 60th NY; sent word to \"his brother\" (Alexander?) about Henry"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-01-28-001","LTR-1862-10-01-001"],"first":"1862-02-04","last":"1862-10-01","s":"LaBlue was a member of Capt. Ransom's Company H in the 60th New York who sent word about Henry's fate and was reported to be \"getting along very well\" in service. He served as an informal messenger within the Champlain soldiers' network that connected the Hubbell brothers across regiments."},{"id":"PER-holcomb","n":"Mr. Holcomb","cat":"civilian","roles":["On same boat from Burlington — going to New York","Respects sent","Respects sent to -- acquaintance in Champlain"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-01-28-001","LTR-1862-07-27-001"],"first":"1862-02-04","last":"1862-07-27","s":"Mr. Holcomb was a Champlain acquaintance who traveled on the same boat from Burlington as one of the Hubbell family and was heading to New York. Respects were sent to him in multiple letters, marking him as part of the family's civilian social circle."},{"id":"PER-cook","n":"Mr. Cook","cat":"civilian","roles":["Anxious about Albert — not heard from in a month","Going to Charleston for signal corps with Miner"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-05-001","LTR-1862-10-06-001"],"first":"1862-02-05","last":"1862-10-06","s":"Mr. Cook was a Champlain father anxiously awaiting news of his son Albert, who had not been heard from for over a month after the Battle of Shepherdstown in 1862. His anxiety paralleled Mother's anguish over Henry — another Champlain family caught in the silence that followed battle."},{"id":"PER-ladue","n":"Col. Ladue","cat":"military","roles":["Returned to command 34th NY","Returned to regiment; rumored promoted to Brigadier General"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-05-002","LTR-1862-02-24-001"],"first":"1862-02-05","last":"1862-02-24","s":"Col. Ladue commanded the 34th New York and was rumored to have been promoted to Brigadier General upon his return to the regiment in early 1862. Henry noted his return at Camp McClellan as the regiment prepared to march toward Harpers Ferry, a command change that coincided with the 34th's transition from garrison to active campaigning."},{"id":"PER-hubbell","n":"Henry Hubbell","cat":"family","roles":["Mother, family correspondent","Recipient, home correspondent"],"rel":"Son","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-05-003","LTR-1862-02-07-001"],"first":"1862-02-05","last":"1862-02-07","s":"Henry Hubbell, the eldest Hubbell brother, served in the 34th New York from 1861 until his death at Antietam on September 17, 1862. His disappearance — seen falling with rebels within two rods, never confirmed dead or prisoner — became the central mystery of the family's correspondence, driving months of anguished inquiry by Mother, Charles, and Alexander."},{"id":"PER-dobbin","n":"Dobbin","cat":"civilian","roles":["Academy trustee with \"scolding wife\"","Possibly remaining another term (school? military?)"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-11-001","LTR-1862-03-06-001"],"first":"1862-02-11","last":"1862-03-06","s":"Dobbin was a Champlain figure who served as an Academy trustee with a \"scolding wife\" and was possibly remaining for another term in some capacity. Alexander mentioned the Dobbins' domestic quarrels — including a memorable orange-peel argument — as hometown gossip shared between brothers."},{"id":"PER-george-dodds","n":"George Dodds","cat":"civilian","roles":["Fellow soldier, financially troubled","In hospital, \"run down,\" applying for discharge"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-11-002","LTR-1862-06-06-001"],"first":"1862-02-11","last":"1862-06-06","s":"George Dodds was a fellow soldier from the Champlain area who was financially troubled in the army and later ended up in the hospital, \"run down\" and applying for discharge. His declining health represented the slow attrition of disease and exhaustion that claimed more soldiers than combat."},{"id":"PER-aunt-laurie","n":"Aunt Laurie","cat":"civilian","roles":["Aunt, negligent correspondent (waits 4–6 weeks to reply); \"turned rebel\" (joking)","Correspondent (lazy with mail)"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-17-001","LTR-1862-02-23-001"],"first":"1862-02-17","last":"1862-02-23","s":"Aunt Laurie (also Laura) was a family correspondent whom Alexander teased mercilessly for her slow replies, joking that she had \"turned rebel\" for her negligence. She waited four to six weeks to answer letters, prompting Alexander to worry in a postscript that he had blown her up \"too hard\" and she might stop writing entirely."},{"id":"PER-willie-savage","n":"Willie Savage","cat":"civilian","roles":["Letter received; prospering","New correspondent"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-17-001","LTR-1862-04-11-001"],"first":"1862-02-17","last":"1862-04-11","s":"Willie Savage was a new correspondent who wrote to Alexander from Champlain and was reported to be prospering. His letter notably omitted any mention of Albert Savage's death, an absence that puzzled and bothered Alexander when he learned the news from other sources."},{"id":"PER-willie","n":"Willie","cat":"civilian","roles":["Taking care of Pony","Wrote Alexander recently; said nothing about Albert Savage's death"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-23-001","LTR-1863-02-28-001"],"first":"1862-02-23","last":"1863-02-28","s":"Willie, likely Willie Savage, was entrusted with caring for the family pony at home and corresponded with Alexander without mentioning the death of Albert Savage — an omission that unsettled Alexander when he learned of it separately."},{"id":"PER-webb","n":"Lieut. Webb","cat":"military","roles":["\"Miserable old hag\" — source of mother's distress","Invited Henry to his tent — \"very fine fellow\""],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-03-06-001","LTR-1862-07-12-001"],"first":"1862-03-06","last":"1862-07-12","s":"The name Webb appeared in two distinct contexts: Mrs. Webb was a Champlain resident whom Alexander called a \"miserable old hag\" for causing his mother distress, while Lt. Webb was a 34th New York officer who invited Henry to his tent and was described as \"a very fine fellow\" during the Peninsula Campaign."},{"id":"PER-peter-guyan","n":"Peter Guyan","cat":"civilian","roles":["Tapped Henry's boots for $1","Tapped boots — $1"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-03-21-001","LTR-1862-05-01-002"],"first":"1862-03-21","last":"1862-05-01","s":"Peter Guyan tapped Henry's boots for one dollar at Fortress Monroe, a small transaction that Henry recorded in his careful financial accounting alongside sutler debts, cook fees, and express charges — the granular economics of a private soldier's pay."},{"id":"PER-henry-cooper","n":"Henry Cooper","cat":"civilian","roles":["Being discharged; carrying letter to mail from Washington","Sick, likely discharged"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-03-23-001","LTR-1862-04-02-002"],"first":"1862-03-23","last":"1862-04-02","s":"Henry Cooper was a soldier being discharged on medical grounds who carried Henry's letter to mail from Washington, and was separately noted as sick and likely to be discharged. He served as one of the informal couriers soldiers relied on when the postal system was uncertain."},{"id":"PER-aunt-mary","n":"Aunt Mary","cat":"civilian","roles":["Southern connection — \"pities us\"; husband drills on double quick","Will come North"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-04-01-001","LTR-1864-11-04-001"],"first":"1862-04-01","last":"1864-11-04","s":"Aunt Mary was one of the Hubbell family's Southern connections whose husband drilled on the double quick for the Confederacy while she \"pities us\" from the South. Alexander's rage at the family's Confederate relatives was directed partly at her household, though he noted she planned to come North — a family divided by the war."},{"id":"PER-professor-bass","n":"Professor Bass","cat":"civilian","roles":["Returning to Champlain — \"half rebel,\" \"old croaker\"","Teacher — Alexander likes him"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-04-01-001","LTR-1863-11-21-001"],"first":"1862-04-01","last":"1863-11-21","s":"Professor Bass was a Champlain schoolteacher whom Alexander called a \"half rebel\" and \"old croaker\" for his Southern sympathies, yet also acknowledged liking him personally. Bass was later shot — probably by Dodds — in a sensational 1864 incident that Alexander found grimly amusing from the battlefields of Georgia."},{"id":"PER-cooper","n":"Gen. Cooper","cat":"military","roles":["Harangued brigade on July 4th","Still present; Henry mailing both letters through him"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-04-06-001","LTR-1862-07-04-001"],"first":"1862-04-06","last":"1862-07-04","s":"Gen. Cooper was a brigade officer who harangued the troops on the Fourth of July and through whom Henry mailed letters home. He appeared in Henry's earliest correspondence as part of the command structure near Washington in 1861."},{"id":"PER-little-doolittle","n":"\"Little Doolittle\"","cat":"civilian","roles":["Insulted James; Alexander wants to flog him","Nickname or reference — unclear"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-04-11-001","LTR-1862-07-26-001"],"first":"1862-04-11","last":"1862-07-26","s":"Little Doolittle was an academy peer who bullied and insulted James Hubbell, prompting Alexander to write about the incident with protective indignation in February 1862."},{"id":"PER-s-p--hubbell","n":"S.P. Hubbell","cat":"civilian","roles":["\"Prospects of S.P. Hubbell\" — on magazine cover","Attorney-at-law, Champlain — sarcasm about not enlisting"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-05-10-001","LTR-1864-04-07-001"],"first":"1862-05-10","last":"1864-04-07","s":"S.P. Hubbell was an attorney-at-law in Champlain whom Alexander mocked with heavy sarcasm for his patriotic rhetoric without enlisting. Alexander noted his \"prospects\" appearing on a magazine cover and derided him as one of the \"noble patriots\" who talked about the war while others fought it."},{"id":"PER-jeff-davis","n":"Jeff Davis","cat":"civilian","roles":["Confederate president; rebels \"do not like Jeff\"","Sarcasm — Confederate casualty lists"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-06-22-001","LTR-1864-08-08-002"],"first":"1862-06-22","last":"1864-08-08","s":"Jefferson Davis, the Confederate president, appeared in the Hubbell letters as a figure of contempt — Henry reported that Southern soldiers themselves \"do not like Jeff,\" and Alexander invoked him sarcastically when discussing Confederate casualty lists during the Atlanta Campaign."},{"id":"PER-doolittle","n":"Mrs. Doolittle","cat":"civilian","roles":["Came with Capt. Ransom to visit","Says husband doesn't have enough to eat"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-10-02-001","LTR-1862-12-08-001"],"first":"1862-10-02","last":"1862-12-08","s":"Mrs. Doolittle came with Capt. Ransom to visit the troops and separately reported that her husband did not have enough to eat in the army. Her complaint represented the home-front perspective on soldier deprivation that reached Champlain through wives' reports."},{"id":"PER-r","n":"Mrs. R. (Mrs. Ransom)","cat":"civilian","roles":["Leaving next week — then Mother is \"alone\"","Will leave first of Nov."],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-10-06-001","LTR-1862-10-29-001"],"first":"1862-10-06","last":"1862-10-29","s":"Mrs. R. — Mrs. Ransom, wife of Capt. Ransom of the 60th New York — was staying in Champlain and her planned departure in November 1862 meant Mother would be left \"alone,\" underscoring Frances Hubbell's increasing isolation as her sons went to war and her companions departed."},{"id":"PER-murray","n":"Murray","cat":"civilian","roles":["Carrying towel, handkerchief, testament home for Charles","Renting the farm"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-10-15-001","LTR-1863-07-25-001"],"first":"1862-10-15","last":"1863-07-25","s":"Murray was a Champlain connection who carried Charles's towel, handkerchief, and testament home from Camp Mohawk during mobilization, and later rented the Hubbell farm — filling the practical roles of courier and caretaker that the family depended on with their sons away at war."},{"id":"PER-joel-savage","n":"Joel Savage","cat":"civilian","roles":["Owed $27.69","Separate acct. — $37.49"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-10-15-001","LTR-1863-08-21-001"],"first":"1862-10-15","last":"1863-08-21","s":"Joel Savage was a Champlain debtor who owed the Hubbell family $27.69 on one account and $37.49 on a separate one, part of the web of small financial obligations that Charles and Alexander tracked meticulously from the field and instructed Mother to collect."},{"id":"PER-frances-fannie","n":"Frances/Fannie","cat":"civilian","roles":["Sister — addressed directly, teased about destitution","Teaching French; likes Mr. Simons better than Mr. Karns"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-11-18-001","LTR-1863-01-13-001"],"first":"1862-11-18","last":"1863-01-13","s":"Frances (Fannie) Hubbell was the brothers' sister who taught French, preferred Mr. Simons over Mr. Karns at the Academy, and served as Mother's companion and the family's second correspondent at home. Alexander teased her about destitution and writing only \"when the fit is upon you,\" but she was essential to the family's emotional and practical survival."},{"id":"PER-charles-dodds","n":"Charles Dodds","cat":"civilian","roles":["\"Would not stay home for anything — too lonesome\"","Acquaintance — orderly for officer in Washington, $1/day"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-11-28-001","LTR-1863-04-11-001"],"first":"1862-11-28","last":"1863-04-11","s":"Charles Dodds was a Champlain acquaintance who worked as an orderly for an officer in Washington at one dollar per day and declared he \"would not stay home for anything — too lonesome.\" His preference for military service over civilian isolation echoed the restlessness that drew many small-town men into the army."},{"id":"PER-riley","n":"Miss Riley","cat":"civilian","roles":["Associated with Frances's school","From Corbeaus — receives papers that Charles reads"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-08-001","LTR-1864-02-18-001"],"first":"1862-12-08","last":"1864-02-18","s":"Riley (or Miss Riley) appeared in two contexts: as a soldier from Corbeaux who received newspapers that Charles borrowed and read, and as a woman associated with Frances's school. The name connected both the military and civilian threads of Champlain's wartime community."},{"id":"PER-pollard","n":"Pollard","cat":"civilian","roles":["Getting married to Mary Jane (Brinkerhoff girl)","May have married Mary Ann Brinkerhoff — Charles asks"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-08-001","LTR-1862-12-09-001"],"first":"1862-12-08","last":"1862-12-09","s":"Pollard was a Champlain man who was reportedly getting married to Mary Ann Brinkerhoff, a match Charles inquired about from Alexandria. His domestic news was the kind of hometown gossip that filled the margins of wartime letters alongside battle reports and financial instructions."},{"id":"PER-joe","n":"Joe","cat":"civilian","roles":["Doing chores at home","Whipping pony in stable — got a lecture"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-09-001","LTR-1863-01-02-001"],"first":"1862-12-09","last":"1863-01-02","s":"Joe was a young helper doing chores at the Hubbell home in Champlain who once got a lecture for whipping the pony in the stable. His presence — staying nights at the house, managing animals — reflected the family's reliance on hired help with the brothers away at war."},{"id":"PER-welky","n":"Sergeant Welky","cat":"military","roles":["Has History of Rome, France, U.S. in tent","One of 3 literate men in tent"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-26-001","LTR-1863-06-10-001"],"first":"1862-12-26","last":"1863-06-10","s":"Sergeant Welky was one of only three literate men in Charles's twelve-man tent in the 153rd New York and kept copies of the History of Rome, France, and the United States in the tent. His reading material and literacy made him an exception in a company where Charles wrote most of the other men's letters home."},{"id":"PER-dixon","n":"Mr. Dixon","cat":"civilian","roles":["Brought letter to hospital","Teacher in the teacher class — Frances helped Tombe during his recitation to Mr. Dixon"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-26-001","LTR-1864-08-22-001"],"first":"1862-12-26","last":"1864-08-22","s":"Mr. Dixon appeared as both a teacher in a teacher-training class where Frances helped S. Tombe during his recitation, and as the soldier who brought a letter to Alexander at the hospital near Atlanta. The name likely referred to two different individuals spanning the family's civilian and military worlds."},{"id":"PER-sigel","n":"Gen. Sigel","cat":"military","roles":["Commands Reserve Grand Division","Refused to sign furlough"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-01-02-001","LTR-1863-02-06-001"],"first":"1863-01-02","last":"1863-02-06","s":"Gen. Franz Sigel commanded the Reserve Grand Division that included Alexander's corps in early 1863 and later refused to sign a furlough, frustrating the soldiers under his authority. His name appeared in Alexander's organizational references as the brothers tracked which generals commanded which formations."},{"id":"PER-rosecrans","n":"Rosecrans","cat":"civilian","roles":["Possible destination for march","Possible reinforcement destination"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-01-13-001","LTR-1863-01-14-001"],"first":"1863-01-13","last":"1863-01-14","s":"Gen. William Rosecrans appeared in the Hubbell letters as a destination for possible reinforcement and the focus of Charles's strategic analysis — if Rosecrans was defeated, Charles predicted the war could last seven more years. The 12th Corps' transfer west to reinforce Rosecrans after Chickamauga directly affected Alexander's service trajectory."},{"id":"PER-simonds","n":"Mr. Simonds","cat":"civilian","roles":["Frances's friend","Teacher — lame wrist, rod for students"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-02-13-001","LTR-1863-08-25-001"],"first":"1863-02-13","last":"1863-08-25","s":"Mr. Simonds was a teacher in Champlain and a friend of Frances Hubbell who had a lame wrist and used a rod on his students. Alexander teased Frances about him, and Charles inquired after him — a local figure who connected the brothers to Frances's professional world."},{"id":"PER-howes","n":"Sergeant Howes","cat":"military","roles":["Reduced to ranks for drunkenness","Singing in Alex's tent"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-03-12-001","LTR-1863-08-25-001"],"first":"1863-03-12","last":"1863-08-25","s":"Sergeant Howes of Company H, 60th New York was Alexander's roommate and messmate who delivered Mother's midnight letter in Baltimore and later sang in Alexander's tent. He was reduced to the ranks for drunkenness in August 1863, his position going to Amos Luther — a cautionary fall that Alexander witnessed firsthand."},{"id":"PER-freeman-bowron","n":"Freeman Bowron","cat":"civilian","roles":["From Washington; visited","Orderly sergeant, cavalry — will reenlist; visiting from Washington"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-03-12-001","LTR-1863-04-11-001"],"first":"1863-03-12","last":"1863-04-11","s":"Freeman Bowron (also Houron/Bouron) was a cavalry orderly sergeant from the Champlain area who visited Charles from Washington and planned to re-enlist. His brother Ben traveled to Yorktown to visit another brother Lyman, illustrating how multiple members of individual families served simultaneously across different units."},{"id":"PER-henry-gregory","n":"Henry Gregory","cat":"civilian","roles":["\"Too big a heart\"","\"Too big a heart\" — Dr. Coyt said"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-03-19-001","LTR-1863-03-29-002"],"first":"1863-03-19","last":"1863-03-29","s":"Henry Gregory was a soldier diagnosed by Dr. Coyt as having \"too big a heart\" — a medical condition Charles mentioned twice in consecutive letters from Alexandria in 1863. The diagnosis likely referred to an enlarged heart, one of the many non-combat medical conditions that sidelined Civil War soldiers."},{"id":"PER-clark","n":"Mr. Clark","cat":"civilian","roles":["Caring for horse and heifer","Caring for horse/heifer at home","Home aide — cares for horse and animals","Predicted good luck — won't come true"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-03-29-001","LTR-1863-08-21-001"],"first":"1863-03-29","last":"1863-08-21","s":"Mr. Clark was a Champlain neighbor who cared for the family's horse and heifer while the brothers were away at war, a practical home-front role that Charles and Alexander tracked and managed through their letters to Mother."},{"id":"PER-abram-fry","n":"Abram Fry","cat":"civilian","roles":["Story \"very doubtful\" — dismissed","Witnessed/described Henry's grave headboard"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-04-27-001","LTR-1863-05-13-001"],"first":"1863-04-27","last":"1863-05-13","s":"Abram Fry of the New York artillery reported seeing Henry Hubbell's name on a headboard grave marker at the Antietam battlefield — the first and only report of a physical marker for Henry's burial. Charles initially relayed the report but later dismissed it as \"very doubtful,\" leaving the family without definitive proof of where Henry lay."},{"id":"PER-grandmother-mcneill","n":"Frances M. Hubbell (Mother)","cat":"family","roles":["At Charlotte","Health inquired about"],"rel":"Mother","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-07-06-001","LTR-1863-07-20-001"],"first":"1863-07-06","last":"1863-07-20","s":"Grandmother McNeill (also McNiel), the family matriarch living at Charlotte, was a figure of concern as the brothers inquired about her health and urged Mother to visit. She and Grandmother McNiel referenced elsewhere in the letters appear to be the same person, the maternal grandmother whose household provided refuge from Champlain's wartime isolation."},{"id":"PER-mary-mcneill","n":"Mary McNeill","cat":"civilian","roles":["Cousin — Charles has never met her","Letter received from her"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-08-16-001","LTR-1863-09-25-001"],"first":"1863-08-16","last":"1863-09-25","s":"Mary McNeill was a cousin from Michigan whom Charles had never met but who wrote to him during the war. Her letter from Atlas, Michigan provided a civilian perspective on draft resistance and anti-emancipation sentiment in the rural North — a mirror of the discontent Charles observed among soldiers."},{"id":"PER-brikerhoff","n":"Mrs. Brikerhoff","cat":"civilian","roles":["Regards","Will live with Mother this winter","`stated`"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-09-25-001","LTR-1864-08-08-001"],"first":"1863-09-25","last":"1864-08-08","s":"Mrs. Brikerhoff (also Brinkerhoff) was a Champlain acquaintance who planned to live with Mother during the winter, her presence offering companionship during the lonely months when the brothers were all away at war. Alexander sent her regards from near Atlanta in 1864."},{"id":"PER-greene","n":"Brevet Maj. Gen. Greene","cat":"military","roles":["Brigade commander — commandant of Murfreesboro garrison","Formerly Colonel of 60th — commands 3rd Brigade"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-10-11-001","LTR-1865-06-16-001"],"first":"1863-10-11","last":"1865-06-16","s":"Brevet Maj. Gen. George S. Greene, formerly colonel of the 60th New York, commanded the 3rd Brigade and later served as commandant of the Murfreesboro garrison. He had led the brigade's famous defense of Culp's Hill at Gettysburg — the action in which Alexander's regiment fought — and his promotion reflected distinguished service that the men of the 60th witnessed firsthand."},{"id":"PER-unnamed","n":"Col. [unnamed]","cat":"military","roles":["Trying to get reg't to Philadelphia; may choose Chattanooga","Wrote Mother — tried for discharge, \"futile\""],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-11-15-001","LTR-1863-11-20-001"],"first":"1863-11-15","last":"1863-11-20","s":"An unnamed colonel tried to get the regiment reassigned to Philadelphia and later wrote to Mother attempting to secure a discharge for one of the brothers, an effort he described as \"futile.\" His advocacy, though unsuccessful, represented the officer-level intercession that families sometimes mobilized on behalf of their soldiers."},{"id":"PER-sergt--long","n":"Sergt. Long","cat":"civilian","roles":["Co. H — wounded? (Alexander unsure)","Not hurt — confirmed"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-12-01-001","LTR-1863-12-16-001"],"first":"1863-12-01","last":"1863-12-16","s":"Sergeant Long of Company H was initially reported by Alexander as possibly wounded after a battle, but was later confirmed unhurt. His brief appearance illustrated the fog of war that surrounded casualty reports, where even men in the same company could not immediately confirm who had been hit."},{"id":"PER-kelly","n":"Corporal Kelly","cat":"military","roles":["Co. H — wounded","Tent mate"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-12-01-001","LTR-1864-02-21-001"],"first":"1863-12-01","last":"1864-02-21","s":"Corporal Kelly of Company H was Alexander's tent mate and was wounded in action, one of the steady stream of Co. H casualties that Alexander documented from Chancellorsville through the Atlanta Campaign."},{"id":"PER-goddard","n":"Col Goddard","cat":"military","roles":["Name on killed/wounded list","Now Colonel"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-12-09-001","LTR-1864-08-08-002"],"first":"1863-12-09","last":"1864-08-08","s":"Col. Goddard's name appeared on the killed-and-wounded list and he was later confirmed as the new colonel of the 60th New York after the regimental consolidation with the 134th New York in 1864. His progression through the regiment's officer ranks tracked the attrition of command that characterized the war's later years."},{"id":"PER-doten","n":"Doten","cat":"civilian","roles":["Co. H — killed","Co. H — killed at Missionary Ridge/Ringgold"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-12-13-001","LTR-1863-12-16-001"],"first":"1863-12-13","last":"1863-12-16","s":"Doten was a soldier of Company H, 60th New York, killed at Missionary Ridge or Ringgold in late 1863. Alexander listed him among the company's casualties — one of the men from the Champlain area whose death marked the 60th's costly service in the Western theater."},{"id":"PER-davis","n":"Col. Davis","cat":"military","roles":["Returned to regiment — cheered by men","Under arrest — court martial; men like him"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-01-19-001","LTR-1864-02-17-001"],"first":"1864-01-19","last":"1864-02-17","s":"Col. Davis of the 153rd New York was court-martialed for bucking and gagging soldiers, attempting to remove U.S. marks from a government horse, and false musters for bounty fraud. Despite the charges, Charles reported that the men liked him and cheered his return to the regiment — a popular commander undone by the army's disciplinary system."},{"id":"PER-lafountain","n":"Lafountain","cat":"civilian","roles":["Bought Alexander's boots for $6.50","Visiting — brother in Co. didn't re-enlist; 6th Cavalry"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-01-19-001","LTR-1864-05-06-001"],"first":"1864-01-19","last":"1864-05-06","s":"Lafountain was a Champlain acquaintance who bought Alexander's boots during the war — the price quoted at both $6.50 and $8.50 in different letters — and whose brother served in the 6th Cavalry but did not re-enlist. He visited the regiment at Capitol Hill in early 1864, part of the stream of hometown visitors who connected the soldiers to Champlain."},{"id":"PER-chas--carte","n":"Chas. Carte","cat":"civilian","roles":["Visited — attended reenlisted soldiers party at Biglows","Visited — saw Champlainers at Biglows"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-01-29-001","LTR-1864-02-15-001"],"first":"1864-01-29","last":"1864-02-15","s":"Chas. Carte was a Champlain visitor who attended a re-enlisted soldiers' party at Biglows and saw other Champlainers there. Charles teased that Carte had taken Helen Scott to the event, part of the hometown social gossip that filled letters between military dispatches."},{"id":"PER-helen-scott","n":"Helen Scott","cat":"civilian","roles":["Charles guessed Carte took her to party","Guessed by Carte — someone who took book"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-01-29-001","LTR-1864-02-15-001"],"first":"1864-01-29","last":"1864-02-15","s":"Helen Scott was a young woman from the Champlain area whom Charles guessed Chas. Carte had taken to a soldiers' party at Biglows. She appeared only in passing, part of the social world back home that the brothers tracked from a distance."},{"id":"PER-sergt--peltier","n":"Sergt. Peltier","cat":"civilian","roles":["Furlough to Kerbo","Returning from furlough — will forward letters"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-02-15-001","LTR-1864-04-15-001"],"first":"1864-02-15","last":"1864-04-15","s":"Sergeant Peltier of the 153rd New York took a furlough to Kerbo and was later returning from furlough with a promise to forward letters. He served as an informal mail courier between the regiment and home, a common role for soldiers traveling on leave."},{"id":"PER-joseph-martin","n":"Mrs. Joseph Martin","cat":"civilian","roles":["Creditor/acquaintance; Charles asked Mother to pay her $20","Frenchman from Champlain — $20 to wife"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-06-13-001","LTR-1865-06-12-001"],"first":"1864-06-13","last":"1865-06-12","s":"Mrs. Joseph Martin was a Champlain creditor to whom Charles asked Mother to pay twenty dollars owed by her husband, a Frenchman from Champlain serving in the regiment. The debt illustrated the financial obligations soldiers left behind and asked their families to honor."},{"id":"PER-martin","n":"Martin","cat":"civilian","roles":["Paid 30¢ share of freight","Soldier in regiment — sent $70 home with Charles and James"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-06-29-001","LTR-1865-07-13-001"],"first":"1864-06-29","last":"1865-07-13","s":"Martin was a soldier in the 153rd New York who sent seventy dollars home through Charles and James and paid a thirty-cent share of freight costs. His use of the Hubbell brothers as financial couriers reflected the trust networks soldiers built for transmitting money home."},{"id":"PER-hardee","n":"Gen. Hardee","cat":"military","roles":["Confederate — rebels cheered him","Confederate — was near Polk"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-07-00-001","LTR-1864-07-08-001"],"first":"1864-07-00","last":"1864-07-08","s":"Confederate Gen. William Hardee appeared in Alexander's letters from the Atlanta Campaign, where rebels cheered his name — though Alexander described the sound as \"something very like a wail — and a sorrowful wriggle\" rather than a confident cheer. Hardee's presence near Gen. Polk was noted in Alexander's tracking of the enemy command structure."},{"id":"PER-mcpherson","n":"McPherson","cat":"civilian","roles":["Army moved to right — battle","Former commander — Howard replaced him"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-08-08-001","LTR-1864-08-08-002"],"first":"1864-08-08","last":"1864-08-08","s":"Gen. James B. McPherson commanded Union forces in the Atlanta Campaign until his death on July 22, 1864, after which Gen. Howard replaced him. Alexander referenced McPherson's army in his detailed Peachtree Creek account to Capt. Ransom, noting the rightward flanking movement that McPherson and Schofield's forces executed."},{"id":"PER-pelkier","n":"Sgt. Pelkier (Pelky)","cat":"military","roles":["From Corbeau — shot through leg","Shot in leg — carried to barn, left for rebs, still there when they came back"],"rel":"","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-10-22-001","LTR-1864-11-04-001"],"first":"1864-10-22","last":"1864-11-04","s":"Sergeant Pelkier (Pelky) from Corbeau in Clinton County was shot through the leg at Cedar Creek in October 1864, carried to a barn, left behind when the rebels advanced, and found still there when Union forces recaptured the position. His ordeal — wounded, abandoned, recovered — compressed the chaos of the battle into a single soldier's experience."},{"id":"PER-alex","n":"Alexander F. Hubbell","cat":"family","roles":["Home on furlough — not entitled to vote","Not written yet — will wait till he rejoins regt."],"rel":"Son","lc":2,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-11-30-001","LTR-1864-12-27-001"],"first":"1864-11-30","last":"1864-12-27","s":"Alexander F. Hubbell appeared as a subject rather than author in two letters: Mother noted he was home on furlough but not entitled to vote, and Charles reported he had not yet written because he was waiting to rejoin his regiment. These glimpses from others' perspectives captured the veteran between campaigns — restless and eager to return to service."},{"id":"PER-capt","n":"The Capt.","cat":"military","roles":["Company commander (likely Capt. Reich)"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-07-21-001"],"first":"1861-07-21","last":"1861-07-21","s":"The Captain, likely Capt. Reich, commanded Henry Hubbell's company in the 34th New York and was mentioned in Henry's July 1861 letter from Washington on the day of the First Battle of Bull Run."},{"id":"PER-ex-president-van-buren","n":"Ex President Van Buren","cat":"civilian","roles":["Martin Van Buren, 8th President"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-08-06-001"],"first":"1861-08-06","last":"1861-08-06","s":"Martin Van Buren, the eighth President of the United States, was referenced by James Hubbell at West Point in connection with official mourning observances — cannon salutes, flags at half-mast, and crape worn for six months — following Van Buren's death in 1862."},{"id":"PER-brinckerhoff","n":"Mrs. Brinckerhoff","cat":"civilian","roles":["Family acquaintance from Champlain"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-08-06-001"],"first":"1861-08-06","last":"1861-08-06","s":"Mrs. Brinckerhoff was a family acquaintance from Champlain mentioned by James in his letter from West Point, providing a thread of home-community connection during his cadet years."},{"id":"PER-the-capt","n":"\"the Capt.\"","cat":"military","roles":["Company captain"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-09-05-001"],"first":"1861-09-05","last":"1861-09-05","s":"The unnamed company captain of the 34th New York was mentioned by Henry in September 1861 alongside the regimental doctor in a letter describing camp illness and an accidental shooting death in the regiment."},{"id":"PER-the-dr","n":"\"the Dr.\"","cat":"military","roles":["Regimental doctor"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-09-05-001"],"first":"1861-09-05","last":"1861-09-05","s":"The unnamed regimental doctor of the 34th New York treated soldiers at the Washington camp and was referenced by Henry in September 1861 as he dealt with fever and ague symptoms that kept him from drilling."},{"id":"PER-unnamed-co--h-private","n":"Unnamed Co. H private","cat":"military","roles":["Killed in accident"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-09-05-001"],"first":"1861-09-05","last":"1861-09-05","s":"An unnamed private of Company H, 34th New York, was killed in an accidental shooting in camp near Washington in September 1861 — the third such death in the regiment, underscoring the danger of untrained soldiers handling weapons."},{"id":"PER-a-half-tipsy-fellow","n":"\"a half tipsy fellow\"","cat":"civilian","roles":["Fellow recruit on train"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-09-20-001"],"first":"1861-09-20","last":"1861-09-20","s":"A half-drunk recruit on Alexander Hubbell's train to the barracks at Ogdensburg demanded music from the other soldiers, a colorful detail from Alexander's first letter describing his arrival at military service in September 1861."},{"id":"PER-edgar-graves","n":"Edgar Graves","cat":"civilian","roles":["Fellow soldier, from Chazy Village"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-09-21-001"],"first":"1861-09-21","last":"1861-09-21","s":"Edgar Graves was a fellow soldier from Chazy Village, Clinton County, mentioned by Henry in September 1861 as a local connection from the 34th New York's recruiting area near the Hubbell home in Champlain."},{"id":"PER-loring-e--white","n":"Loring E. White","cat":"civilian","roles":["First Lieutenant, elected, \"pious young man\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-09-23-001"],"first":"1861-09-23","last":"1861-09-23","s":"Loring E. White was elected First Lieutenant of Alexander Hubbell's company at Camp Wheeler, Ogdensburg, in September 1861 and was described approvingly by Alexander as \"a pious young man.\""},{"id":"PER-the-surgeon","n":"\"The surgeon\"","cat":"civilian","roles":["Regimental surgeon"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-09-23-001"],"first":"1861-09-23","last":"1861-09-23","s":"The unnamed regimental surgeon examined troops at Camp Wheeler in Ogdensburg during September 1861, part of the daily routine Alexander Hubbell described in his detailed account of early camp life."},{"id":"PER-wilson","n":"Wilson","cat":"civilian","roles":["Fellow soldier — Alexander wrote a letter for him"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-09-26-001"],"first":"1861-09-26","last":"1861-09-26","s":"Wilson was a fellow soldier at Camp Wheeler for whom Alexander Hubbell wrote a letter home, a small but revealing detail about the literacy gap among enlisted men in the early months of the war."},{"id":"PER-h--cooper","n":"H. Cooper","cat":"civilian","roles":["Fellow soldier, on picket"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-09-27-001"],"first":"1861-09-27","last":"1861-09-27","s":"H. Cooper was a fellow soldier in the 34th New York who served on picket duty with Henry Hubbell near Washington in September 1861."},{"id":"PER-h","n":"Sergeant H.","cat":"military","roles":["Sergeant, on picket"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-09-27-001"],"first":"1861-09-27","last":"1861-09-27","s":"Sergeant H. served on picket duty alongside Henry Hubbell and Lt. Scott near Washington in September 1861, part of the 34th New York's guard detail along the Potomac."},{"id":"PER-the-col","n":"\"The Col.\"","cat":"military","roles":["Colonel of 34th NY"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-09-27-001"],"first":"1861-09-27","last":"1861-09-27","s":"The Colonel of the 34th New York was referenced by Henry in September 1861 in connection with enforcing the regiment's card-playing ban and maintaining discipline in camp near Washington."},{"id":"PER-second-officer-of-the-society","n":"\"Second officer of the Society\"","cat":"civilian","roles":["Wrote on first page of letter; Alexander distrusts"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-09-27-002"],"first":"1861-09-27","last":"1861-09-27","s":"The second officer of the Brother Craftsmen mutual aid society wrote on the first page of Alexander Hubbell's letter in September 1861, but Alexander distrusted him and sought his mother's counsel on whether to join the organization."},{"id":"PER-rich-reich","n":"Rich/Reich","cat":"civilian","roles":["Soldier/courier — not yet returned"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-10-17-001"],"first":"1861-10-17","last":"1861-10-17","s":"Rich (or Reich) was a soldier or courier from the 34th New York who had not yet returned to camp when Henry wrote home in October 1861, likely the same family as Capt. Reich of Henry's company."},{"id":"PER-brigadier-general-gormon","n":"Brigadier General Gormon","cat":"military","roles":["Brigade commander, inspected regiment"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-10-17-001"],"first":"1861-10-17","last":"1861-10-17","s":"Brigadier General Gorman inspected the 34th New York near Washington in October 1861, during a period when the regiment earned the newspaper nickname \"sharp shooters\" for their marksmanship."},{"id":"PER-escaped-prisoner","n":"Escaped prisoner (unnamed)","cat":"civilian","roles":["Former POW, returned to regiment"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-10-17-001"],"first":"1861-10-17","last":"1861-10-17","s":"An unnamed Union prisoner of war escaped Confederate captivity by feigning illness, accumulating opium doses, and drugging his guard's coffee — one of the most dramatic narratives Henry relayed in his October 1861 letter."},{"id":"PER-f-and-c","n":"F and C","cat":"civilian","roles":["Family members at Rouses Point — likely Frances and Charles"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-11-13-001"],"first":"1861-11-13","last":"1861-11-13","s":"\"F and C\" — almost certainly Frances and Charles Hubbell — met Alexander at Rouses Point during his regiment's transit south from Ogdensburg to Baltimore in November 1861."},{"id":"PER-the-quartermaster","n":"\"The Quartermaster\"","cat":"civilian","roles":["Blamed for hunger at Kalarama Heights"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-11-13-001"],"first":"1861-11-13","last":"1861-11-13","s":"The unnamed regimental quartermaster of the 60th New York was blamed by Alexander Hubbell for the soldiers' hunger during their miserable stopover at Kalorama Heights (\"Starvation Heights\") in Washington, November 1861."},{"id":"PER-a-black-cook","n":"\"A black cook\"","cat":"civilian","roles":["Hired for company"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-11-13-001"],"first":"1861-11-13","last":"1861-11-13","s":"An unnamed Black cook was hired for Alexander Hubbell's company at Camp Jackson near Baltimore in November 1861, a passing detail that reflected the employment of African Americans in support roles within Union regiments."},{"id":"PER-the-adjutant","n":"\"The Adjutant\"","cat":"civilian","roles":["Regimental adjutant, carrying money to Washington"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-11-15-001"],"first":"1861-11-15","last":"1861-11-15","s":"The regimental adjutant of the 34th New York carried money to Washington in November 1861, serving as part of the financial pipeline through which Henry Hubbell sent pay home to his family in Champlain."},{"id":"PER-b--moore","n":"B. Moore","cat":"civilian","roles":["Express recipient at Champlain"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-11-15-001"],"first":"1861-11-15","last":"1861-11-15","s":"B. Moore was a Champlain resident who served as a recipient for express packages and money transfers sent home by Henry Hubbell from Camp McClellan in November 1861."},{"id":"PER-g--dunning","n":"G. Dunning","cat":"civilian","roles":["Express recipient at Champlain"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-11-15-001"],"first":"1861-11-15","last":"1861-11-15","s":"G. Dunning was a Champlain resident who, like B. Moore, received express deliveries on behalf of the Hubbell family, facilitating the transfer of Henry's military pay back home."},{"id":"PER-confederate-deserter","n":"Confederate deserter (unnamed)","cat":"civilian","roles":["Drunk, armed, crossed river in small boat"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-11-30-001"],"first":"1861-11-30","last":"1861-11-30","s":"An unnamed Confederate deserter crossed the Potomac River in a small boat while drunk and armed, an incident Henry Hubbell reported from picket duty in November 1861."},{"id":"PER-david-dava---way","n":"David Dava[?]way","cat":"civilian","roles":["Picket — accidental shooting, thumb lost"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-12-08-001"],"first":"1861-12-08","last":"1861-12-08","s":"David Dava[?]way was a soldier on picket who lost his thumb in an accidental shooting, one of several firearms mishaps Alexander Hubbell documented from camp near Baltimore in December 1861."},{"id":"PER-george-dunning","n":"George Dunning","cat":"civilian","roles":["Champlain — receives money transfers"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-12-08-001"],"first":"1861-12-08","last":"1861-12-08","s":"George Dunning of Champlain handled money transfers for the Hubbell family, receiving funds that Alexander sent home from his post near Baltimore in December 1861."},{"id":"PER-the-commissary","n":"\"The Commissary\"","cat":"civilian","roles":["Tents with Alexander"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-12-08-002"],"first":"1861-12-08","last":"1861-12-08","s":"The unnamed commissary officer shared a tent with Alexander Hubbell near Baltimore in December 1861, one of Alexander's closer associations in the 60th New York."},{"id":"PER-sergeant--company-c","n":"Sergeant, Company C","cat":"military","roles":["Took Alexander's knapsack by mistake"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-12-08-002"],"first":"1861-12-08","last":"1861-12-08","s":"An unnamed sergeant from Company C mistakenly took Alexander Hubbell's knapsack in December 1861, a minor camp mishap that reflected the confusion of shared quarters and identical military equipment."},{"id":"PER-fireman","n":"The fireman (unnamed)","cat":"civilian","roles":["On engine — burned, died 1-2 days later"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-12-16-001"],"first":"1861-12-16","last":"1861-12-16","s":"An unnamed fireman aboard a railroad engine near Alexander's post was badly burned in a train wreck in December 1861 and died one to two days later."},{"id":"PER-engineer","n":"The engineer (unnamed)","cat":"civilian","roles":["On engine — thrown 200-300 ft, killed"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-12-16-001"],"first":"1861-12-16","last":"1861-12-16","s":"An unnamed railroad engineer was thrown two to three hundred feet and killed in a train wreck near Alexander Hubbell's post in December 1861, one of the non-combat dangers of guarding the Baltimore & Ohio Railroad."},{"id":"PER-haine","n":"Haine (?)","cat":"civilian","roles":["Soldier — body fell near Ransom"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-12-16-001"],"first":"1861-12-16","last":"1861-12-16","s":"Haine was a soldier whose body fell near Captain Ransom during the December 1861 railroad disaster that Alexander Hubbell described in detail from his post along the B&O line."},{"id":"PER-amos","n":"Amos","cat":"civilian","roles":["Person contacted for Chaney names"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-12-16-001"],"first":"1861-12-16","last":"1861-12-16","s":"Amos was a person contacted for the names of individuals from Chaney, mentioned briefly by Alexander in December 1861 as the family tracked acquaintances across the war."},{"id":"PER-e--graves","n":"E. Graves","cat":"civilian","roles":["Tentmate, from Chazy"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-12-19-001"],"first":"1861-12-19","last":"1861-12-19","s":"E. Graves of Chazy was one of Henry Hubbell's tentmates in the 34th New York during the winter of 1861, part of the Clinton County contingent that made up much of Company D."},{"id":"PER-john-green","n":"John Green","cat":"civilian","roles":["Tentmate"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-12-19-001"],"first":"1861-12-19","last":"1861-12-19","s":"John Green was one of Henry Hubbell's tentmates in the 34th New York in December 1861, sharing close quarters during the regiment's winter encampment."},{"id":"PER-a--perkins","n":"A. Perkins","cat":"civilian","roles":["Tentmate"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-12-19-001"],"first":"1861-12-19","last":"1861-12-19","s":"A. Perkins was one of Henry Hubbell's tentmates in the 34th New York during the December 1861 winter encampment near the Potomac."},{"id":"PER-l","n":"Col. L---","cat":"military","roles":["Colonel, resigned — thought a coward, good voice"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-12-19-001"],"first":"1861-12-19","last":"1861-12-19","s":"Colonel L--- of the 34th New York resigned his commission in late 1861; Henry described him as thought a coward by the men, though he acknowledged the colonel had a good voice."},{"id":"PER-the-old-sutler","n":"\"The old sutler\"","cat":"civilian","roles":["Drummed out for selling liquor"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-12-19-001"],"first":"1861-12-19","last":"1861-12-19","s":"The old sutler attached to the 34th New York was drummed out of camp in December 1861 for selling liquor to the soldiers, a common disciplinary action in Union regiments."},{"id":"PER-b--miner","n":"B. Miner","cat":"civilian","roles":["Carrying letters to Champlain"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-12-20-001"],"first":"1861-12-20","last":"1861-12-20","s":"B. Miner carried letters from Henry Hubbell's camp back to Champlain in December 1861, serving as an informal courier between the 34th New York and the home community."},{"id":"PER-bill-robers","n":"Bill Robers","cat":"civilian","roles":["Offered accordion lessons"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-12-20-001"],"first":"1861-12-20","last":"1861-12-20","s":"Bill Robers offered Henry Hubbell accordion lessons in camp in December 1861, a glimpse of the musical pastimes that helped soldiers endure the monotony of winter quarters."},{"id":"PER-preston-king","n":"Preston King","cat":"civilian","roles":["U.S. Senator from NY — in Baltimore, lobbying to send regiment north"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-12-23-001"],"first":"1861-12-23","last":"1861-12-23","s":"Preston King, the U.S. Senator from New York, was spotted by Alexander in Baltimore in December 1861, reportedly lobbying to have Alexander's regiment sent north closer to home."},{"id":"PER-mason-and-slidell","n":"Mason and Slidell","cat":"civilian","roles":["Confederate diplomats — reported released (Trent Affair)"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-12-23-001"],"first":"1861-12-23","last":"1861-12-23","s":"James Mason and John Slidell were the Confederate diplomats at the center of the Trent Affair, whose reported release Alexander noted in his December 1861 letter as a significant diplomatic development."},{"id":"PER-inspector-general","n":"The Inspector General","cat":"military","roles":["To inspect regiment at muster for pay"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1861-12-29-001"],"first":"1861-12-29","last":"1861-12-29","s":"The Inspector General was expected to inspect Alexander Hubbell's regiment at muster for pay in late December 1861, a routine but eagerly anticipated event as soldiers waited for their wages."},{"id":"PER-my-minister","n":"\"My Minister\"","cat":"civilian","roles":["Alexander's minister back home"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-01-01-001"],"first":"1862-01-01","last":"1862-01-01","s":"Alexander Hubbell's unnamed minister back home in Champlain was a moral touchstone referenced in his January 1862 letter, reflecting the deep religious faith that sustained him through camp life."},{"id":"PER-reich-s-brother","n":"Capt. Reich's brother","cat":"military","roles":["Sutler for the regiment"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-01-09-001"],"first":"1862-01-09","last":"1862-01-09","s":"Captain Reich's brother served as sutler for the 34th New York, a family business connection Henry Hubbell noted in January 1862 that was typical of the commercial networks surrounding Civil War regiments."},{"id":"PER-myers","n":"Corporal Myers","cat":"military","roles":["Sick — Alexander ordered to replace him"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-01-15-001"],"first":"1862-01-15","last":"1862-01-15","s":"Corporal Myers of the 60th New York fell sick in January 1862, and Alexander Hubbell was ordered to take over his duties, marking one of Alexander's early steps toward increased responsibility."},{"id":"PER-porter","n":"Porter (Edwin Porter)","cat":"civilian","roles":["Killed by train on railroad track — large man"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-01-17-001"],"first":"1862-01-17","last":"1862-01-17","s":"Edwin Porter, described as a large man, was killed by a train on the railroad tracks near the 60th New York's post in January 1862 — a grim reminder of the dangers of guarding the B&O Railroad."},{"id":"PER-finch","n":"Sergeant Finch","cat":"military","roles":["Reported Porter's death; roared from Lieutenant's door"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-01-17-001"],"first":"1862-01-17","last":"1862-01-17","s":"Sergeant Finch reported Edwin Porter's death to Alexander Hubbell's company in January 1862, delivering the news with a roar from the Lieutenant's doorway."},{"id":"PER-and-mrs--dobbin","n":"Mr. and Mrs. Dobbin","cat":"civilian","roles":["Champlain acquaintances — \"hair pulls,\" orange peel argument"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-01-17-001"],"first":"1862-01-17","last":"1862-01-17","s":"Mr. and Mrs. Dobbin were Champlain acquaintances whom Alexander recalled in January 1862 with humor, referencing their \"hair pulls\" and an argument over orange peel."},{"id":"PER-brink-miner","n":"Brink Miner","cat":"civilian","roles":["Criticized by Alexander — enlisted for \"novelty,\" not patriotism"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-01-17-001"],"first":"1862-01-17","last":"1862-01-17","s":"Brink Miner was criticized by Alexander Hubbell in January 1862 as a man who had enlisted merely for \"novelty\" rather than out of patriotic conviction, reflecting Alexander's strong moral standards for military service."},{"id":"PER-messrs--hoyle-and-finley","n":"Messrs. Hoyle and Finley","cat":"civilian","roles":["Visitors from home — left on \"seven train\" without seeing Alexander"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-01-24-001"],"first":"1862-01-24","last":"1862-01-24","s":"Messrs. Hoyle and Finley visited from home in January 1862 but left on the \"seven train\" without seeing Alexander, a frustrating near-miss that underscored the difficulty of maintaining personal connections during wartime."},{"id":"PER-mcdonel","n":"McDonel","cat":"civilian","roles":["New Corporal in company"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-01-24-002"],"first":"1862-01-24","last":"1862-01-24","s":"McDonel was a newly appointed corporal in Henry Hubbell's company of the 34th New York, mentioned in January 1862 as part of the ongoing leadership changes within the regiment."},{"id":"PER-edwin-porter","n":"Edwin Porter","cat":"civilian","roles":["Killed by cars — \"one of our men\" (confirms enlisted, not officer)"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-01-25-001"],"first":"1862-01-25","last":"1862-01-25","s":"Edwin Porter was confirmed as an enlisted man of the 60th New York who was killed by railroad cars in January 1862, with Alexander noting he was \"one of our men\" rather than an officer."},{"id":"PER-trudell","n":"Trudell","cat":"civilian","roles":["Confrontation over beat; now in hospital"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-01-28-001"],"first":"1862-02-04","last":"1862-02-04","s":"Trudell was a soldier in the 60th New York who had a confrontation with Alexander Hubbell over a beat in February 1862 and subsequently ended up in the hospital."},{"id":"PER-willis-savage","n":"Willis Savage","cat":"civilian","roles":["Wrote to Alexander; letter received"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-01-28-001"],"first":"1862-02-04","last":"1862-02-04","s":"Willis Savage wrote to Alexander Hubbell in February 1862, one of several correspondents from the Champlain community who maintained contact with the Hubbell brothers during their service."},{"id":"PER-upton","n":"Mrs. Upton","cat":"civilian","roles":["Champlain gossip — exaggerates sickness"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-01-28-001"],"first":"1862-02-04","last":"1862-02-04","s":"Mrs. Upton of Champlain was singled out by Alexander in February 1862 as a gossip who exaggerated reports of sickness among the soldiers, spreading unnecessary alarm on the home front."},{"id":"PER-aunt-laurie-cousin-nellie","n":"Aunt Laurie/Cousin Nellie","cat":"civilian","roles":["Letter received"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-01-28-001"],"first":"1862-02-04","last":"1862-02-04","s":"Aunt Laurie and Cousin Nellie were extended family members who wrote to Alexander Hubbell in February 1862, maintaining the wide network of family correspondence that sustained the brothers through the war."},{"id":"PER-graves","n":"Graves","cat":"civilian","roles":["Tent-mate — in picture with Henry"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-04-001"],"first":"1862-02-04","last":"1862-02-04","s":"Graves was Henry Hubbell's tentmate in the 34th New York who appeared alongside Henry in a photograph, likely the same E. Graves from Chazy mentioned in earlier letters."},{"id":"PER-unidentified-poolesville-sender","n":"Unidentified Poolesville sender","cat":"civilian","roles":["Correspondent, sent Valentine"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-07-001"],"first":"1862-02-07","last":"1862-02-07","s":"An unidentified person in Poolesville sent Alexander Hubbell a valentine in February 1862, a small social gesture that hinted at connections the brothers formed near their Maryland postings."},{"id":"PER-little-doolittle","n":"Little Doolittle","cat":"civilian","roles":["Academy peer/bully, insulted James"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-11-001"],"first":"1862-02-11","last":"1862-02-11","s":"Little Doolittle was an academy peer who bullied and insulted James Hubbell, prompting Alexander to write about the incident with protective indignation in February 1862."},{"id":"PER-feminine-household","n":"Feminine household (unspecified)","cat":"civilian","roles":["Alexander's domestic contrast"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-11-001"],"first":"1862-02-11","last":"1862-02-11","s":"The unspecified feminine household was referenced by Alexander in February 1862 as a domestic contrast to his rough camp life, reflecting his longing for the civility of home."},{"id":"PER-officer-of-the-day","n":"Officer of the day","cat":"civilian","roles":["unnamed; enforces guard duty"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-11-002"],"first":"1862-02-11","last":"1862-02-11","s":"The unnamed officer of the day enforced guard duty at the 34th New York's camp in February 1862, part of the routine military authority Henry Hubbell described in his letters."},{"id":"PER-mexican-war-india-veteran","n":"Mexican War/India veteran","cat":"civilian","roles":["Member of Henry's Co., knows military law"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-11-002"],"first":"1862-02-11","last":"1862-02-11","s":"A veteran of the Mexican War and service in India served in Henry Hubbell's company in the 34th New York and was valued for his knowledge of military law, which he shared with his fellow soldiers in February 1862."},{"id":"PER-unidentified-drunk-soldier","n":"Unidentified drunk soldier","cat":"civilian","roles":["Co. D member, disciplined"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-11-002"],"first":"1862-02-11","last":"1862-02-11","s":"An unidentified drunk soldier from Company D of the 34th New York was disciplined in February 1862, one of several incidents of alcohol-related misconduct Henry documented in camp."},{"id":"PER-captain","n":"Captain (unnamed)","cat":"military","roles":["Company/regimental officer, approves of James's West Point appointment"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-12-001"],"first":"1862-02-12","last":"1862-02-12","s":"An unnamed company or regimental captain in the 34th New York expressed approval of James Hubbell's appointment to West Point in February 1862, reflecting the pride the regiment took in the Hubbell family's service."},{"id":"PER-burnside","n":"Gen. Burnside","cat":"military","roles":["Commander of Burnside Expedition"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-12-001"],"first":"1862-02-12","last":"1862-02-12","s":"General Ambrose Burnside was mentioned by Henry Hubbell in February 1862 in connection with the Burnside Expedition, one of the major Union operations that dominated war news that winter."},{"id":"PER-commissary-howes","n":"Commissary Howes","cat":"civilian","roles":["Roommate, messmate, correspondent"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-17-001"],"first":"1862-02-17","last":"1862-02-17","s":"Commissary Howes was Alexander Hubbell's roommate, messmate, and regular correspondent in the 60th New York, one of the closer friendships Alexander formed during his service near Baltimore in early 1862."},{"id":"PER-father","n":"Father (deceased)","cat":"civilian","roles":["Referenced for \"Father's psalm\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-17-001"],"first":"1862-02-17","last":"1862-02-17","s":"The Hubbell brothers' deceased father was invoked by Alexander in February 1862 through the reference to \"Father's psalm,\" a family devotional tradition that connected the brothers to their father's memory and faith."},{"id":"PER-charlotte","n":"Charlotte","cat":"civilian","roles":["New correspondent (identity unclear)"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-17-001"],"first":"1862-02-17","last":"1862-02-17","s":"Charlotte was a new correspondent whose identity remains unclear, mentioned by Alexander in February 1862 as someone who had begun writing to him."},{"id":"PER-robinson","n":"General Robinson","cat":"military","roles":["Brigade commander"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-17-001"],"first":"1862-02-17","last":"1862-02-17","s":"General Robinson commanded the brigade that included the 60th New York and was mentioned by Alexander Hubbell in February 1862 as part of the regiment's chain of command near Baltimore."},{"id":"PER-unidentified-poisoned-soldier","n":"Unidentified poisoned soldier","cat":"civilian","roles":["Co. H, 60th NY member, victim of attempted assassination"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-17-001"],"first":"1862-02-17","last":"1862-02-17","s":"An unidentified soldier from Company H of the 60th New York was poisoned while on picket duty in February 1862, the victim of an attempted assassination by a Confederate sympathizer."},{"id":"PER-unidentified-confederate-woman","n":"Unidentified Confederate woman","cat":"civilian","roles":["Attempted assassin, poisoned soldier on picket"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-17-001"],"first":"1862-02-17","last":"1862-02-17","s":"An unidentified woman with Confederate sympathies attempted to poison a Union soldier on picket duty near Baltimore in February 1862, an incident Alexander Hubbell reported as evidence of the danger posed by local civilians."},{"id":"PER-perkins","n":"Corporal Perkins","cat":"military","roles":["Co. D (34th NY), Henry's messmate; correspondent"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-23-001"],"first":"1862-02-23","last":"1862-02-23","s":"Corporal Perkins of Company D, 34th New York, was Henry Hubbell's messmate and a correspondent who Alexander also mentioned in February 1862, linking the brothers' separate regiments through shared acquaintances."},{"id":"PER-pastor-preacher","n":"Pastor/Preacher (unnamed)","cat":"civilian","roles":["Sharp St. M.E. Church; gave discourse on death"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-23-001"],"first":"1862-02-23","last":"1862-02-23","s":"An unnamed pastor at Sharp Street Methodist Episcopal Church in Baltimore gave a discourse on death that Alexander Hubbell attended in February 1862, reflecting his persistent search for spiritual community during the war."},{"id":"PER-nelhinch","n":"Nelhinch","cat":"civilian","roles":["Wishes to write"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-23-001"],"first":"1862-02-23","last":"1862-02-23","s":"Nelhinch was a person who expressed a wish to write to Alexander Hubbell in February 1862, part of the extended network of home-front correspondents who kept in touch with the soldiers."},{"id":"PER-feris","n":"Feris","cat":"civilian","roles":["Broke from office after fight; left for fleet with Capt. permission"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-02-24-001"],"first":"1862-02-24","last":"1862-02-24","s":"Feris broke from his office after a fight and left for the fleet with the Captain's permission, an incident Henry reported from the 34th New York's camp in February 1862."},{"id":"PER-mix","n":"Miss Mix","cat":"civilian","roles":["Alexander hasn't forgotten \"that of her\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-03-03-001"],"first":"1862-03-03","last":"1862-03-03","s":"Miss Mix was a woman Alexander Hubbell mentioned in March 1862 with the cryptic note that he had not forgotten \"that of her,\" suggesting a personal connection back in Champlain."},{"id":"PER-fitch","n":"Lt. Fitch","cat":"military","roles":["Nearly drowned duck hunting with Luther; boat lost"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-03-03-001"],"first":"1862-03-03","last":"1862-03-03","s":"Lieutenant Fitch nearly drowned during a duck-hunting excursion with Luther when their boat was lost, an off-duty misadventure Alexander reported from the 60th New York in March 1862."},{"id":"PER-everest","n":"Everest","cat":"civilian","roles":["James's tutor/teacher for appointment preparation"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-03-06-001"],"first":"1862-03-06","last":"1862-03-06","s":"Everest served as James Hubbell's tutor, preparing him for the rigorous entrance examinations required for his appointment to West Point, as Alexander noted in March 1862."},{"id":"PER-beaumont","n":"Beaumont","cat":"civilian","roles":["Young man headed to army — warned about reality"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-03-06-001"],"first":"1862-03-06","last":"1862-03-06","s":"Beaumont was a young man from the Champlain area headed to the army whom Alexander warned about the harsh realities of military life in March 1862, drawing on his own experience."},{"id":"PER-bowen","n":"Bowen","cat":"civilian","roles":["Young man headed to army — warned about reality"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-03-06-001"],"first":"1862-03-06","last":"1862-03-06","s":"Bowen was another young man from the home community headed to enlist whom Alexander cautioned in March 1862, urging a realistic understanding of what army service entailed."},{"id":"PER-t--hoyle","n":"Mr. T. Hoyle","cat":"civilian","roles":["Seriously ill — \"good friend to us\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-03-11-001"],"first":"1862-03-11","last":"1862-03-11","s":"Mr. T. Hoyle was seriously ill in March 1862 and described by Alexander Hubbell as \"a good friend to us,\" a valued member of the Champlain community whose potential loss worried the family."},{"id":"PER-price","n":"Price","cat":"civilian","roles":["Confederate general — defeated"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-03-11-001"],"first":"1862-03-11","last":"1862-03-11","s":"Confederate General Sterling Price was mentioned by Alexander in March 1862 as having been defeated, part of the war news from the Western Theater that soldiers eagerly tracked."},{"id":"PER-mcculloch","n":"McCulloch","cat":"civilian","roles":["Confederate general — defeated with Price"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-03-11-001"],"first":"1862-03-11","last":"1862-03-11","s":"Confederate General Ben McCulloch was reported defeated alongside General Price in March 1862, news that Alexander Hubbell relayed as evidence of Union progress in the West."},{"id":"PER-excelsior-brigade-sergeant","n":"Excelsior Brigade Sergeant","cat":"military","roles":["Sickles's brigade; swindled Alexander of $5"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-03-17-001"],"first":"1862-03-17","last":"1862-03-17","s":"A sergeant from General Sickles's Excelsior Brigade swindled Alexander Hubbell out of five dollars in March 1862, one of several encounters that tested Alexander's trust during his service near Baltimore."},{"id":"PER-belgor","n":"Major Belgor","cat":"military","roles":["In charge of troop transportation, Baltimore"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-03-17-001"],"first":"1862-03-17","last":"1862-03-17","s":"Major Belgor was in charge of troop transportation at Baltimore in March 1862, an administrative officer Alexander encountered during the movement of soldiers and supplies along the B&O corridor."},{"id":"PER-joe-laforrd","n":"Joe Laforrd","cat":"civilian","roles":["Plowed Hubbell buckwheat field for Lumis; in McComb Regt."],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-03-17-001"],"first":"1862-03-17","last":"1862-03-17","s":"Joe Laforrd plowed the Hubbell family's buckwheat field for Lumis and served in the McComb Regiment, connecting the home-front agricultural labor with military service as Alexander noted in March 1862."},{"id":"PER-hiram-pettice","n":"Hiram Pettice","cat":"civilian","roles":["Wanted to borrow $3,000 from Hiram Dudley; in McComb Regt."],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-03-17-001"],"first":"1862-03-17","last":"1862-03-17","s":"Hiram Pettice, a soldier in the McComb Regiment, sought to borrow three thousand dollars from Hiram Dudley back home — a substantial sum that Alexander reported with evident interest in March 1862."},{"id":"PER-hiram-dudley","n":"Hiram Dudley","cat":"civilian","roles":["From home — Pettice wanted to borrow from him"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-03-17-001"],"first":"1862-03-17","last":"1862-03-17","s":"Hiram Dudley was a Champlain-area resident from whom Hiram Pettice attempted to borrow a large sum of money, as Alexander relayed in his March 1862 letter about acquaintances in the McComb Regiment."},{"id":"PER-james-sweney","n":"James Sweney","cat":"civilian","roles":["Captain in McComb Regt.; Luther saw him"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-03-17-001"],"first":"1862-03-17","last":"1862-03-17","s":"James Sweney served as a captain in the McComb Regiment and was seen by Luther, a connection Alexander Hubbell noted in March 1862 as he tracked acquaintances across different units."},{"id":"PER-douglas","n":"Mrs. Douglas","cat":"civilian","roles":["Henry owes for bag of flour — asks James to pay"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-03-21-001"],"first":"1862-03-21","last":"1862-03-21","s":"Mrs. Douglas was owed money by Henry Hubbell for a bag of flour, a debt he asked James to settle on his behalf in his March 1862 letter from camp — a small domestic obligation managed across the distance of war."},{"id":"PER-jacen","n":"Jacen","cat":"civilian","roles":["Sick, likely discharged"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-03-23-001"],"first":"1862-03-23","last":"1862-03-23","s":"Jacen was a soldier in the 34th New York who fell sick and was likely discharged, as Henry reported in March 1862 amid the persistent illness that plagued Civil War camps."},{"id":"PER-grant","n":"Gen. Grant","cat":"military","roles":["Union commander; opportunity to prove himself"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-04-01-001"],"first":"1862-04-01","last":"1862-04-01","s":"General Ulysses S. Grant was mentioned by Alexander Hubbell in April 1862 as a Union commander with the opportunity to prove himself, reflecting the early phase of Grant's rising reputation."},{"id":"PER-toombs","n":"Toombs","cat":"civilian","roles":["Confederate politician — \"every woman and child apply the torch\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-04-01-001"],"first":"1862-04-01","last":"1862-04-01","s":"Robert Toombs, the Confederate politician, was quoted by Alexander in April 1862 as urging that \"every woman and child apply the torch\" — rhetoric Alexander cited as evidence of the enemy's extremism."},{"id":"PER-uncle-knapp","n":"Uncle Knapp","cat":"civilian","roles":["Southern connection — Alexander wants to see him \"leg it\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-04-01-001"],"first":"1862-04-01","last":"1862-04-01","s":"Uncle Knapp was a Hubbell family connection with Southern ties whom Alexander mentioned in April 1862, expressing a wish to see him \"leg it\" — a pointed comment on relatives who sided with the Confederacy."},{"id":"PER-octove---julio","n":"Octove / Julio","cat":"civilian","roles":["Southern relatives — Alexander would \"draw sight\" on them"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-04-01-001"],"first":"1862-04-01","last":"1862-04-01","s":"Octave and Julio were Southern relatives of the Hubbell family on whom Alexander declared in April 1862 he would \"draw sight\" if he encountered them, revealing the bitter family divisions created by the war."},{"id":"PER-silas-and-sid","n":"Silas and Sid","cat":"civilian","roles":["Sold 200 acres for $800 + Rogers farm remnant"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-04-01-001"],"first":"1862-04-01","last":"1862-04-01","s":"Silas and Sid sold two hundred acres for eight hundred dollars plus a remnant of the Rogers farm, a land transaction Alexander discussed in April 1862 that shed light on the economic landscape around the Hubbell homestead."},{"id":"PER-fry-clark-s-daughter","n":"Fry Clark's daughter","cat":"civilian","roles":["Wrote fellow in Alexander's company about land sale"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-04-01-001"],"first":"1862-04-01","last":"1862-04-01","s":"Fry Clark's daughter wrote to a fellow soldier in Alexander's company about a local land sale, relaying Champlain community business to the front as Alexander noted in April 1862."},{"id":"PER-aunt","n":"Aunt (unnamed)","cat":"civilian","roles":["Recipient — likely Aunt Laura"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-04-09-001"],"first":"1862-04-09","last":"1862-04-09","s":"An unnamed aunt, likely Aunt Laura, received a letter from Henry Hubbell in April 1862, part of the extended family correspondence network that kept the Hubbells connected across multiple households."},{"id":"PER-white","n":"Lieut. White","cat":"military","roles":["Said they'd have chance to see rebels"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-04-11-001"],"first":"1862-04-11","last":"1862-04-11","s":"Lieutenant White of the 60th New York told the men they would have a chance to see rebels, a prediction Alexander Hubbell recorded in April 1862 as the regiment prepared for potential action."},{"id":"PER-augustus-hammel","n":"Augustus Hammel","cat":"civilian","roles":["\"Gussy Acorn\" — band member"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-04-11-001"],"first":"1862-04-11","last":"1862-04-11","s":"Augustus Hammel, nicknamed \"Gussy Acorn,\" was a band member in the 60th New York mentioned by Alexander in April 1862, one of the regimental musicians who provided morale through camp life."},{"id":"PER-niles","n":"Gen. Niles","cat":"military","roles":["Countermanded marching orders"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-04-11-001"],"first":"1862-04-11","last":"1862-04-11","s":"General Niles countermanded marching orders for the 60th New York in April 1862, an abrupt change of plans Alexander reported as the regiment experienced the frequent false starts typical of army life."},{"id":"PER-timothy-hoyle","n":"Mr. Timothy Hoyle","cat":"civilian","roles":["Ill — \"public calamity\" if dies; James owes him"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-04-11-001"],"first":"1862-04-11","last":"1862-04-11","s":"Mr. Timothy Hoyle was gravely ill in April 1862, and Alexander described his potential death as a \"public calamity,\" while also noting that James owed Hoyle money — intertwining personal debt with community grief."},{"id":"PER-plumer","n":"Plumer","cat":"civilian","roles":["Potential farm hire — Henry doubts reliability"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-04-21-002"],"first":"1862-04-21","last":"1862-04-21","s":"Plumer was a potential farm hire whom Henry discussed in April 1862 but doubted would prove reliable, reflecting the family's ongoing struggle to manage their Champlain farm with the brothers away at war."},{"id":"PER-royal-corbin","n":"Royal Corbin","cat":"civilian","roles":["16th Regt; seen at West Point"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-05-09-001"],"first":"1862-05-09","last":"1862-05-09","s":"Royal Corbin of the 16th Regiment was spotted by Henry Hubbell at West Point in May 1862, a brief encounter with a fellow soldier from the home region."},{"id":"PER-george","n":"George","cat":"civilian","roles":["16th Regt; with Royal Corbin"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-05-09-001"],"first":"1862-05-09","last":"1862-05-09","s":"George accompanied Royal Corbin of the 16th Regiment when Henry Hubbell encountered them at West Point in May 1862."},{"id":"PER-royal--corbin","n":"Royal [Corbin]","cat":"civilian","roles":["16th Regt; shook hands"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-05-09-002"],"first":"1862-05-09","last":"1862-05-09","s":"Royal Corbin of the 16th Regiment shook hands with Henry Hubbell during a chance meeting at West Point in May 1862, a small reunion between soldiers from the same region of New York."},{"id":"PER-john-pickle","n":"John Pickle","cat":"civilian","roles":["Court-martialed — absent 24 hours, lost half of 2 months' pay"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-05-10-001"],"first":"1862-05-10","last":"1862-05-10","s":"John Pickle of the 60th New York was court-martialed for being absent twenty-four hours and lost half of two months' pay, a disciplinary case Alexander reported in May 1862."},{"id":"PER-aunt-jenny","n":"Aunt Jenny","cat":"civilian","roles":["Hasn't answered Alexander's letter"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-05-10-001"],"first":"1862-05-10","last":"1862-05-10","s":"Aunt Jenny had not answered Alexander Hubbell's letter as of May 1862, a lapse in correspondence that Alexander noted with evident frustration."},{"id":"PER-portray","n":"Portray","cat":"civilian","roles":["Leased \"plowed land\" — pony concerns"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-05-10-001"],"first":"1862-05-10","last":"1862-05-10","s":"Portray leased the Hubbell family's plowed land and was associated with concerns about the family pony, a farm-management detail Alexander tracked from his post in May 1862."},{"id":"PER-cyrus","n":"Cyrus","cat":"civilian","roles":["Expected back"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-06-06-001"],"first":"1862-06-06","last":"1862-06-06","s":"Cyrus was expected to return to the 34th New York's camp in June 1862, mentioned briefly by Henry in a letter that tracked the comings and goings of soldiers and acquaintances."},{"id":"PER-jayson","n":"Jayson","cat":"civilian","roles":["Expected back"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-06-06-001"],"first":"1862-06-06","last":"1862-06-06","s":"Jayson was expected back at camp alongside Cyrus in June 1862, as Henry Hubbell noted the movements of men within the 34th New York."},{"id":"PER-albert-h--cook","n":"Albert H. Cook","cat":"civilian","roles":["Surprise reunion; \"fine looking fellow\"; Banks Signal Corps"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-06-08-001"],"first":"1862-06-08","last":"1862-06-08","s":"Albert H. Cook, described by Alexander as a \"fine looking fellow,\" appeared in a surprise reunion in June 1862 while serving in Banks's Signal Corps — an unexpected encounter between Champlain-area men in the Shenandoah Valley."},{"id":"PER-brinkerhoff-miner","n":"Lieut. Brinkerhoff Miner","cat":"military","roles":["\"Same old sixpence\"; Banks Signal Corps"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-06-08-001"],"first":"1862-06-08","last":"1862-06-08","s":"Lieutenant Brinkerhoff Miner, whom Alexander called the \"same old sixpence,\" also served in Banks's Signal Corps and was encountered by Alexander in June 1862 during the Valley Campaign."},{"id":"PER-siegel","n":"Gen. Siegel","cat":"military","roles":["Commands above 60th NY; under Banks"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-06-08-001"],"first":"1862-06-08","last":"1862-06-08","s":"General Franz Sigel commanded the corps above the 60th New York under General Banks, as Alexander noted in June 1862 while describing his regiment's position in the chain of command during the Shenandoah Valley operations."},{"id":"PER-stow","n":"Gen. Stow","cat":"military","roles":["Would object to regiment recall"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-06-08-001"],"first":"1862-06-08","last":"1862-06-08","s":"General Stow was mentioned by Alexander in June 1862 as a commander who would object to the 60th New York being recalled, reflecting debates about the regiment's assignment during the Valley Campaign."},{"id":"PER-peter-gugan","n":"Peter Gugan","cat":"civilian","roles":["Only one who kept knapsack"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-07-08-002"],"first":"1862-07-08","last":"1862-07-08","s":"Peter Gugan was the only soldier in Henry Hubbell's company who managed to keep his knapsack during a grueling march, a distinction Henry noted with dry humor in July 1862."},{"id":"PER-billings","n":"Billings","cat":"civilian","roles":["Officer, sun-struck during charge"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-07-12-001"],"first":"1862-07-12","last":"1862-07-12","s":"Billings was an officer in the 34th New York who was struck by sunstroke during a charge in July 1862, one of the many heat casualties that accompanied summer combat in Virginia."},{"id":"PER-walker","n":"Walker","cat":"civilian","roles":["Current head doctor"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-07-12-001"],"first":"1862-07-12","last":"1862-07-12","s":"Walker served as the current head doctor for the 34th New York as of July 1862, treating the regiment's sick and wounded during the grueling Peninsula Campaign."},{"id":"PER-mary-doolittle","n":"Mary Doolittle","cat":"civilian","roles":["Letter received from her"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-07-14-001"],"first":"1862-07-14","last":"1862-07-14","s":"Mary Doolittle wrote a letter to Alexander Hubbell that he acknowledged receiving in July 1862, one of the Champlain-area correspondents who sustained the brothers through wartime."},{"id":"PER-first-lieutenant","n":"First Lieutenant (unnamed)","cat":"military","roles":["Died July 27 at 10 PM — \"Christian Man\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-07-26-001"],"first":"1862-07-26","last":"1862-07-26","s":"An unnamed First Lieutenant in the 60th New York, described as a \"Christian Man,\" died on July 27, 1862, at 10 PM — a death that deeply affected Alexander and the company."},{"id":"PER-sterns","n":"Sergeant Sterns","cat":"military","roles":["\"Never saw so happy a death\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-07-26-001"],"first":"1862-07-26","last":"1862-07-26","s":"Sergeant Sterns of the 60th New York died in late July 1862 in what Alexander described as the happiest death he had ever witnessed, reflecting the era's ideal of a peaceful Christian passing."},{"id":"PER-foote","n":"Dr. Foote","cat":"military","roles":["Examining physician — found Charles near-sighted"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-09-09-001"],"first":"1862-09-09","last":"1862-09-09","s":"Dr. Foote was the examining physician who found Charles Hubbell to be near-sighted during his enlistment physical in September 1862, a condition that did not ultimately prevent Charles from joining the 118th New York."},{"id":"PER-youngs","n":"Mr. Youngs","cat":"civilian","roles":["Church minister in Plattsburgh"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-09-09-001"],"first":"1862-09-09","last":"1862-09-09","s":"Mr. Youngs was the church minister in Plattsburgh whom Charles Hubbell heard preach in September 1862, shortly before Charles departed for military service with the 118th New York."},{"id":"PER-pet-stacia","n":"Pet Stacia","cat":"civilian","roles":["With Charles at church"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-09-09-001"],"first":"1862-09-09","last":"1862-09-09","s":"Pet Stacia accompanied Charles Hubbell to church in Plattsburgh in September 1862, a brief social detail from the days just before Charles's departure for the army."},{"id":"PER-unnamed-man-of-34th-ny","n":"Unnamed man of 34th NY","cat":"civilian","roles":["Reported Henry missing, \"quite sure not killed\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-09-19-001"],"first":"1862-09-19","last":"1862-09-19","s":"An unnamed man from the 34th New York reported to Alexander that Henry Hubbell was missing after the Battle of Antietam but was \"quite sure\" Henry had not been killed, offering fragile hope in September 1862."},{"id":"PER-michie","n":"Mr. Michie","cat":"civilian","roles":["Cadet Lieutenant of James's company — \"very kind\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-09-21-001"],"first":"1862-09-21","last":"1862-09-21","s":"Mr. Michie served as the cadet lieutenant of James Hubbell's company at West Point and was described by James in September 1862 as \"very kind,\" a rare positive assessment of an authority figure from the young cadet."},{"id":"PER-prof--french","n":"Prof. French","cat":"civilian","roles":["Gave prayer books and \"The Bible and Men of Learning\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-09-21-001"],"first":"1862-09-21","last":"1862-09-21","s":"Professor French at West Point gave James Hubbell prayer books and a copy of \"The Bible and Men of Learning\" in September 1862, nurturing the religious education that the Hubbell family valued deeply."},{"id":"PER-young-hoyt","n":"Young Hoyt","cat":"civilian","roles":["Cadet; \"thrown down into the immortals\" (6th section)"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-09-21-001"],"first":"1862-09-21","last":"1862-09-21","s":"Young Hoyt was a fellow cadet at West Point who had been \"thrown down into the immortals\" — demoted to the sixth academic section — as James reported in September 1862."},{"id":"PER-uncle-hubbell","n":"Uncle Hubbell","cat":"civilian","roles":["Mentioned Hoyt"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-09-21-001"],"first":"1862-09-21","last":"1862-09-21","s":"Uncle Hubbell was a family member who had mentioned the cadet Hoyt to the Hubbells, providing James a connection point when he encountered Hoyt at West Point in September 1862."},{"id":"PER-carter","n":"Mrs. Carter","cat":"civilian","roles":["Neighbor — \"overwhelmed with grief at the death of her son\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-09-21-002"],"first":"1862-09-21","last":"1862-09-21","s":"Mrs. Carter was a neighbor in Champlain who was overwhelmed with grief at the death of her son in battle, as Mother Frances reported in September 1862 amid the wave of casualties from Antietam."},{"id":"PER-carter-s-son","n":"Mrs. Carter's son","cat":"civilian","roles":["Killed (presumably at Antietam or related battle)"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-09-21-002"],"first":"1862-09-21","last":"1862-09-21","s":"Mrs. Carter's son was killed in battle — presumably at Antietam or a related engagement — and his death was reported by Mother Frances in September 1862 as part of the devastating toll the war exacted on the Champlain community."},{"id":"PER-ashmond","n":"Ashmond","cat":"civilian","roles":["Someone at \"the hospitals\" — Mother can't get info from him"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-09-28-001"],"first":"1862-09-28","last":"1862-09-28","s":"Ashmond was a contact at the military hospitals from whom Mother Frances tried unsuccessfully to obtain information about Henry's fate after he went missing at Antietam in September 1862."},{"id":"PER-big-shaw","n":"\"Big Shaw\"","cat":"civilian","roles":["Enlisted at Plattsburgh, over 6 feet, lived on Dudleys farm"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-09-30-002"],"first":"1862-09-30","last":"1862-09-30","s":"\"Big Shaw\" was a soldier over six feet tall who had enlisted at Plattsburgh and lived on the Dudleys' farm, mentioned by Charles Hubbell in September 1862 as a recognizable local figure now in uniform."},{"id":"PER-r-w--mcdonald","n":"R.W. McDonald","cat":"civilian","roles":["Author, Henry's tentmate in 34th NY"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-10-01-001"],"first":"1862-10-01","last":"1862-10-01","s":"R.W. McDonald was Henry Hubbell's tentmate in the 34th New York who wrote to the Hubbell family in October 1862, providing firsthand information about Henry's status after the Battle of Antietam."},{"id":"PER-doctor-thurman","n":"Doctor Thurman","cat":"civilian","roles":["Went back to see to the wounded; expected to return with news"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-10-01-001"],"first":"1862-10-01","last":"1862-10-01","s":"Doctor Thurman went back to attend to the wounded after the Battle of Antietam and was expected to return with news about Henry Hubbell's fate, as McDonald reported in October 1862."},{"id":"PER-stonewall-jackson","n":"Stonewall Jackson","cat":"civilian","roles":["Confederate general — headquarters 7.5 miles from McDonald's position"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-10-01-001"],"first":"1862-10-01","last":"1862-10-01","s":"Confederate General Stonewall Jackson's headquarters were reported to be just seven and a half miles from McDonald's position in October 1862, a measure of the close proximity between Union and Confederate forces after Antietam."},{"id":"PER-davalls","n":"DaValls","cat":"civilian","roles":["Detailed to examine the battlefield; says Henry Hubbell missing"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-10-02-001"],"first":"1862-10-02","last":"1862-10-02","s":"DaValls was detailed to examine the Antietam battlefield and reported that Henry Hubbell was among the missing, information Mother Frances relayed in her anguished October 1862 letter."},{"id":"PER-young-fiser","n":"Young Fiser","cat":"civilian","roles":["Reports: \"Henry was seen to fall, rebels within two rods\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-10-06-001"],"first":"1862-10-06","last":"1862-10-06","s":"Young Fiser provided the most specific eyewitness account of Henry's fate, reporting that \"Henry was seen to fall\" with rebels within two rods — information Mother Frances recorded in her October 1862 letter as the family sought answers."},{"id":"PER-safin","n":"Col. Safin[?]","cat":"military","roles":["Colonel of the 34th NY — Mother writing him for information"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-10-06-001"],"first":"1862-10-06","last":"1862-10-06","s":"Colonel Safin of the 34th New York was the regimental commander to whom Mother Frances wrote seeking information about Henry's status after Antietam, turning to the chain of command when informal channels failed."},{"id":"PER-the-little-french-boy","n":"\"The little French boy\"","cat":"civilian","roles":["Caring for Tony (pony) this winter"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-10-06-001"],"first":"1862-10-06","last":"1862-10-06","s":"The little French boy was caring for Tony, the Hubbell family pony, during the winter of 1862, a small domestic detail Mother Frances mentioned while consumed with anxiety over Henry's disappearance."},{"id":"PER-bummings","n":"Mr. Bummings","cat":"civilian","roles":["Mother leaving letter at his house for Mr. Ransom"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-10-06-001"],"first":"1862-10-06","last":"1862-10-06","s":"Mr. Bummings was a Champlain neighbor at whose house Mother Frances left a letter for Mr. Ransom in October 1862, using the community's informal courier network to reach Captain Ransom for news of Henry."},{"id":"PER-halleck","n":"Gen. Halleck","cat":"military","roles":["Quoted: \"no favor to a soldier to increase the weight of his knapsack\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-10-06-001"],"first":"1862-10-06","last":"1862-10-06","s":"General Henry Halleck was quoted by Mother Frances in October 1862 as declaring it \"no favor to a soldier to increase the weight of his knapsack,\" a policy she referenced while preparing packages for her sons."},{"id":"PER-r-m--hubbell","n":"Mrs. R.M. Hubbell","cat":"civilian","roles":["Charles called on her at Plattsburgh"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-10-10-001"],"first":"1862-10-10","last":"1862-10-10","s":"Mrs. R.M. Hubbell was a relative in Plattsburgh whom Charles Hubbell called upon in October 1862, maintaining family connections as he prepared for service with the 118th New York."},{"id":"PER-david-s","n":"David S.","cat":"civilian","roles":["Charles sorry he couldn't see him"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-10-10-001"],"first":"1862-10-10","last":"1862-10-10","s":"David S. was someone Charles regretted not being able to see during his time in Plattsburgh in October 1862, a missed connection during the rushed days before deployment."},{"id":"PER-savages","n":"The Savages","cat":"civilian","roles":["Store owners — \"always treated me well\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-10-10-001"],"first":"1862-10-10","last":"1862-10-10","s":"The Savages were store owners whom Charles Hubbell praised in October 1862 for having \"always treated me well,\" a valued commercial relationship in the small community around Champlain."},{"id":"PER-marshall","n":"Mr. Marshall","cat":"civilian","roles":["Carrying Charles's $25 check home"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-10-15-001"],"first":"1862-10-15","last":"1862-10-15","s":"Mr. Marshall carried Charles Hubbell's twenty-five-dollar check home to the family in October 1862, serving as a trusted courier for the financial transfers that sustained the home front."},{"id":"PER-j---r-l--savage","n":"J & R.L. Savage","cat":"civilian","roles":["Owed $16.55"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-10-15-001"],"first":"1862-10-15","last":"1862-10-15","s":"J. and R.L. Savage were owed sixteen dollars and fifty-five cents by Charles Hubbell, a debt he noted in October 1862 as he settled his affairs before deploying with the 118th New York."},{"id":"PER-little-joe","n":"Little Joe","cat":"civilian","roles":["Stays nights at the Hubbell home"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-10-29-001"],"first":"1862-10-29","last":"1862-10-29","s":"Little Joe stayed nights at the Hubbell home in Champlain in October 1862, providing Mother Frances with company and perhaps help around the house while her sons were away at war."},{"id":"PER-clark-copeland","n":"Clark Copeland","cat":"civilian","roles":["Sick with typhoid fever"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-10-29-001"],"first":"1862-10-29","last":"1862-10-29","s":"Clark Copeland was sick with typhoid fever in October 1862, as Mother Frances reported — likely a relative of Mr. Copeland, the family advisor Alexander had consulted about the Brother Craftsmen society."},{"id":"PER-tom","n":"Tom","cat":"civilian","roles":["Visited — thinks Charles not robust enough for army; was discharged himself for being \"not able bodied\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-10-29-001"],"first":"1862-10-29","last":"1862-10-29","s":"Tom visited the Hubbell home in October 1862 and expressed doubt that Charles was robust enough for army service, speaking from personal experience as someone who had been discharged himself for not being \"able bodied.\""},{"id":"PER-fannie-frances-hubbell","n":"Frances ('Fannie') Hubbell","cat":"family","roles":["**SISTER** to Charles (and brothers) — signs \"your sister\""],"rel":"Sister","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-11-10-001"],"first":"1862-11-10","last":"1862-11-10","s":"Frances \"Fannie\" Hubbell was the Hubbell brothers' sister who signed her letters \"your sister\" and served as a primary correspondent and anchor of the family's home-front network throughout the war."},{"id":"PER-young-hubbell","n":"Young Hubbell (Confederate)","cat":"civilian","roles":["Rebel officer seen by Misner in Richmond — probably Inlis or Octave"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-11-10-001"],"first":"1862-11-10","last":"1862-11-10","s":"Young Hubbell was a Confederate officer — probably Inlis or Octave — spotted by Misner in Richmond, a jarring reminder of the family's divided loyalties as Mother Frances reported in November 1862."},{"id":"PER-uncle-l","n":"Uncle L.","cat":"civilian","roles":["Family member — Confederate Hubbell is his nephew"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-11-10-001"],"first":"1862-11-10","last":"1862-11-10","s":"Uncle L. was a Hubbell family member whose nephew served as a Confederate officer in Richmond, embodying the painful split between Northern and Southern branches of the extended Hubbell family."},{"id":"PER-bash----loring","n":"Bash[?] Loring","cat":"civilian","roles":["In the Confederate Hubbell's regiment as Quartermaster"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-11-10-001"],"first":"1862-11-10","last":"1862-11-10","s":"Bash Loring served as quartermaster in the same Confederate regiment as the young Hubbell officer seen in Richmond, a detail Mother Frances noted in November 1862 that deepened the picture of divided family allegiances."},{"id":"PER-j--morrison","n":"J. Morrison","cat":"civilian","roles":["Replaced Alek Douglas at [?store/position]"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-11-10-001"],"first":"1862-11-10","last":"1862-11-10","s":"J. Morrison replaced Alek Douglas at a local position or store, a minor community personnel change Mother Frances reported in November 1862 alongside far graver war news."},{"id":"PER-vossey--miss-cundia--ida-niles","n":"Mr. Vossey, Miss Cundia, Ida Niles","cat":"civilian","roles":["Wrote letters to Mother about Henry — \"heard the worst\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-11-10-001"],"first":"1862-11-10","last":"1862-11-10","s":"Mr. Vossey, Miss Cundia, and Ida Niles all wrote letters to Mother Frances about Henry after Antietam, reporting they had \"heard the worst\" — part of the flood of concerned correspondence that followed Henry's disappearance."},{"id":"PER-averill-s-servant-girl","n":"Mr. Averill's servant girl","cat":"civilian","roles":["Mother writes French love letters for her"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-11-10-001"],"first":"1862-11-10","last":"1862-11-10","s":"Mr. Averill's servant girl enlisted Mother Frances's help writing French love letters in November 1862, an unexpectedly tender domestic detail amid the family's anxious search for news of Henry."},{"id":"PER-sam-pelky","n":"Sam Pelky","cat":"civilian","roles":["Tentmate"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-11-18-001"],"first":"1862-11-18","last":"1862-11-18","s":"Sam Pelky was Charles Hubbell's tentmate in the 118th New York in November 1862, one of Charles's first close associations as he settled into military life."},{"id":"PER-jones","n":"Jones (James Hubbell)","cat":"civilian","roles":["At West Point — Charles wrote him yesterday"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-11-18-001"],"first":"1862-11-18","last":"1862-11-18","s":"Jones was James Hubbell's designation at West Point — cadets were often known by surname — and Charles noted in November 1862 that he had written to James the previous day."},{"id":"PER-col--frentress","n":"Lieut. Col. Frentress[?]","cat":"military","roles":["Friendly to Charles, strict — Fonda men don't like him"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-11-18-001"],"first":"1862-11-18","last":"1862-11-18","s":"Lieutenant Colonel Frentress of the 118th New York was described by Charles in November 1862 as friendly toward him but strict in discipline, which made him unpopular with the Fonda men in the regiment."},{"id":"PER-sabastine","n":"Col. Sabastine[?]","cat":"military","roles":["Absent, has been sick"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-11-18-001"],"first":"1862-11-18","last":"1862-11-18","s":"Colonel Sabastine of the 118th New York had been absent from the regiment due to illness, as Charles reported in November 1862, leaving the unit under subordinate command."},{"id":"PER-karns","n":"Mr. Karns","cat":"civilian","roles":["Frances's less-preferred colleague"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-11-18-001"],"first":"1862-11-18","last":"1862-11-18","s":"Mr. Karns was a colleague whom Frances Hubbell found less agreeable, a personal dynamic Charles mentioned in his November 1862 letter while relaying news from the home front."},{"id":"PER-battiste","n":"Battiste","cat":"civilian","roles":["May draw the cedar logs"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-11-18-001"],"first":"1862-11-18","last":"1862-11-18","s":"Battiste was expected to draw cedar logs for the Hubbell property, a farm-management detail Charles discussed in November 1862 as the family juggled agricultural work with wartime separation."},{"id":"PER-ruth-hubbell---sons","n":"Mrs. Ruth Hubbell & sons","cat":"civilian","roles":["Prospering — land purchase discussed"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-11-18-001"],"first":"1862-11-18","last":"1862-11-18","s":"Mrs. Ruth Hubbell and her sons were prospering and involved in a land purchase discussion, as Charles noted in November 1862 — likely extended family whose fortunes contrasted with the war-disrupted Hubbell household."},{"id":"PER-a--whiteside","n":"Mr. A. Whiteside","cat":"civilian","roles":["Paying for a substitute — \"craven hearted coward\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-11-25-001"],"first":"1862-11-25","last":"1862-11-25","s":"Mr. A. Whiteside paid for a substitute to avoid military service, earning Alexander Hubbell's scathing label of \"craven hearted coward\" in his November 1862 letter."},{"id":"PER-e--doolittle","n":"E. Doolittle","cat":"civilian","roles":["Paying for a substitute — \"craven hearted coward\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-11-25-001"],"first":"1862-11-25","last":"1862-11-25","s":"E. Doolittle also paid for a substitute rather than serve, condemned alongside A. Whiteside by Alexander Hubbell as a \"craven hearted coward\" in November 1862."},{"id":"PER-f--borron","n":"F. Borron","cat":"civilian","roles":["Acquaintance from Champlain — cavalry, \"would not go home for anything\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-11-28-001"],"first":"1862-11-28","last":"1862-11-28","s":"F. Borron was an acquaintance from Champlain serving in the cavalry who told Charles in November 1862 that he \"would not go home for anything,\" expressing the determined spirit of a committed volunteer."},{"id":"PER-mathews","n":"Mathews","cat":"civilian","roles":["In Henry's regiment — provided information about Henry"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-08-001"],"first":"1862-12-08","last":"1862-12-08","s":"Mathews served in Henry's regiment, the 34th New York, and provided information about Henry to Charles in December 1862 as the family continued piecing together what had happened to Henry at Antietam."},{"id":"PER-bethune","n":"Bethune","cat":"civilian","roles":["Capt. Rich's old company — had newspaper clipping of killed & wounded"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-08-001"],"first":"1862-12-08","last":"1862-12-08","s":"Bethune was a member of Captain Rich's old company whose newspaper clipping listing killed and wounded soldiers was referenced in Charles Hubbell's December 1862 letter from the convalescent camp near Alexandria."},{"id":"PER-ben-bouron","n":"Ben Bouron","cat":"civilian","roles":["Going to Yorktown to see brother Lyman; cost $16 to come here"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-08-001"],"first":"1862-12-08","last":"1862-12-08","s":"Ben Bouron was a Champlain community member who traveled to Yorktown in December 1862 to visit his brother Lyman, spending sixteen dollars for the journey, as reported by Charles Hubbell."},{"id":"PER-freeman-houron","n":"Freeman Houron","cat":"civilian","roles":["With Ben Bouron"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-08-001"],"first":"1862-12-08","last":"1862-12-08","s":"Freeman Houron accompanied Ben Bouron on his December 1862 trip to visit soldiers in Virginia, mentioned in passing by Charles Hubbell in a letter to their mother."},{"id":"PER-staats","n":"Staats","cat":"civilian","roles":["\"If he ever gets home they will not catch him again\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-08-001"],"first":"1862-12-08","last":"1862-12-08","s":"Staats was a local man about whom Charles Hubbell reported in December 1862 that if he ever got home, they would not catch him again — suggesting desertion or draft evasion."},{"id":"PER-neshan","n":"Neshan","cat":"civilian","roles":["Could draw the saw logs"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-08-001"],"first":"1862-12-08","last":"1862-12-08","s":"Neshan was a Champlain-area resident mentioned by Charles Hubbell in December 1862 as someone who could draw the saw logs, reflecting the home-front labor concerns that threaded through wartime correspondence."},{"id":"PER-lyman","n":"Lyman","cat":"civilian","roles":["Ben Bouron's brother at Yorktown — Ben going to visit him"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-08-001"],"first":"1862-12-08","last":"1862-12-08","s":"Lyman was Ben Bouron's brother stationed at Yorktown, Virginia, whom Ben traveled to visit in December 1862, as noted in Charles Hubbell's letter home."},{"id":"PER-mary-ann-brinkerhoff","n":"Mary Ann Brinkerhoff","cat":"civilian","roles":["Woman Pollard may have married — Charles asks about it"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-08-001"],"first":"1862-12-08","last":"1862-12-08","s":"Mary Ann Brinkerhoff was a woman whom Charles Hubbell asked about in December 1862, inquiring whether Pollard had married her — part of the community gossip exchanged between soldiers and home."},{"id":"PER-dennis-hayes","n":"Dennis Hayes","cat":"civilian","roles":["Buried by strangers before company returned — Henry may have been too"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-09-001"],"first":"1862-12-09","last":"1862-12-09","s":"Dennis Hayes was a soldier buried by strangers before his company returned from battle, cited by Mother Frances Hubbell in December 1862 as a parallel to Henry's possible fate at Antietam."},{"id":"PER-monsieur-cushas","n":"Monsieur Cushas","cat":"civilian","roles":["\"Days are numbered\" — leaving?"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-09-001"],"first":"1862-12-09","last":"1862-12-09","s":"Monsieur Cushas was a Champlain resident whose \"days are numbered\" according to Mother Frances Hubbell's December 1862 letter, suggesting he was either departing the area or in declining health."},{"id":"PER-carter-boys","n":"The Carter boys","cat":"civilian","roles":["Having heavy shirts made to send to soldiers"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-09-001"],"first":"1862-12-09","last":"1862-12-09","s":"The Carter boys were Champlain community members who had heavy shirts made to send to soldiers in the field, mentioned by Mother Frances in her December 1862 letter as part of home-front support efforts."},{"id":"PER-mary-jane-brinkerhoff","n":"Mary Jane Brinkerhoff","cat":"civilian","roles":["Marrying Pollard this week — 'a Mrs. Brinkerhoff old girl'"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-09-001"],"first":"1862-12-09","last":"1862-12-09","s":"Mary Jane Brinkerhoff was a local woman whom Mother Frances reported in December 1862 was marrying Pollard that week, described as \"a Mrs. Brinkerhoff old girl.\""},{"id":"PER-j-b--ransom","n":"Capt. J.B. Ransom","cat":"military","roles":["Visited Charles's Co.; said Alexander was in \"first place\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-26-001"],"first":"1862-12-26","last":"1862-12-26","s":"Captain J.B. Ransom of the 60th New York Volunteers visited Charles Hubbell's company in December 1862 and reported that Alexander Hubbell held \"first place\" in his unit."},{"id":"PER-mcmartin","n":"Col. McMartin","cat":"military","roles":["Told men they're better off here than at front; expects to stay winter"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-26-001"],"first":"1862-12-26","last":"1862-12-26","s":"Colonel McMartin commanded the regiment at the convalescent camp near Alexandria in late 1862, telling his men they were better off there than at the front and expecting to stay through the winter."},{"id":"PER-s--tombe","n":"S. Tombe","cat":"civilian","roles":["Frances's old friend — to be discharged; asked about Frances"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-26-001"],"first":"1862-12-26","last":"1862-12-26","s":"S. Tombe was an old friend of Frances Hubbell's who was about to be discharged from service in December 1862 and asked after Frances when encountered near camp by Charles."},{"id":"PER-1st-lieut--mcguire","n":"1st Lieut. McGuire","cat":"military","roles":["Catholic — takes squad to Catholic church Sundays"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-26-001"],"first":"1862-12-26","last":"1862-12-26","s":"First Lieutenant McGuire was a Catholic officer in Charles Hubbell's regiment who took a squad to the Catholic church on Sundays, noted in a December 1862 letter about camp religious life."},{"id":"PER-tromby","n":"Tromby","cat":"civilian","roles":["Actually wrote Savagham's letter"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-26-001"],"first":"1862-12-26","last":"1862-12-26","s":"Tromby was the actual author of a letter attributed to Savagham, mentioned by Charles Hubbell in December 1862 as part of the chain of correspondence connecting soldiers and home."},{"id":"PER-norman","n":"Norman","cat":"civilian","roles":["Cyrus Kellogg's uncle — Kellogg visits him at camp"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-26-001"],"first":"1862-12-26","last":"1862-12-26","s":"Norman was Cyrus Kellogg's uncle whom Kellogg visited at camp, mentioned by Charles Hubbell in a December 1862 letter cataloging the visitors and social connections passing through the garrison."},{"id":"PER-nodiah-moores","n":"Mrs. Nodiah Moores","cat":"civilian","roles":["Hosted a party that S. Tombe was invited to"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-26-001"],"first":"1862-12-26","last":"1862-12-26","s":"Mrs. Nodiah Moores was a community acquaintance who hosted a party to which S. Tombe was invited, mentioned in Charles Hubbell's December 1862 letter about social life in the Champlain circle."},{"id":"PER-vanberton","n":"Mrs. Vanberton","cat":"civilian","roles":["Urged Tombe to come see Charles — 'he was very polite'"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1862-12-26-001"],"first":"1862-12-26","last":"1862-12-26","s":"Mrs. Vanberton urged S. Tombe to visit Charles Hubbell at camp, noting that Tombe \"was very polite,\" as reported in Charles's December 1862 letter home."},{"id":"PER-james-luther","n":"James Luther","cat":"civilian","roles":["Sends respects — looks fat and healthy"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-01-02-001"],"first":"1863-01-02","last":"1863-01-02","s":"James Luther was a soldier who sent his respects to the Hubbell family and was described by Charles in January 1863 as looking \"fat and healthy\" — a common wartime measure of wellbeing."},{"id":"PER-turners","n":"Mrs. Turners","cat":"civilian","roles":["Neighbor — Mr. Savage sold lots near her property"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-01-02-001"],"first":"1863-01-02","last":"1863-01-02","s":"Mrs. Turners was a Champlain neighbor mentioned by Charles Hubbell in January 1863 in connection with Mr. Savage selling lots near her property, reflecting home-front real estate dealings."},{"id":"PER-c--fullerton","n":"C. Fullerton","cat":"civilian","roles":["Frances's correspondent — letter on Observer wrapper"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-01-31-001"],"first":"1863-01-31","last":"1863-01-31","s":"C. Fullerton was a correspondent of Frances Hubbell's who wrote a letter on an Observer newspaper wrapper, mentioned by Charles in January 1863."},{"id":"PER-mrs--copeland","n":"Mr. & Mrs. Copeland","cat":"civilian","roles":["Remembered"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-02-06-001"],"first":"1863-02-06","last":"1863-02-06","s":"Mr. and Mrs. Copeland were Champlain acquaintances to whom Alexander Hubbell sent his remembrances in a February 1863 letter written from near Stafford House, Virginia."},{"id":"PER-d--v--savage","n":"D. V. Savage","cat":"civilian","roles":["Wrote to Charles"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-02-13-001"],"first":"1863-02-13","last":"1863-02-13","s":"D. V. Savage was a correspondent who wrote to Charles Hubbell, mentioned in Charles's February 1863 letter during Hooker's army reorganization period."},{"id":"PER-gokey","n":"Gokey","cat":"civilian","roles":["Visited Mrs. Burdick with Douglas"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-02-13-001"],"first":"1863-02-13","last":"1863-02-13","s":"Gokey was a local acquaintance who visited Mrs. Burdick with Douglas, mentioned in Charles Hubbell's February 1863 letter to Frances as part of community news from the home front."},{"id":"PER-sydney","n":"Sydney","cat":"civilian","roles":["Asked about — does he work? Has a boy?"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-02-22-001"],"first":"1863-02-22","last":"1863-02-22","s":"Sydney was a person Charles Hubbell asked about in February 1863, inquiring whether he worked and whether he had a boy — part of the routine home-front queries soldiers maintained through letters."},{"id":"PER-charles--charley","n":"Charles/\"Charley\"","cat":"civilian","roles":["Sleeping with \"the fat Frenchman\" — Alexander wants to send socks"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-02-28-001"],"first":"1863-02-28","last":"1863-02-28","s":"Charles, called \"Charley,\" was a young person — likely a neighbor or family acquaintance — whom Alexander Hubbell mentioned in February 1863 as sleeping with \"the fat Frenchman\" and to whom he wanted to send socks."},{"id":"PER-savagham--the-fat-frenchman","n":"Savagham/\"the fat Frenchman\"","cat":"civilian","roles":["Charles's sleeping partner"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-02-28-001"],"first":"1863-02-28","last":"1863-02-28","s":"Savagham, nicknamed \"the fat Frenchman,\" was Charles's sleeping partner at camp, a figure of gentle humor in Alexander Hubbell's February 1863 letter teasing Frances about the Champlain Frenchmen."},{"id":"PER-delany","n":"Capt. Delany","cat":"military","roles":["New captain — formerly Lieut. of Co. F; \"one after my own heart\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-03-08-001"],"first":"1863-03-08","last":"1863-03-08","s":"Captain Delany, formerly a lieutenant in Company F of the 60th New York, became the new captain of Company H in March 1863 after complete officer turnover, praised by Alexander Hubbell as \"a most excellent man — one after my own heart.\""},{"id":"PER-cousin-mary-mcneil","n":"Cousin Mary McNeil","cat":"civilian","roles":["From Goodrich, Michigan — sent political letter"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-03-08-001"],"first":"1863-03-08","last":"1863-03-08","s":"Cousin Mary McNeil of Goodrich, Michigan, sent Alexander Hubbell a political letter in March 1863, offering a civilian perspective from the rural Midwest on the war and emancipation."},{"id":"PER-charley-charles","n":"Charley/Charles","cat":"civilian","roles":["Asked about — still with \"the fat Frenchman\"?"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-03-08-001"],"first":"1863-03-08","last":"1863-03-08","s":"Charley was a young acquaintance Alexander Hubbell asked about in March 1863, wondering whether he was still living with \"the fat Frenchman\" — a recurring thread of domestic curiosity in his letters to Frances."},{"id":"PER-cleveland","n":"Cleveland","cat":"civilian","roles":["May get the homestead — concern"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-03-12-001"],"first":"1863-03-12","last":"1863-03-12","s":"Cleveland was a person who might acquire the Hubbell family homestead, a concern Charles Hubbell raised in his March 1863 letter alongside news of visiting Alexander at Aquia Creek."},{"id":"PER-richard-resham","n":"Richard/Resham","cat":"civilian","roles":["Neighbor — fence building question"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-03-12-001"],"first":"1863-03-12","last":"1863-03-12","s":"Richard, also called Resham, was a Champlain neighbor about whom Charles Hubbell asked a fence-building question in March 1863, reflecting the ongoing property management concerns of absent soldiers."},{"id":"PER-dana-lyman","n":"Dana Lyman","cat":"civilian","roles":["Died — Mrs. Hoyle sent notice"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-03-16-001"],"first":"1863-03-16","last":"1863-03-16","s":"Dana Lyman was a community member whose death was reported to Alexander Hubbell by Mrs. Hoyle in March 1863, noted in a letter about mail misdirection between the Hubbell brothers' regiments."},{"id":"PER-beardsley","n":"Beardsley","cat":"civilian","roles":["Corporal — conceited but good-natured; sister sends papers"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-03-19-001"],"first":"1863-03-19","last":"1863-03-19","s":"Beardsley was a corporal described by Charles Hubbell in March 1863 as conceited but good-natured, whose sister sent newspapers to the regiment — one of the small social bonds connecting camp and home."},{"id":"PER-s--p--hubbell-silas","n":"S. P. Hubbell/Silas","cat":"civilian","roles":["Counsellor-at-law — lectured at Academy; teased about hens and heifer"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-03-22-001"],"first":"1863-03-22","last":"1863-03-22","s":"S. P. Hubbell, called Silas, was a counsellor-at-law who lectured at the Academy in Champlain, teased by Alexander Hubbell in March 1863 for his oratory about hens and heifers."},{"id":"PER-misses-crowly","n":"Misses Crowly","cat":"civilian","roles":["With Mr. Simons"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-03-22-001"],"first":"1863-03-22","last":"1863-03-22","s":"The Misses Crowly were young women mentioned alongside Mr. Simons in Alexander Hubbell's March 1863 letter, part of the Champlain social circle he inquired about from camp."},{"id":"PER-alma-mcyath","n":"Miss Alma Mcyath","cat":"civilian","roles":["Has a \"promise\" — Alexander expects to fulfill it in ~90 days"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-03-22-001"],"first":"1863-03-22","last":"1863-03-22","s":"Miss Alma Mcyath was a young woman to whom Alexander Hubbell had made some \"promise\" he expected to fulfill within ninety days, mentioned in his optimistic March 1863 letter predicting an honorable discharge."},{"id":"PER-misses-crowley","n":"Misses Crowley","cat":"civilian","roles":["Silas may be courting one"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-03-29-001"],"first":"1863-03-29","last":"1863-03-29","s":"The Misses Crowley were Champlain acquaintances whom Silas Hubbell may have been courting, mentioned by Alexander in a March 1863 letter teasing Frances about local romantic prospects."},{"id":"PER-loyal-widow","n":"Loyal widow (Baltimore)","cat":"civilian","roles":["Captain's admirer; supports regiment; \"noble woman\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-03-29-001"],"first":"1863-03-29","last":"1863-03-29","s":"The loyal widow of Baltimore was a Union-supporting woman who admired the regiment's captain and supported the troops, described by Alexander Hubbell in March 1863 as a \"noble woman.\""},{"id":"PER-jim-morrison","n":"Jim Morrison","cat":"civilian","roles":["Clerk — Frances knows"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-03-29-002"],"first":"1863-03-29","last":"1863-03-29","s":"Jim Morrison was a clerk known to Frances Hubbell, mentioned by Charles in his March 1863 letter — the same letter in which he stated for the second time that he feared Henry had been killed at Antietam."},{"id":"PER-albert-contreras","n":"Albert Contreras","cat":"civilian","roles":["Clerk — Frances knows"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-03-29-002"],"first":"1863-03-29","last":"1863-03-29","s":"Albert Contreras was a clerk known to Frances Hubbell, mentioned by Charles in his March 1863 letter alongside Jim Morrison as part of routine social references."},{"id":"PER-coyt","n":"Dr. Coyt","cat":"military","roles":["Said Henry Gregory has \"too big a heart\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-03-29-002"],"first":"1863-03-29","last":"1863-03-29","s":"Dr. Coyt was a military physician who commented that Henry Gregory had \"too big a heart,\" mentioned by Charles Hubbell in March 1863 among notes about soldiers' health and character."},{"id":"PER-crea","n":"Mr. Crea","cat":"civilian","roles":["Claims a corner of land — \"hard man, very close\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-04-11-001"],"first":"1863-04-11","last":"1863-04-11","s":"Mr. Crea was a local man described by Charles Hubbell in April 1863 as claiming a corner of land and being \"a hard man, very close\" — part of the property disputes the Hubbells monitored from the field."},{"id":"PER-mary-j--mcneil","n":"Mary J. McNeil","cat":"civilian","roles":["Cousin from Atlas/Goodrich, Michigan"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-04-13-001"],"first":"1863-04-13","last":"1863-04-13","s":"Mary J. McNeil was the Hubbell family's cousin from Atlas, Michigan, whose April 1863 letter described draft resistance, anti-emancipation sentiment, and high civilian mortality in the rural Midwest."},{"id":"PER-uncle-charles","n":"Uncle Charles","cat":"civilian","roles":["Thinks truce possible"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-04-13-001"],"first":"1863-04-13","last":"1863-04-13","s":"Uncle Charles was a family elder mentioned in Cousin Mary McNeil's April 1863 letter as believing a truce between North and South was possible — a view from the Michigan home front."},{"id":"PER-henry-smith","n":"Henry Smith","cat":"civilian","roles":["Mother/Frances visiting?"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-04-13-001"],"first":"1863-04-13","last":"1863-04-13","s":"Henry Smith was a person the family may have been visiting, mentioned in Cousin Mary McNeil's April 1863 letter from Michigan alongside other social news."},{"id":"PER-laura","n":"Miss Laura","cat":"civilian","roles":["Initials on wrapper sentences"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-04-16-001"],"first":"1863-04-16","last":"1863-04-16","s":"Miss Laura was an acquaintance whose initials appeared on wrapper sentences in Alexander Hubbell's April 1863 letter, part of the coded social references in wartime correspondence."},{"id":"PER-vaughan","n":"Vaughan","cat":"civilian","roles":["Paid off debt"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-04-23-001"],"first":"1863-04-23","last":"1863-04-23","s":"Vaughan was a debtor who paid off what he owed, mentioned by Alexander Hubbell in April 1863 in a letter focused on sending nearly forty-eight dollars home to Mother before the Battle of Chancellorsville."},{"id":"PER-unnamed-34th-ny-soldier","n":"Unnamed 34th NY soldier","cat":"civilian","roles":["Described Henry — knew him personally"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-04-24-001"],"first":"1863-04-24","last":"1863-04-24","s":"An unnamed soldier from the 34th New York Volunteers who had served with Henry Hubbell described Henry's moral character to Alexander in April 1863 — recalling that Henry refused plundered goods, saying \"the partaker is as bad as the thief.\""},{"id":"PER-sutler","n":"Sutler","cat":"civilian","roles":["Wrote Frances; fat and husky"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-04-24-001"],"first":"1863-04-24","last":"1863-04-24","s":"The sutler was a camp merchant who wrote to Frances Hubbell and was described by Alexander in April 1863 as \"fat and husky,\" mentioned in the same letter containing the vivid portrait of Henry's character."},{"id":"PER-dan-moore","n":"Dan Moore","cat":"civilian","roles":["Sugar business dispute"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-04-27-001"],"first":"1863-04-27","last":"1863-04-27","s":"Dan Moore was involved in a sugar business dispute mentioned by Charles Hubbell in April 1863, in the same letter that reported Abram Fry's sighting of Henry's grave marker at Antietam."},{"id":"PER-surgeon","n":"Surgeon (unnamed)","cat":"civilian","roles":["Searched field — Henry not among identified dead"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-04-30-001"],"first":"1863-04-30","last":"1863-04-30","s":"An unnamed surgeon searched the Antietam battlefield but did not find Henry Hubbell among the identified dead, as reported in Mother Frances's formal inquiry letter of April 1863 — the only letter written in her own hand in the collection."},{"id":"PER-the-rev","n":"\"The Rev.\"","cat":"civilian","roles":["Advised Mother to write"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-04-30-001"],"first":"1863-04-30","last":"1863-04-30","s":"\"The Rev.\" was a local clergyman who advised Mother Frances Hubbell to write to authorities about Henry's fate, mentioned in her April 1863 formal inquiry — the only letter she authored in the entire collection."},{"id":"PER-mckee","n":"McKee","cat":"civilian","roles":["Wrote Mother: \"I did not see Henry after he was wounded\" — contradicts McDonald"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-05-04-001"],"first":"1863-05-04","last":"1863-05-04","s":"McKee was a soldier who wrote to Mother Frances in May 1863 contradicting McDonald's earlier claim, stating plainly, \"I did not see Henry after he was wounded\" — a key piece of evidence in the family's investigation of Henry's death."},{"id":"PER-isaac-dedds","n":"Isaac Dedds","cat":"civilian","roles":["Shot Mr. Bass and himself — jealousy over Mrs. Allison"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-05-04-001"],"first":"1863-05-04","last":"1863-05-04","s":"Isaac Dedds was a Champlain man who shot Mr. Bass and then himself out of jealousy over Mrs. Allison, a sensational local incident Mother Frances recounted in her emotionally raw May 1863 letter."},{"id":"PER-allison","n":"Mrs. Allison","cat":"civilian","roles":["Widow — Dedds was in love with her; her arm to be amputated"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-05-04-001"],"first":"1863-05-04","last":"1863-05-04","s":"Mrs. Allison was a widow over whom Isaac Dedds committed a shooting in Champlain; her arm required amputation, as reported by Mother Frances in May 1863 alongside her grief over rereading Henry's letters."},{"id":"PER-young-carter","n":"Young Carter","cat":"civilian","roles":["34th NY — thumb shot off at Antietam; left field before Henry wounded"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-05-04-001"],"first":"1863-05-04","last":"1863-05-04","s":"Young Carter was a soldier in the 34th New York who lost his thumb to a gunshot at Antietam and left the field before Henry Hubbell was wounded, making him unable to provide information about Henry's fate."},{"id":"PER-ballerjy","n":"Ballerjy","cat":"civilian","roles":["Killed — 4th position in ranks, next to Henry"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-05-04-001"],"first":"1863-05-04","last":"1863-05-04","s":"Ballerjy was a soldier killed at Antietam who stood in the fourth position in the ranks next to Henry Hubbell, noted by Mother Frances in May 1863 as she pieced together the circumstances of Henry's wounding."},{"id":"PER-uncle-fullerton","n":"Uncle Fullerton","cat":"civilian","roles":["Contracted $90,000 army clothing; has factories \"at the east\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-05-04-001"],"first":"1863-05-04","last":"1863-05-04","s":"Uncle Fullerton was a family member who had secured a ninety-thousand-dollar army clothing contract and operated factories \"at the east,\" mentioned by Mother Frances in May 1863 as an example of wartime enterprise within the extended family."},{"id":"PER-lester-bond","n":"Lester Bond","cat":"civilian","roles":["Killed — shot through forehead during bayonet charge"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-05-11-001"],"first":"1863-05-11","last":"1863-05-11","s":"Lester Bond was a soldier in Company H of the 60th New York Volunteers who was killed at the Battle of Chancellorsville in May 1863, shot through the forehead during a bayonet charge."},{"id":"PER-orderly","n":"Orderly (unnamed)","cat":"civilian","roles":["Blown into air by rebel shell — survived"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-05-11-001"],"first":"1863-05-11","last":"1863-05-11","s":"An unnamed orderly in the 60th New York was blown into the air by a rebel shell at Chancellorsville in May 1863 but survived, one of the vivid casualty details Alexander Hubbell recorded."},{"id":"PER-l--bond","n":"L. Bond","cat":"civilian","roles":["Killed — shot through forehead, bayonet charge"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-05-16-001"],"first":"1863-05-16","last":"1863-05-16","s":"L. Bond was killed at the Battle of Chancellorsville in May 1863, listed first among Company H casualties in Alexander Hubbell's detailed post-battle report to his family."},{"id":"PER-g--ploof","n":"G. Ploof","cat":"civilian","roles":["Wounded"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-05-16-001"],"first":"1863-05-16","last":"1863-05-16","s":"G. Ploof was a soldier in Company H of the 60th New York Volunteers who was wounded at the Battle of Chancellorsville in May 1863, listed on Alexander Hubbell's casualty report."},{"id":"PER-h--myers","n":"H. Myers","cat":"civilian","roles":["Wounded"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-05-16-001"],"first":"1863-05-16","last":"1863-05-16","s":"H. Myers was a soldier in Company H of the 60th New York Volunteers who was wounded at the Battle of Chancellorsville in May 1863."},{"id":"PER-p--brookway","n":"P. Brookway","cat":"civilian","roles":["Wounded"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-05-16-001"],"first":"1863-05-16","last":"1863-05-16","s":"P. Brookway was a soldier in Company H of the 60th New York Volunteers who was wounded at Chancellorsville in May 1863 but remained with the company as orderly sergeant."},{"id":"PER-i--stone","n":"I. Stone","cat":"civilian","roles":["Missing"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-05-16-001"],"first":"1863-05-16","last":"1863-05-16","s":"I. Stone was a soldier in Company H of the 60th New York Volunteers listed as missing after the Battle of Chancellorsville in May 1863."},{"id":"PER-b--brame","n":"B. Brame","cat":"civilian","roles":["Missing"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-05-16-001"],"first":"1863-05-16","last":"1863-05-16","s":"B. Brame was a soldier in Company H of the 60th New York Volunteers listed as missing after the Battle of Chancellorsville in May 1863."},{"id":"PER-redington","n":"Colonel Redington","cat":"military","roles":["60th NY — singing at battle of Chancellorsville; 'a man of courage'","Described: minimum height, slender, erect, bargainer, man of courage; singing during shellfire"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-06-03-001"],"first":"1863-06-03","last":"1863-06-03","s":"Colonel Redington commanded the 60th New York Volunteers and was described by Alexander Hubbell in June 1863 as singing during shellfire at both Antietam and Chancellorsville, using the screams of shells as \"a key to pitch his voice on\" — a man of minimum height, slender build, and extraordinary courage."},{"id":"PER-luther-s-father","n":"Luther's father","cat":"civilian","roles":["Visiting camp"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-06-03-001"],"first":"1863-06-03","last":"1863-06-03","s":"Luther's father visited the 60th New York's camp in June 1863, one of the civilian family members who made the journey to see soldiers in the field during the war."},{"id":"PER-m--smith","n":"M. Smith","cat":"civilian","roles":["At Charlotte — Mother and Frances should visit"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-06-10-001"],"first":"1863-06-10","last":"1863-06-10","s":"M. Smith was a person at Charlotte whom Charles Hubbell urged Mother and Frances to visit, mentioned in his June 1863 letter — the same letter in which he declared Henry was killed \"without a doubt.\""},{"id":"PER-ransome","n":"Capt. Ransome","cat":"military","roles":["Company captain — \"deserves especial praise for his bravery\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-07-04-001"],"first":"1863-07-04","last":"1863-07-04","s":"Captain Ransome of the 60th New York Volunteers was praised by Alexander Hubbell for his bravery at the Battle of Gettysburg in July 1863, singled out for \"especial praise\" in the first-person battle report."},{"id":"PER-milebus-ayres","n":"Milebus Ayres","cat":"civilian","roles":["Co. H — killed at Gettysburg"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-07-04-001"],"first":"1863-07-04","last":"1863-07-04","s":"Milebus Ayres was a soldier in Company H of the 60th New York Volunteers who was killed at the Battle of Gettysburg on July 2-3, 1863, listed among the company's casualties in Alexander Hubbell's battlefield letter."},{"id":"PER-john-tickle","n":"Private John Tickle","cat":"military","roles":["Co. H — wounded at Gettysburg"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-07-04-001"],"first":"1863-07-04","last":"1863-07-04","s":"Private John Tickle of Company H, 60th New York Volunteers, was wounded at the Battle of Gettysburg in July 1863, listed in Alexander Hubbell's casualty report written from the battlefield on Independence Day."},{"id":"PER-brend-miner","n":"Brend Miner","cat":"civilian","roles":["Asked about"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-07-06-001"],"first":"1863-07-06","last":"1863-07-06","s":"Brend Miner was a Champlain acquaintance Charles Hubbell asked about in July 1863, in a letter written from Alexandria before he learned of Alexander's survival at Gettysburg."},{"id":"PER-a--cook","n":"A. Cook","cat":"civilian","roles":["Asked about"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-07-06-001"],"first":"1863-07-06","last":"1863-07-06","s":"A. Cook was a Champlain acquaintance Charles Hubbell inquired about in his July 1863 letter, written while still unaware that Alexander had survived Gettysburg two days earlier."},{"id":"PER-cynthia-bourns","n":"Mrs. Cynthia Bourns","cat":"civilian","roles":["Husband discussed — \"only one fault\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-07-06-001"],"first":"1863-07-06","last":"1863-07-06","s":"Mrs. Cynthia Bourns was a Champlain woman whose husband was discussed by Charles Hubbell in July 1863 as having \"only one fault,\" part of the community gossip threaded through wartime letters."},{"id":"PER-h--d","n":"H. D.","cat":"civilian","roles":["Got exempt from draft — Alexander angry"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-07-30-001"],"first":"1863-07-30","last":"1863-07-30","s":"H. D. was a Champlain man who obtained an exemption from the draft, provoking Alexander Hubbell's anger in his July 1863 letter that also contained the collection's most vivid Gettysburg combat writing."},{"id":"PER-beardslee","n":"Corporal Beardslee","cat":"military","roles":["Wants pictures/papers — saving money"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-09-07-001"],"first":"1863-09-07","last":"1863-09-07","s":"Corporal Beardslee was a soldier in Charles Hubbell's regiment who wanted pictures and papers sent from home and was saving his money, mentioned in a September 1863 letter about garrison life."},{"id":"PER-hiram-i","n":"Hiram I.","cat":"civilian","roles":["Appointment to West Point?"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-09-15-001"],"first":"1863-09-15","last":"1863-09-15","s":"Hiram I. was a person whose possible appointment to West Point was discussed in Charles Hubbell's September 1863 letter, alongside news of James Hubbell's cadet demerits."},{"id":"PER-ran-som","n":"Capt. Ran[som]","cat":"military","roles":["\"Baltimore widows\" teasing"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-10-11-001"],"first":"1863-10-11","last":"1863-10-11","s":"Captain Ransom was the Company H officer whom Alexander Hubbell teased about \"Baltimore widows\" in an October 1863 letter from Murfreesboro, Tennessee — the 60th New York's first posting in the Western theater."},{"id":"PER-belle-boyd","n":"Belle Boyd","cat":"civilian","roles":["Confederate spy — confined at Carroll Prison, shows secesh flag"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-10-21-001"],"first":"1863-10-21","last":"1863-10-21","s":"Belle Boyd was the famous Confederate spy whom Charles Hubbell observed confined at Carroll Prison in Washington in October 1863 while on guard duty, noting a visit from a woman in broadcloth pants likely identifiable as Dr. Mary Walker."},{"id":"PER-foster","n":"\"Foster\"","cat":"civilian","roles":["Woman in broadcloth pants — visited Boyd; Bloomer doctor, unionist"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-10-21-001"],"first":"1863-10-21","last":"1863-10-21","s":"\"Foster\" was a woman in broadcloth pants who visited Belle Boyd at Carroll Prison in October 1863, described by Charles Hubbell as a \"Bloomer doctor\" employed by the Union army — almost certainly Dr. Mary Walker, identified by a misremembered name."},{"id":"PER-alex--douglas","n":"Alex [Douglas]","cat":"civilian","roles":["Brought box from home; arrived Oct 29"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-10-31-001"],"first":"1863-10-31","last":"1863-10-31","s":"Alex Douglas was a local man who brought a box from home to Charles Hubbell at camp, arriving on October 29, 1863, one of the personal delivery chains that supplemented the wartime postal system."},{"id":"PER-moses-cokey","n":"Moses Cokey","cat":"civilian","roles":["Leaving for Champlain tonight"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-10-31-001"],"first":"1863-10-31","last":"1863-10-31","s":"Moses Cokey was a soldier leaving for Champlain in late October 1863, mentioned by Charles Hubbell in a letter where he declined a furlough home because he had only a dollar and fifty cents to his name."},{"id":"PER-george-s--greene","n":"Gen. George S. Greene","cat":"military","roles":["Brigade commander — seriously wounded at Wauhatchie"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-11-01-001"],"first":"1863-11-01","last":"1863-11-01","s":"General George S. Greene, the hero of Culp's Hill at Gettysburg who had originally commanded the 60th New York as colonel, was seriously wounded at the Battle of Wauhatchie on October 28, 1863, as reported by Alexander Hubbell."},{"id":"PER-aunt-wry","n":"Aunt Wry","cat":"civilian","roles":["Would she pity them for being in Alabama?"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-11-01-001"],"first":"1863-11-01","last":"1863-11-01","s":"Aunt Wry was a family relative whom Alexander Hubbell wondered about in November 1863, asking whether she would pity them for being stationed in Alabama — part of his wry humor about the Western theater's hardships."},{"id":"PER-ruth-hubbell","n":"Mrs. Ruth Hubbell","cat":"civilian","roles":["Sid's wife — Alexander agrees with her"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-11-01-001"],"first":"1863-11-01","last":"1863-11-01","s":"Mrs. Ruth Hubbell was Sid Hubbell's wife with whom Alexander Hubbell expressed agreement in his November 1863 letter from the Chattanooga campaign, connecting extended family opinion to wartime correspondence."},{"id":"PER-d","n":"Col. D.","cat":"military","roles":["Former Col. — \"my claim upon you is stronger than country\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-11-15-001"],"first":"1863-11-15","last":"1863-11-15","s":"Col. D. was a former officer whose wife once told Mother Frances, \"my claim upon you is stronger than country\" — a sentiment Frances cited in her November 1863 letter urging Alexander to value family over duty."},{"id":"PER-havelock","n":"Havelock","cat":"civilian","roles":["Model Christian soldier — reference"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-11-15-001"],"first":"1863-11-15","last":"1863-11-15","s":"Havelock was the British general invoked by Mother Frances Hubbell in November 1863 as a model Christian soldier, part of her moral instruction to Alexander through letters during the Chattanooga campaign."},{"id":"PER-roberts","n":"Mr. Roberts","cat":"civilian","roles":["From C. — cousin visiting; photograph business $48/month"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-11-19-001"],"first":"1863-11-19","last":"1863-11-19","s":"Mr. Roberts was a cousin visiting from C. who ran a photograph business earning forty-eight dollars per month, mentioned by Charles Hubbell in his November 1863 letter about rebel prisoners and camp life."},{"id":"PER-strain-strange","n":"Major Strain/Strange","cat":"military","roles":["In command while Col. gone"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-11-19-001"],"first":"1863-11-19","last":"1863-11-19","s":"Major Strain, also rendered as Strange, commanded the 153rd New York while the colonel was away, mentioned by Charles Hubbell in his November 1863 letter about transporting rebel prisoners to Old Capitol Prison."},{"id":"PER-strange","n":"Major Strange","cat":"military","roles":["Commanding regiment while Col. away","Doesn't like Alexandria"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-11-20-001"],"first":"1863-11-20","last":"1863-11-20","s":"Major Strange commanded Charles Hubbell's regiment while the colonel was away and reportedly disliked Alexandria, mentioned in Charles's November 1863 letter about money stolen from his pocket book."},{"id":"PER-george-hays","n":"George Hays","cat":"civilian","roles":["Co. H — killed at Lookout Mountain"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-12-01-001"],"first":"1863-12-01","last":"1863-12-01","s":"George Hays was a soldier in Company H of the 60th New York Volunteers who was killed at the Battle of Lookout Mountain on November 24, 1863, listed among the casualties in Alexander Hubbell's letter written from a hospital at Tullahoma."},{"id":"PER-ish","n":"Corporal Ish","cat":"military","roles":["Co. H — wounded"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-12-01-001"],"first":"1863-12-01","last":"1863-12-01","s":"Corporal Ish of Company H, 60th New York Volunteers, was wounded at the Battle of Lookout Mountain in November 1863, listed on Alexander Hubbell's casualty report alongside his own wounding."},{"id":"PER-sidney-rider","n":"Private Sidney Rider","cat":"military","roles":["Co. H — wounded"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-12-01-001"],"first":"1863-12-01","last":"1863-12-01","s":"Private Sidney Rider of Company H, 60th New York Volunteers, was wounded at the Battle of Lookout Mountain in November 1863, appearing on the same casualty list where Alexander Hubbell recorded his own minie ball wound."},{"id":"PER-lemage","n":"Lemage","cat":"civilian","roles":["Co. H — wounded"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-12-13-001"],"first":"1863-12-13","last":"1863-12-13","s":"Lemage was a soldier in Company H of the 60th New York Volunteers who was wounded during the Chattanooga campaign engagements of late November 1863, listed in Alexander Hubbell's comprehensive battle narrative."},{"id":"PER-tees","n":"Tees","cat":"civilian","roles":["Co. H — wounded"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-12-13-001"],"first":"1863-12-13","last":"1863-12-13","s":"Tees was a soldier in Company H of the 60th New York Volunteers who was wounded during the Chattanooga campaign battles in late November 1863."},{"id":"PER-majo","n":"Majo","cat":"civilian","roles":["Co. H — killed"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-12-13-001"],"first":"1863-12-13","last":"1863-12-13","s":"Majo was a soldier in Company H of the 60th New York Volunteers who was killed during the Chattanooga campaign engagements in late November 1863, listed in Alexander Hubbell's battle narrative covering Wauhatchie through Ringgold."},{"id":"PER-housedean","n":"Housedean","cat":"civilian","roles":["Co. H — killed (Michael)"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-12-13-001"],"first":"1863-12-13","last":"1863-12-13","s":"Housedean was a soldier in Company H of the 60th New York Volunteers who was killed during the Chattanooga campaign, identified as Michael in Alexander Hubbell's comprehensive December 1863 battle narrative."},{"id":"PER-longstreet","n":"Longstreet","cat":"civilian","roles":["Fought at Wauhatchie, Oct 28"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-12-13-001"],"first":"1863-12-13","last":"1863-12-13","s":"Lieutenant General James Longstreet's Confederate forces fought at the Battle of Wauhatchie on October 28, 1863, as reported in Alexander Hubbell's comprehensive narrative of the 60th New York's Tennessee engagements."},{"id":"PER-michael-housedean","n":"Michael Housedean","cat":"civilian","roles":["Co. H — killed"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-12-16-001"],"first":"1863-12-16","last":"1863-12-16","s":"Michael Housedean of Company H, 60th New York Volunteers, was killed at the Battle of Missionary Ridge or Ringgold in late November 1863, confirmed in Alexander Hubbell's December letter after returning early from hospital."},{"id":"PER-alfred-lemage","n":"Alfred Lemage","cat":"civilian","roles":["Co. H — wounded"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-12-16-001"],"first":"1863-12-16","last":"1863-12-16","s":"Alfred Lemage of Company H, 60th New York Volunteers, was wounded at the Battle of Ringgold in late November 1863, confirmed by Alexander Hubbell after he returned to the regiment from hospital."},{"id":"PER-thomas-small","n":"Thomas Small","cat":"civilian","roles":["Co. H — wounded, leg torn off"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1863-12-16-001"],"first":"1863-12-16","last":"1863-12-16","s":"Thomas Small of Company H, 60th New York Volunteers, had his leg torn off at the Battle of Ringgold in late November 1863, one of the most severe casualties Alexander Hubbell recorded."},{"id":"PER-geo--dodds","n":"Geo. Dodds","cat":"civilian","roles":["Champlainer — visited from hospital, fever"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-01-03-001"],"first":"1864-01-03","last":"1864-01-03","s":"Geo. Dodds was a Champlain man who visited Charles Hubbell from the hospital with a fever in early January 1864, during a letter describing New Year's celebrations and re-enlistment bounty debates."},{"id":"PER-trombly","n":"Corporal Trombly","cat":"military","roles":["2nd Lieut. in colored regt. — passed examining board","`stated`"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-01-19-001"],"first":"1864-01-19","last":"1864-01-19","s":"Corporal Trombly passed the examining board and received a commission as second lieutenant in a United States Colored Troops regiment, mentioned by Charles Hubbell in January 1864 as an example of the new pathways the war opened for enlisted men."},{"id":"PER-spalding","n":"Capt. Spalding","cat":"military","roles":["Dismissed for incompetency — preferred charges against Col. in revenge"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-01-19-001"],"first":"1864-01-19","last":"1864-01-19","s":"Captain Spalding was dismissed from the 153rd New York for incompetency and subsequently preferred charges against the colonel in revenge, part of the regimental scandal Charles Hubbell described in January 1864."},{"id":"PER-prentoff","n":"Prentoff","cat":"civilian","roles":["Ex-quartermaster — must give account"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-01-19-001"],"first":"1864-01-19","last":"1864-01-19","s":"Prentoff was the former quartermaster of the 153rd New York who was required to give an accounting of his tenure, mentioned amid the court-martial scandal Charles Hubbell reported in January 1864."},{"id":"PER-joel-s","n":"Joel S.","cat":"civilian","roles":["Creditor — $27.60 acc't from Plattsburgh"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-01-29-001"],"first":"1864-01-29","last":"1864-01-29","s":"Joel S. was a Plattsburgh creditor owed twenty-seven dollars and sixty cents by the Hubbell family, mentioned by Charles in his January 1864 letter about attending Congressional debates and church services in Washington."},{"id":"PER-sidney","n":"Sidney","cat":"civilian","roles":["Having family trouble"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-01-29-001"],"first":"1864-01-29","last":"1864-01-29","s":"Sidney was an acquaintance described by Charles Hubbell in January 1864 as having family trouble, part of the home-front social updates exchanged through wartime letters."},{"id":"PER-sam-underhill","n":"Sam Underhill","cat":"civilian","roles":["Not entitled to town bounty — dispute with Alex"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-02-15-001"],"first":"1864-02-15","last":"1864-02-15","s":"Sam Underhill was a man whose dispute with Alexander Hubbell over entitlement to a town bounty was mentioned by Charles in February 1864, reflecting the financial complexities of Civil War enlistment incentives."},{"id":"PER-thos--small","n":"Thos. Small","cat":"civilian","roles":["Lost leg at Ringgold — hospital No. 8, Nashville"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-02-18-001"],"first":"1864-02-18","last":"1864-02-18","s":"Thomas Small, who had lost his leg at the Battle of Ringgold, was visited by Alexander Hubbell at Hospital No. 8 in Nashville in February 1864 and reported to be doing well despite his amputation."},{"id":"PER-rider","n":"Mrs. Rider","cat":"civilian","roles":["Associated with Frances's school"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-02-18-001"],"first":"1864-02-18","last":"1864-02-18","s":"Mrs. Rider was associated with Frances Hubbell's school, mentioned by Alexander in his February 1864 letter during transit through Nashville."},{"id":"PER-oliver-lafountain","n":"Oliver Lafountain","cat":"civilian","roles":["Tent mate"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-02-21-001"],"first":"1864-02-21","last":"1864-02-21","s":"Oliver Lafountain was Alexander Hubbell's tent mate at Stephenson, Alabama, in February 1864, mentioned in the letter containing Alexander's lyrical Sabbath morning description and his conversation with a captured rebel captain."},{"id":"PER-george-bully","n":"George Bully","cat":"civilian","roles":["Tent mate"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-02-21-001"],"first":"1864-02-21","last":"1864-02-21","s":"George Bully was Alexander Hubbell's tent mate in February 1864 at Stephenson, Alabama, named alongside Oliver Lafountain in a letter featuring one of the collection's most literary landscape passages."},{"id":"PER-morgan","n":"Gen. Morgan","cat":"military","roles":["\"Horse thief\" — 8,000 men threatening"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-02-21-001"],"first":"1864-02-21","last":"1864-02-21","s":"General John Hunt Morgan was the Confederate cavalry raider described by Alexander Hubbell in February 1864 as a \"horse thief\" threatening with eight thousand men, reflecting Union soldiers' contempt for irregular warfare."},{"id":"PER-johnson","n":"Johnson (Johnston)","cat":"civilian","roles":["Replaced Bragg — army demoralized"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-02-21-001"],"first":"1864-02-21","last":"1864-02-21","s":"General Joseph E. Johnston replaced Bragg as commander of the Confederate Army of Tennessee, noted by Alexander Hubbell in February 1864 as leading a demoralized force unlikely to stand and fight."},{"id":"PER-kilpatrick","n":"Kilpatrick","cat":"civilian","roles":["Tore up railroads — prevented Confederates from removing guns"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-02-24-001"],"first":"1864-02-24","last":"1864-02-24","s":"Brigadier General Judson Kilpatrick's cavalry raid tore up railroads and prevented Confederate forces from removing their heavy guns, as Alexander Hubbell reported in his February 1864 letter from Charleston, Tennessee."},{"id":"PER-bostwick","n":"Mrs. Bostwick","cat":"civilian","roles":["Having dolorous times with Mother","`stated`"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-03-07-001"],"first":"1864-03-07","last":"1864-03-07","s":"Mrs. Bostwick was a Champlain acquaintance described by Alexander Hubbell in March 1864 as having \"dolorous times\" with Mother Frances, suggesting shared worrying on the home front."},{"id":"PER-nathaniel-nichols","n":"Nathaniel Nichols","cat":"civilian","roles":["Asked about"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-03-20-001"],"first":"1864-03-20","last":"1864-03-20","s":"Nathaniel Nichols was a Champlain acquaintance Alexander Hubbell asked about in March 1864, in the letter widely regarded as his most literary composition — a panoramic portrait of camp life from picket fires to Taps."},{"id":"PER-mother-alice","n":"Frances M. Hubbell (Mother)","cat":"family","roles":["\"Queen\" compared to Southern women"],"rel":"Mother","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-03-20-001"],"first":"1864-03-20","last":"1864-03-20","s":"Frances M. Hubbell, the Hubbell brothers' mother in Champlain, New York, was compared by Alexander in March 1864 to a \"queen\" relative to the Southern women he had encountered — an affectionate tribute embedded in his most literary letter."},{"id":"PER-fox","n":"Capt. Fox","cat":"military","roles":["Dismissed — disloyalty and conduct unbecoming"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-03-25-001"],"first":"1864-03-25","last":"1864-03-25","s":"Captain Fox was dismissed from the 153rd New York for disloyalty and conduct unbecoming an officer, mentioned by Charles Hubbell in March 1864 alongside descriptions of New Orleans street life."},{"id":"PER-m-m--bass","n":"M.M. Bass","cat":"civilian","roles":["Alexander sure he voted against soldiers voting"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-04-06-001"],"first":"1864-04-06","last":"1864-04-06","s":"M. M. Bass was a Champlain politician whom Alexander Hubbell was certain had voted against soldiers' voting rights, mentioned in an April 1864 letter about the formation of the 20th Army Corps."},{"id":"PER-reams","n":"Mrs. Reams (Cousin Nell)","cat":"civilian","roles":["Cousin — recently married; wants letters"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-04-07-001"],"first":"1864-04-07","last":"1864-04-07","s":"Mrs. Reams, called Cousin Nell, was a recently married cousin who wanted letters from Alexander Hubbell, mentioned in his April 1864 letter describing a game of \"Union Base\" — an early form of baseball played between regiments."},{"id":"PER-old-nye","n":"Old Nye","cat":"civilian","roles":["Running the family bush (sugar-making)"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-04-07-001"],"first":"1864-04-07","last":"1864-04-07","s":"Old Nye was running the family's sugar bush operation in the spring of 1864, mentioned by Alexander Hubbell as he inquired about maple sugar-making season back in Champlain."},{"id":"PER-gov--seymour","n":"Gov. Seymour","cat":"civilian","roles":["Slim chances of commission through him"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-04-18-001"],"first":"1864-04-18","last":"1864-04-18","s":"Governor Horatio Seymour of New York was mentioned by Alexander Hubbell in April 1864 as offering slim chances for a military commission, reflecting the political channels soldiers navigated for advancement."},{"id":"PER-burroughs","n":"Mr. Burroughs","cat":"civilian","roles":["Local man — Thomas compared to his build","`stated`"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-04-18-001"],"first":"1864-04-18","last":"1864-04-18","s":"Mr. Burroughs was a large local man from Champlain whose build Alexander Hubbell compared to that of General George Thomas — \"tremendous large man, beard snow white\" — when describing the general's April 1864 troop review."},{"id":"PER-john-m--geary","n":"Gen. John M. Geary","cat":"military","roles":["Div. commander — same as before"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-04-22-001"],"first":"1864-04-22","last":"1864-04-22","s":"Major General John M. Geary commanded the division containing Alexander Hubbell's 60th New York in the 20th Army Corps, noted in Alexander's April 1864 order-of-battle letter as the Atlanta Campaign was about to begin."},{"id":"PER-cousin-mary","n":"Cousin Mary","cat":"civilian","roles":["Sent 2 newspapers"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-05-23-001"],"first":"1864-05-23","last":"1864-05-23","s":"Cousin Mary sent two newspapers to Charles Hubbell, mentioned in his May 1864 letter announcing that James Hubbell had arrived and joined the 153rd New York — reuniting two brothers in the same regiment."},{"id":"PER-james-thurber","n":"James Thurber","cat":"civilian","roles":["Co. H — wounded through arm and shoulder"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-05-31-001"],"first":"1864-05-31","last":"1864-05-31","s":"James Thurber of Company H, 60th New York Volunteers, was wounded through the arm and shoulder at the Battle of Dallas / New Hope Church in late May 1864, listed in Alexander Hubbell's letter written under active fire."},{"id":"PER-john-rawlins","n":"John Rawlins","cat":"civilian","roles":["Co. H — wounded through right side"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-05-31-001"],"first":"1864-05-31","last":"1864-05-31","s":"John Rawlins of Company H, 60th New York Volunteers, was wounded through the right side at the Battle of Dallas / New Hope Church in late May 1864, part of the twenty-eight total regiment casualties Alexander Hubbell reported."},{"id":"PER-barker","n":"Gen. Barker","cat":"military","roles":["Killed — mistaken for Hooker"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-07-00-001"],"first":"1864-07-00","last":"1864-07-00","s":"General Barker was killed during the Atlanta Campaign near Marietta, reportedly mistaken for General Hooker, as Alexander Hubbell noted in a July 1864 letter describing Sherman's flanking tactics."},{"id":"PER-logan","n":"Gen. Logan","cat":"military","roles":["14th Corps — holds part of line"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-07-00-001"],"first":"1864-07-00","last":"1864-07-00","s":"Major General John A. Logan commanded forces holding part of the Union line during the Atlanta Campaign, mentioned by Alexander Hubbell in his July 1864 letter about operations near Marietta, Georgia."},{"id":"PER-m--h--cook","n":"M. H. Cook","cat":"civilian","roles":["Tell him Eastern troops are above par"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-07-00-001"],"first":"1864-07-00","last":"1864-07-00","s":"M. H. Cook was a Champlain acquaintance to whom Alexander Hubbell asked his family to relay that Eastern troops were performing \"above par\" in the Atlanta Campaign, a point of regional pride among New York soldiers serving in the Western theater."},{"id":"PER-bishop-polk","n":"Bishop Polk","cat":"civilian","roles":["Lt. Gen. — killed by cannon shot Alexander witnessed"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-07-08-001"],"first":"1864-07-08","last":"1864-07-08","s":"Lieutenant General Leonidas Polk, the \"Fighting Bishop\" of the Confederacy, was killed by a cannon shot on Pine Mountain on June 14, 1864 — an event Alexander Hubbell witnessed from the Union lines and reported in his July 1864 letter."},{"id":"PER-johnston","n":"Johnston","cat":"civilian","roles":["Confederate commander — was near Polk"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-07-08-001"],"first":"1864-07-08","last":"1864-07-08","s":"General Joseph E. Johnston was the Confederate commander standing near Bishop Polk when the fatal cannon shot struck on Pine Mountain in June 1864, as described in Alexander Hubbell's eyewitness account."},{"id":"PER-fremont","n":"Fremont","cat":"civilian","roles":["Presidential candidate — little army favor"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-07-08-001"],"first":"1864-07-08","last":"1864-07-08","s":"John C. Fremont was a presidential candidate in 1864 who had little favor among the soldiers, according to Alexander Hubbell's July 1864 letter from the Atlanta Campaign."},{"id":"PER-canby","n":"Gen. Canby","cat":"military","roles":["Troops in Maryland"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-08-02-001"],"first":"1864-08-02","last":"1864-08-02","s":"General Edward R.S. Canby was mentioned by Alexander Hubbell in August 1864 in connection with Union troop movements in Maryland during the Atlanta Campaign period."},{"id":"PER-howard","n":"Howard","cat":"civilian","roles":["Commands formerly McPherson's forces"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-08-08-002"],"first":"1864-08-08","last":"1864-08-08","s":"Major General Oliver O. Howard took command of the forces formerly led by McPherson after McPherson's death at the Battle of Atlanta, noted by Alexander Hubbell in his August 1864 letter to Captain Ransom."},{"id":"PER-p--h--brookway","n":"P. H. Brookway","cat":"civilian","roles":["1st Lieut. commission — commands Co. H"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-08-08-002"],"first":"1864-08-08","last":"1864-08-08","s":"P. H. Brookway received a commission as first lieutenant and commanded Company H of the 60th New York during the Atlanta Campaign, mentioned in Alexander Hubbell's detailed August 1864 account of the Battle of Peachtree Creek."},{"id":"PER-elliott","n":"Capt. Elliott","cat":"military","roles":["Now Major"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-08-08-002"],"first":"1864-08-08","last":"1864-08-08","s":"Captain Elliott was promoted to major of the 60th New York during the Atlanta Campaign, noted in Alexander Hubbell's August 1864 letter to Captain Ransom containing the collection's most detailed battle narrative."},{"id":"PER-hobart","n":"Hobart","cat":"civilian","roles":["Capt. of Co. I"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-08-08-002"],"first":"1864-08-08","last":"1864-08-08","s":"Captain Hobart commanded Company I of the 60th New York during the Atlanta Campaign, mentioned in Alexander Hubbell's August 1864 Peachtree Creek account written to Captain Ransom."},{"id":"PER-bundy","n":"Bundy","cat":"civilian","roles":["Battery — rebs had use of guns"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-08-08-002"],"first":"1864-08-08","last":"1864-08-08","s":"Bundy commanded a battery that the rebels briefly captured during the Battle of Peachtree Creek in July 1864, as described in Alexander Hubbell's detailed account to Captain Ransom."},{"id":"PER-147th-penn","n":"147th Penn.","cat":"civilian","roles":["Credit with 60th for saving Division batteries"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-08-08-002"],"first":"1864-08-08","last":"1864-08-08","s":"The 147th Pennsylvania Infantry shared credit with the 60th New York for saving their division's batteries during the Battle of Peachtree Creek in July 1864, as Alexander Hubbell reported to Captain Ransom."},{"id":"PER-f--a--cook","n":"F. A. Cook","cat":"civilian","roles":["At Champlain — \"hot for Lincoln\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-08-22-001"],"first":"1864-08-22","last":"1864-08-22","s":"F. A. Cook was a Champlain resident described by Alexander Hubbell in August 1864 as being \"hot for Lincoln\" in the upcoming presidential election."},{"id":"PER-sheridan","n":"Gen. Sheridan","cat":"military","roles":["Commands army — 19th, 6th, 8th Corps, cavalry"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-08-26-001"],"first":"1864-08-26","last":"1864-08-26","s":"Major General Philip Sheridan commanded the Union army in the Shenandoah Valley with the 19th, 6th, and 8th Corps and a large cavalry force, as Charles Hubbell reported from near Harper's Ferry in August 1864."},{"id":"PER-jack-ingram","n":"Jack Ingram","cat":"civilian","roles":["Letter received"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-09-18-001"],"first":"1864-09-18","last":"1864-09-18","s":"Jack Ingram was a correspondent whose letter Alexander Hubbell acknowledged receiving in September 1864, mentioned in a letter declaring his political identity as a \"war man.\""},{"id":"PER-lincoln","n":"Lincoln","cat":"civilian","roles":["Favored — 37 votes on train"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-10-10-001"],"first":"1864-10-10","last":"1864-10-10","s":"Abraham Lincoln was favored thirty-seven to five over McClellan in a straw poll Alexander Hubbell conducted on a hospital train from Chattanooga to Nashville in October 1864."},{"id":"PER-lucas","n":"Lucas","cat":"civilian","roles":["Badly wounded — \"only one you know in the company\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-10-29-001"],"first":"1864-10-29","last":"1864-10-29","s":"Lucas was a soldier described by James Hubbell in October 1864 as badly wounded and \"the only one you know in the company\" — part of James's account of the Battle of Cedar Creek, where he himself was wounded in the left thigh."},{"id":"PER-de-sweeney","n":"De Sweeney (Dr. Sweeney)","cat":"military","roles":["Captured morning of fight — escaped that night"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-11-04-001"],"first":"1864-11-04","last":"1864-11-04","s":"Dr. Sweeney was a regimental surgeon captured on the morning of the Battle of Cedar Creek in October 1864 who escaped that same night, as reported by Charles Hubbell in November 1864."},{"id":"PER-hood","n":"Gen. Hood","cat":"military","roles":["Before Nashville — \"bogus Confederacy\""],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-12-05-001"],"first":"1864-12-05","last":"1864-12-05","s":"General John Bell Hood commanded the Confederate Army of Tennessee besieging Nashville in December 1864, dismissed by Alexander Hubbell as leading a \"bogus Confederacy\" in one of his final wartime letters."},{"id":"PER-avey","n":"Miss Avey","cat":"civilian","roles":["`stated`"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1864-12-09-001"],"first":"1864-12-09","last":"1864-12-09","s":"Miss Avey was an acquaintance mentioned by James Hubbell in his December 1864 letter from Chestnut Hill Hospital in Philadelphia, where he was recovering from his Cedar Creek thigh wound."},{"id":"PER-tom-moore","n":"Tom Moore","cat":"civilian","roles":["Witnessed corporal's punishment"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1865-01-11-001"],"first":"1865-01-11","last":"1865-01-11","s":"Tom Moore was a Champlain resident who witnessed a corporal's punishment, mentioned by Mother Frances in her January 1865 letter — the most detailed home-front portrait in the collection, describing Champlain under military rule after the St. Albans Raid."},{"id":"PER-joe-moore","n":"Joe Moore","cat":"civilian","roles":["$500/year for stables"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1865-01-11-001"],"first":"1865-01-11","last":"1865-01-11","s":"Joe Moore received five hundred dollars per year for providing stables to the military garrison in Champlain, mentioned by Mother Frances in January 1865 as the town operated under martial law following the St. Albans Raid."},{"id":"PER-p--ransom-s-wife","n":"P. Ransom's wife","cat":"civilian","roles":["Going to masquerade"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1865-01-11-001"],"first":"1865-01-11","last":"1865-01-11","s":"P. Ransom's wife was planning to attend a masquerade in Champlain, mentioned by Mother Frances in her January 1865 letter describing social life continuing on the home front despite the war's final months."},{"id":"PER-denys","n":"Mrs. Denys","cat":"civilian","roles":["Champlain neighbor visited by family","Getting pretty — making bags for soldiers"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1865-01-11-001"],"first":"1865-01-11","last":"1865-01-11","s":"Miss Denys was a Champlain neighbor described by Mother Frances in January 1865 as getting pretty and making bags for soldiers — one of the small home-front contributions that sustained the war effort."},{"id":"PER-chris-greene","n":"Chris Greene","cat":"civilian","roles":["Gen. Greene's son/aide — leg amputated at Ringgold"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1865-06-16-001"],"first":"1865-06-16","last":"1865-06-16","s":"Chris Greene was the son and aide of General George S. Greene whose leg was amputated at the Battle of Ringgold after it was severed with a pocket knife on the field — a visceral detail Alexander Hubbell recounted in his June 1865 post-war letter."},{"id":"PER-bartlette","n":"Gen. Bartlette","cat":"military","roles":["Commands veteran division"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1865-06-16-001"],"first":"1865-06-16","last":"1865-06-16","s":"General Bartlette commanded the veteran division during the post-war demobilization period, mentioned by Alexander Hubbell in his June 1865 letter about the breakup of corps and divisions."},{"id":"PER-hoff","n":"Mrs. Hoff","cat":"civilian","roles":["Family friend — Alexander sends love"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1865-06-27-001"],"first":"1865-06-27","last":"1865-06-27","s":"Mrs. Hoff was a family friend to whom Alexander Hubbell sent his love in his June 1865 valedictory letter — the letter announcing the 60th New York Veteran Volunteers' muster-out from service."},{"id":"PER-john-sweeney","n":"Dr. John Sweeney","cat":"military","roles":["Brother of regimental surgeon — Division Hospital"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1865-07-13-001"],"first":"1865-07-13","last":"1865-07-13","s":"Dr. John Sweeney was the brother of the regimental surgeon, serving at the Division Hospital, mentioned by Charles Hubbell in his July 1865 letter from post-war Savannah alongside the first peacetime Fourth of July celebration."},{"id":"PER-ranch-malcolm","n":"Ranch Malcolm","cat":"civilian","roles":["Graduated medical school with John Sweeney — former asst. surgeon"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1865-07-13-001"],"first":"1865-07-13","last":"1865-07-13","s":"Ranch Malcolm was a former assistant surgeon who graduated from medical school alongside John Sweeney, mentioned by Charles Hubbell in his July 1865 letter from Savannah."},{"id":"PER-harry-hummel","n":"Capt. Harry Hummel","cat":"military","roles":["Possibly heard from in Washington"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1865-07-13-001"],"first":"1865-07-13","last":"1865-07-13","s":"Captain Harry Hummel was an officer Charles Hubbell may have heard from in Washington, mentioned in his July 1865 post-war letter from Savannah."},{"id":"PER-uncle-william","n":"Uncle William","cat":"civilian","roles":["Built cheese factory — Thanksgiving host"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1870-11-28-001"],"first":"1870-11-28","last":"1870-11-28","s":"Uncle William built a cheese factory with a five-thousand-dollar investment and ten stockholders, producing eighty-pound cheeses at fourteen cents per pound — part of the postwar enterprise Mother Frances described in the collection's final letter of November 1870."},{"id":"PER-uncle-james","n":"Uncle James","cat":"civilian","roles":["At Thanksgiving"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1870-11-28-001"],"first":"1870-11-28","last":"1870-11-28","s":"Uncle James attended the family Thanksgiving gathering in November 1870, mentioned in Mother Frances's final letter — the collection's closing document, written five years after the war's end."},{"id":"PER-aunt-louise","n":"Aunt Louise","cat":"civilian","roles":["Took them to Uncle Henry's"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1870-11-28-001"],"first":"1870-11-28","last":"1870-11-28","s":"Aunt Louise took Mother Frances and company to Uncle Henry's home, mentioned in the November 1870 Thanksgiving letter that closes the collection with a portrait of family life restored."},{"id":"PER-aunt-meade","n":"Aunt Meade","cat":"civilian","roles":["Visited at Burlington — elderly, 86"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1870-11-28-001"],"first":"1870-11-28","last":"1870-11-28","s":"Aunt Meade was an elderly relative of eighty-six years whom Mother Frances visited at Burlington, mentioned in the collection's final letter of November 1870."},{"id":"PER-sam-hewitt","n":"Sam Hewitt","cat":"civilian","roles":["Aunt Meade's son — recently died"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1870-11-28-001"],"first":"1870-11-28","last":"1870-11-28","s":"Sam Hewitt was Aunt Meade's son who had recently died, noted by Mother Frances in her November 1870 Thanksgiving letter — a postwar loss amid the family's recovery."},{"id":"PER-edgar","n":"Mrs. Edgar","cat":"civilian","roles":["Aunt Meade's companion/relation — estate matters"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1870-11-28-001"],"first":"1870-11-28","last":"1870-11-28","s":"Mrs. Edgar was Aunt Meade's companion or relation involved in estate matters, mentioned in Mother Frances's November 1870 letter — the collection's final document."},{"id":"PER-hennings","n":"Mrs. Hennings","cat":"civilian","roles":["Staying with aunt — suspected of theft"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1870-11-28-001"],"first":"1870-11-28","last":"1870-11-28","s":"Mrs. Hennings was staying with an aunt and suspected of theft, a piece of domestic intrigue Mother Frances reported in her November 1870 Thanksgiving letter."},{"id":"PER-mendes","n":"The Mendes","cat":"civilian","roles":["Visitors at Uncle Henry's evening gathering"],"rel":"","lc":1,"ltrs":["LTR-1870-11-28-001"],"first":"1870-11-28","last":"1870-11-28","s":"The Mendes were visitors at Uncle Henry's evening gathering, mentioned in Mother Frances's November 1870 letter — the last names recorded in a collection that began with soldiers marching to war."}];
